@c ===================================================================
@node Overview
@chapter Overview

CMacs is a customized build of Emacs that adds first-class GLib and
GObject support.  It is built from the Emacs source tree with
additional C modules under @file{cmacs/} and elisp under
@file{lisp/cmacs/}.

Key components:

@table @strong
@item GLib Event Loop
Integrates a GMainContext into Emacs's @code{pselect()}-based event
loop so that GLib sources (timers, D-Bus, sockets) fire naturally
within the Emacs event cycle---no threads, no races.

@item GObject Bridge
Wraps GObjects as elisp user-pointers with automatic reference
counting, property access, signal connection, and GClosure marshalling.

@item GObject Introspection
Loads typelibs at runtime and calls any GI-annotated function or method
from elisp, with full bidirectional type marshalling.

@item D-Bus Service
Exposes an @code{org.cmacs.Editor1} D-Bus interface so external
processes can call into CMacs for elisp evaluation, GI calls, and file
operations.

@item Bacon Shell
Runs the Bacon shell inside Emacs via vterm, with the @command{cmacsgi}
builtin providing 60+ subcommands for buffer management, text editing,
search/replace, window layout, project navigation, version control,
and package management---all without writing elisp.

@item Crispy C Scripting
Evaluates C code strings at runtime via the Crispy library, capturing
stdout/stderr output.

@item Gowl Wayland Compositor
Embeds a wlroots-based Wayland compositor into Emacs.  With
@option{--gowl}, Emacs becomes a tiling window manager with 49+ DEFUNs
for spawning clients, managing tags, switching layouts, and embedding
Wayland clients directly inside Emacs buffer windows.

@item Podomation Automation
Embeds the Podomation event-driven automation engine.  Provides 17
DEFUNs for engine lifecycle, DSL evaluation, REPL, and event emission.
Includes built-in @code{cmacs} and @code{gowl} PodModules that bridge
Emacs events and compositor commands into the DSL, plus 80+ loadable
plugin modules.

@item Org-Ex Interactive Widgets
Embeds interactive widgets---GTK controls, web views, buffer windows,
and code evaluation results---directly inside Org mode buffers.
Widgets are declared with @code{#+BEGIN_WIDGET} blocks, bound
reactively to document properties, and exported to HTML and text.

@item Cintrospect / Cpatch
Runtime C self-introspection (libdw DWARF reader + Phase-2
libgccjit JIT) and runtime C hot-patching (atomic
@code{Lisp_Subr.function} swap + Phase-3 trampoline detours).
Together: a C layer as live and inspectable as the Lisp layer ---
the @emph{ultimate hackable editor} story.  Cintrospect default-on;
cpatch off by default, requires explicit
@option{--enable-cmacs-cpatch}.
@end table

@noindent
Source layout:

@example
cmacs/
  glib/           GLib event loop, GObject bridge, GClosure, D-Bus,
                  eval dispatch
  gobject/        (symlinked into glib/)
  gi/             GObject Introspection bridge
  crispy/         Crispy C scripting
  bacon/          Bacon shell: C primitives, IPC, cmacs_bacon_main()
    modules/      cmacsgi builtin (transport abstraction + 60+ commands)
  gowl/           Wayland compositor (wlroots, 49+ DEFUNs, embedding)
  org-ex/         Org-Ex interactive widgets (liborgex-1.0.so + DEFUN bridge)
  cintrospect/    Runtime C self-introspection (libdw DWARF + libgccjit JIT)
  cpatch/         Runtime C hot-patching (DEFUN pointer swap + detours)
deps/bacon/       Bundled bacon source (built as libbacon-1.0.a)
deps/gowl/        Bundled gowl library (wlroots-based compositor)
lisp/cmacs/       Elisp integration layers
src/              Symlinks to cmacs/ files (for the Emacs build system)
@end example

@c ===================================================================
@node GLib Event Loop
@chapter GLib Event Loop Integration

@cindex GMainContext
@cindex event loop, GLib

CMacs creates a private @code{GMainContext} (separate from the GLib
default context that PGTK/GTK may own) and integrates it into Emacs's
@code{pselect()}-based event loop in @file{src/process.c}.

@section How It Works

@enumerate
@item
Before each @code{pselect()} call, @code{cmacs_glib_prepare()} queries
the GMainContext for its file descriptors and timeouts via
@code{g_main_context_query()}.  These fds are merged into Emacs's
@code{fd_set}s, and the timeout is reduced if GLib needs an earlier
wake-up.  Returns the highest GLib fd (for @code{nfds}).

@item
After @code{pselect()} returns, @code{cmacs_glib_dispatch()} maps the
results back to GLib's @code{GPollFD} structures, then calls
@code{g_main_context_check()} and @code{g_main_context_dispatch()} to
fire any ready GLib sources.

@item
If @code{pselect()} fails (@code{nfds < 0}), the context is released
without dispatching.
@end enumerate

@noindent
All GLib callbacks fire on the Emacs main thread.  No additional
threads are created.

@section Elisp Primitives

@table @code
@item cmacs-glib-context-p
Return non-nil if the GMainContext exists.

@item cmacs-glib-iteration &optional BLOCK
Run one iteration of the GLib main loop.

@item cmacs-glib-pending-p
Return non-nil if any GLib sources are pending.

@item cmacs-glib-timeout-add MS CALLBACK
Add a timeout source.  @var{CALLBACK} is called every @var{MS}
milliseconds.  If it returns nil, the timer is removed.  Returns a
source ID.

@item cmacs-glib-idle-add CALLBACK
Add an idle source.  Returns a source ID.

@item cmacs-glib-source-remove ID
Remove a GLib source by its ID.
@end table

@section Files

@table @file
@item cmacs/glib/cmacs-glib-loop.c
C implementation (hooks into @file{src/process.c}).
@item cmacs/glib/cmacs-glib-loop.h
Header with @code{cmacs_glib_prepare()} and @code{cmacs_glib_dispatch()}.
@end table

@c ===================================================================
@node GObject Bridge
@chapter GObject Bridge

@cindex GObject, elisp bridge
@cindex properties, GObject
@cindex signals, GObject

CMacs wraps GObjects as Emacs @code{user-ptr} values with automatic
reference counting.  When a wrapped GObject is garbage-collected,
@code{g_object_unref()} is called.

@section Elisp Primitives

@table @code
@item gobject-new TYPE &rest PROPERTIES
Construct a new GObject.  @var{TYPE} is the GType name string.
Properties are passed as alternating key-value pairs.

@item gobject-p OBJECT
Return non-nil if @var{OBJECT} is a wrapped GObject.

@item gobject-type-name OBJECT
Return the GType name of @var{OBJECT} as a string.

@item gobject-get OBJECT PROPERTY
Get a property value.  Marshals GValues to elisp automatically.

@item gobject-set OBJECT PROPERTY VALUE
Set a property value.  Marshals elisp to GValues automatically.

@item gobject-connect OBJECT SIGNAL CALLBACK
Connect @var{CALLBACK} (an elisp function) to @var{SIGNAL}.  Returns a
handler ID.  The callback receives signal arguments marshalled to elisp.

@item gobject-disconnect OBJECT HANDLER-ID
Disconnect a signal handler.

@item gobject-list-properties OBJECT
Return a list of property names.

@item gobject-list-signals OBJECT
Return a list of signal names.
@end table

@section GValue Marshalling

The bridge automatically converts between GValues and elisp:

@multitable @columnfractions .3 .3 .4
@headitem GType @tab Elisp Type @tab Notes
@item @code{G_TYPE_BOOLEAN} @tab @code{t}/@code{nil} @tab
@item @code{G_TYPE_INT} etc. @tab fixnum @tab All integer types
@item @code{G_TYPE_FLOAT/DOUBLE} @tab float @tab
@item @code{G_TYPE_STRING} @tab string @tab
@item @code{G_TYPE_ENUM/FLAGS} @tab fixnum @tab
@item @code{G_TYPE_OBJECT} @tab user-ptr @tab Wrapped GObject
@end multitable

@section GClosure Integration

Elisp functions can be used anywhere GLib expects a @code{GClosure}.
The @code{gobject-connect} primitive creates a closure that marshals
signal parameters to elisp, calls the function, and marshals any
return value back.

@section Files

@table @file
@item cmacs/gobject/cmacs-gobject.c
GObject wrapping, property access, signal connection.
@item cmacs/gobject/cmacs-gclosure.c
GClosure marshalling bridge.
@end table

@c ===================================================================
@node GObject Introspection
@chapter GObject Introspection

@cindex GI, GObject Introspection
@cindex typelib

CMacs can load GI typelibs at runtime and call any function or method
from any GI-annotated library (GLib, GTK, GStreamer, etc.) directly
from elisp.

@section Elisp Primitives

@table @code
@item gi-require NAMESPACE &optional VERSION
Load a typelib.  Example: @code{(gi-require "GLib" "2.0")}.

@item gi-call NAMESPACE FUNCTION &rest ARGS
Call a GI function.  Arguments are marshalled based on GI type info.
@example
(gi-require "GLib" "2.0")
(gi-call "GLib" "get_user_name")  @result{} "zach"
@end example

@item gi-method OBJECT METHOD &rest ARGS
Call a method on a GObject instance.

@item gi-enum NAMESPACE ENUM MEMBER
Resolve an enum value.
@example
(gi-enum "GLib" "ChecksumType" "sha256")  @result{} 2
@end example

@item gi-list-functions NAMESPACE
Return a list of all function names in @var{NAMESPACE}.

@item gi-function-info NAMESPACE FUNCTION
Return an alist describing the function signature: @code{name},
@code{args} (list of @code{(name . type)}), and @code{return-type}.
@end table

@section High-Level Elisp Interface

The @file{lisp/cmacs/cmacs-gi.el} layer provides convenience wrappers:

@table @code
@item cmacs-gi-require NAMESPACE &optional VERSION
Load a namespace and auto-generate elisp wrapper functions.  After
@code{(cmacs-gi-require "Gtk" "4.0")}, you can call
@code{(gtk-application-new "com.example" 0)}.

@item cmacs-gi-describe-function NAMESPACE FUNCTION
Interactive: display the function signature in a help buffer.
@end table

@section Files

@table @file
@item cmacs/gi/cmacs-gi.c
C bridge: typelib loading, argument marshalling, function invocation.
@item lisp/cmacs/cmacs-gi.el
Elisp convenience layer with namespace auto-generation.
@end table

