@node Crispy C Scripting
@chapter Crispy C Scripting

@cindex Crispy
@cindex C scripting

CMacs integrates the Crispy library for evaluating C code at runtime.
Scripts are compiled to shared objects, cached by content hash (SHA256),
and loaded into the Emacs process via @code{g_module_open()}.  Because
scripts run in-process, they can call back into Emacs through the
CMacs API (@pxref{CMacs API}).

@section Writing Config in C

Place an @file{init.c} in @file{~/.config/cmacs/} (or
@code{user-emacs-directory} as a fallback).  CMacs checks
@code{$XDG_CONFIG_HOME/cmacs/} first, then @file{~/.emacs.d/}, so
you can keep CMacs config separate from vanilla Emacs.  The file is
loaded after @file{init.el} via @code{crispy-run}.  The CMacs API
headers and library are automatically available---no manual
@code{CRISPY_PARAMS} needed.

@xref{CMacs API, , Example init.c}, for a complete example.

@section Elisp Primitives

@table @code
@item crispy-eval CODE
Compile and execute inline C @var{CODE}.  Returns the exit code.
@item crispy-eval-string CODE
Execute C @var{CODE}, capturing stdout as a string.
@item crispy-run FILE &rest ARGS
Compile and execute a @file{.c} file with arguments.
@item crispy-compile FILE
Compile @var{FILE}, return the cached binary path.
@item crispy-repl-eval CODE
Evaluate in the persistent REPL; returns the exit code.  Preprocessor
directives, function definitions, and type declarations accumulate in
a preamble that stays in scope for later calls.  Output goes to
Emacs's own stdout.
@item crispy-repl-eval-string CODE
Like @code{crispy-repl-eval}, but returns the combined stdout and
stderr output as a string.  Bare expressions auto-print as
@samp{=> VALUE}.  Compilation errors signal @code{crispy-error} with
the gcc diagnostic.  This is what the @file{*crispy*} REPL buffer and
the scratchpad use.
@item crispy-repl-preamble
Return the accumulated REPL preamble as a string.
@item crispy-repl-reset
Reset persistent REPL state (clear the preamble).
@item crispy-cache-status
Return the crispy cache directory path.
@end table

@section The Crispy REPL

@cindex crispy REPL
@cindex colon commands

@kbd{M-x crispy-repl} opens the @file{*crispy*} buffer, a comint-based
C REPL backed by @code{crispy-repl-eval-string}.  The prompt is
read-only; @code{#include} directives, function definitions, and type
declarations accumulate in the preamble; bare expressions auto-print
as @samp{=> VALUE}.  @kbd{RET} evaluates only when braces balance ---
an unclosed @code{@{} continues on an indented new line (@kbd{C-c C-c}
or @kbd{M-RET} force-send, @kbd{C-j} forces a continuation).
@kbd{RET} on a blank line always sends, even when unbalanced, so a
typo like @code{g_print("world)";} cannot trap the REPL in
continuation mode --- gcc reports the real error instead; the
terminal REPL treats a blank line the same way.  @kbd{TAB} completes
colon commands.  REPL state is shared with @code{%crispy} scratchpad
blocks.

GHCi-style colon commands (aliases in parentheses):

@table @code
@item :help (:h :?)
Show command help.
@item :type EXPR (:t)
Show the C type of @var{EXPR}, with the preamble in scope.  Compiles
a probe using @code{__typeof__} and
@code{__builtin_types_compatible_p}.
@item :info NAME (:i)
cintrospect information: struct/enum layout, symbol info, or DEFUN
info for @var{NAME}.
@item :def NAME (:d)
Show @samp{file:line} of C function @var{NAME} and visit it in
another window.
@item :doc NAME
Docstring of Lisp function or variable @var{NAME}.
@item :browse GLOB (:b)
List C symbols matching @var{GLOB} via cintrospect.
@item :preamble (:p)
Show the accumulated preamble.
@item :load FILE (:l)
Load a C file into the preamble.
@item :reset (:r)
Reset REPL state (clear the preamble).
@item :clear
Clear the REPL buffer.
@item :cache
Show the crispy cache directory.
@item :elisp EXPR (:e)
Evaluate @var{EXPR} as Emacs Lisp.
@item :bacon CMD (:!)
Run @var{CMD} in bacon when running, else via the shell.
@item :quit (:q)
Bury the REPL buffer.
@end table

The cintrospect-backed commands (@code{:info}, @code{:def},
@code{:browse}) degrade gracefully with a message when cmacs is built
without @option{--with-cmacs-cintrospect}.

@section Scratch Buffer Polyglot Eval

@cindex scratchpad
@cindex polyglot scratch buffer

@code{cmacs-scratchpad-mode} (auto-enabled in @file{*scratch*} and
Doom scratch buffers; see @code{cmacs-scratchpad-buffer-regexp}) lets
the scratch buffer execute crispy and bacon blocks alongside ordinary
Emacs Lisp.  A block starts with a @code{%crispy} or @code{%bacon}
marker line and ends at a blank line, the next marker, previous
output, or end of buffer; without a marker the block is Emacs Lisp.

@example
;; plain elisp -- C-j still works, or C-c C-c:
(+ 1 2)

%crispy
for (int i = 0; i < 3; i++)
    g_print("%d\n", i);

%bacon
ls /tmp | head -2
@end example

@kbd{C-c C-c} (@code{cmacs-scratchpad-eval-block}) evaluates the
block at point and inserts the output inline below it; re-evaluation
replaces the previous output instead of stacking.  @kbd{C-c C-k}
(@code{cmacs-scratchpad-clear-output}) deletes the output at point
(prefix argument: all output in the buffer).  Crispy blocks share the
persistent REPL with the @file{*crispy*} buffer.

@section Command-line Usage

@cindex --crispy

@option{--crispy} runs crispy code from the command line without
starting the editor, analogous to @option{--batch} for Lisp.  It is
checked before any Emacs initialization, like @option{--bacon}:

@example
emacs --crispy SCRIPT [ARGS...]   # run a .c script; exit code propagates
emacs --crispy -i CODE            # run inline C statements
emacs --crispy -                  # read a full script from stdin
emacs --crispy                    # tty: interactive terminal REPL;
                                  # pipe: evaluate stdin quietly
@end example

With no arguments and piped stdin, the input is evaluated with full
REPL semantics (preamble accumulation, @samp{=> VALUE} auto-print,
multi-line @code{@{ @}} blocks) but with no banner and no prompts ---
only the code's own output reaches stdout; compile errors go to
stderr and the exit code is nonzero.  As at the REPL prompt, plain
statements do not persist between chunks --- group statements that
share locals in one @code{@{ ... @}} block.

@option{-i} wraps bare statements in a @code{main()} with GLib
includes; script-file and explicit @option{-} stdin modes expect a
complete translation unit with its own @code{main()}.  Since no Emacs is running in this
mode, the CMacs API's in-process DIRECT transport is unavailable, but
the other transports can reach a separately running cmacs.

@section Files

@table @file
@item cmacs/crispy/cmacs-crispy.c
C primitive implementation.  Registers the CMacs API direct dispatch
table so crispy scripts can call back into Emacs without IPC.  Also
provides @code{cmacs_crispy_main}, the @option{--crispy} entry point.
@item lisp/cmacs/cmacs-crispy.el
Elisp layer: REPL with colon commands, minor mode, region/buffer
evaluation.
@item lisp/cmacs/cmacs-scratchpad.el
Polyglot scratch-buffer eval (@code{%crispy} / @code{%bacon} blocks).
@end table

@c ===================================================================
@node Gowl Wayland Compositor
@chapter Gowl Wayland Compositor

@cindex Gowl
@cindex Wayland compositor
@cindex window manager
@cindex client embedding

Gowl is a wlroots-based Wayland compositor embedded in CMacs.  When
launched with @option{--gowl}, Emacs becomes a tiling window manager:
the editor renders as a Wayland client inside its own compositor, and
other Wayland applications tile alongside or embed directly inside
Emacs windows.

The integration provides 49+ C primitives (DEFUNs) for full compositor
control from Elisp.

@section Starting the Compositor

@example
src/emacs --gowl
@end example

@noindent
Inside Emacs, the compositor is controlled via @code{cmacs-gowl-mode},
which is auto-enabled by @option{--gowl}.

@subsection Running as a GDM Login Session (in-repo debug build)
@cindex GDM session
@cindex debug session
@cindex clamshell testing

To test compositor changes in a @emph{real seat session} (not nested)
without rebuilding an OS image---useful on an immutable/bootc host---add a
custom Wayland session that launches the freshly-built in-repo binary, then
pick @samp{CMacs (Debug)} from GDM's gear menu on the login screen.

First, a standup script that exports the same module/typelib environment as
@code{just gowl} and @emph{forces the seat backend}.  On an ostree/bootc
host put it in @file{/usr/local/bin} (which is @file{/var/usrlocal/bin}:
writable, on @env{PATH}, and persistent across reboots and image upgrades);
@code{chmod 0755} it and @code{restorecon -F} on SELinux:

@verbatim
# /usr/local/bin/cmacs-debug
#!/bin/bash
REPO=/path/to/cmacs          # <-- this checkout
# A real seat session has no parent compositor, but the systemd --user
# env can leak a stale WAYLAND_DISPLAY/DISPLAY (+ a leftover wayland-0
# socket).  cmacs treats a non-empty WAYLAND_DISPLAY as "nested" and forces
# WLR_BACKENDS=wayland, which then fails to connect.  Force the seat
# backend: cmacs only does setenv(WLR_BACKENDS,...,0) (no-overwrite), so
# this wins, and wlroots autocreate honours WLR_BACKENDS over the
# WAYLAND_DISPLAY/GDK heuristics.
unset WAYLAND_DISPLAY WAYLAND_SOCKET DISPLAY
export WLR_BACKENDS=libinput,drm
cd "$REPO" || exit 1
export CMACS_MODULE_DIR="$REPO/cmacs/bacon/modules"
export CMACS_GSURF_MODULE_DIR="$REPO/cmacs/gsurf/modules"
export CMACS_GOWL_MODULE_DIR="$REPO/deps/gowl/build/release/modules"
export CMACS_SCREENSAVER_MODULE_DIR="$REPO/deps/screensavers/build/release"
export CMACS_SCREENSAVER_RENDER_BIN="$REPO/src/cmacs-screensaver-render"
export GI_TYPELIB_PATH="$REPO/deps/libregnum/build/release/gir:$REPO/deps/libregnum/deps/graylib/build/gir${GI_TYPELIB_PATH:+:$GI_TYPELIB_PATH}"
exec "$REPO/src/emacs" --gowl "$@"
@end verbatim

Then a Wayland session entry.  GDM hands the @code{Exec} value to
@command{gdm-wayland-session}, which accepts @emph{one} positional
argument, so @code{Exec} must be a @emph{single bare token} --- no
@samp{bash -lc "..."} (the quotes are split and the session dies with
@samp{gdm-wayland-session takes one argument} before cmacs launches).  Use
@file{/usr/local/share/wayland-sessions} (also persistent);
@file{/usr/share/wayland-sessions} works too but is wiped on the next
reboot if it was only made writable via @code{bootc usr-overlay}:

@verbatim
# /usr/local/share/wayland-sessions/cmacs-debug.desktop
[Desktop Entry]
Name=CMacs (Debug)
Comment=CMacs in-repo dev build (cmacs --gowl)
Exec=/usr/local/bin/cmacs-debug
Type=Application
DesktopNames=CMacs-Debug:wlroots
@end verbatim

@code{DesktopNames} ends in @code{:wlroots} so @env{XDG_CURRENT_DESKTOP}
becomes @code{CMacs-Debug:wlroots}.  The @code{wlroots} element is what
the @command{xdg-desktop-portal-wlr} backend matches on (its
@code{UseIn} lists @code{wlroots;sway;Wayfire;river;phosh;Hyprland}), so
screen-sharing apps (Zoom, OBS, browsers) reach the wlroots
@code{ScreenCast} portal instead of the GNOME backend, which fails under
gowl because it requires Mutter.  Without @code{:wlroots} the portal
frontend finds no @code{ScreenCast} backend and the share picker
silently does nothing.

@emph{Monitor vs window capture.}  Sharing a whole monitor works whenever
gowl is built against wlroots 0.19 or 0.20.  Sharing a @emph{single
window} (Zoom's ``use system window capture'', OBS window sources)
additionally needs gowl built against @emph{wlroots 0.20+} --- it
requires the @code{ext-image-copy-capture} + @code{ext-foreign-toplevel}
protocols that 0.19 lacks.  gowl auto-builds against the newest wlroots
present; on a host with only 0.19 (e.g.@: Fedora 42/43) window capture is
unavailable and the app's window list comes up empty (share a monitor
instead).

Log out, pick @samp{CMacs (Debug)}, then rebuild (@code{rm -f src/temacs &&
make}) and log back in to test changes --- no OS image rebuild.  For
@emph{nested} testing inside an existing session use @code{just gowl}
instead (it keeps @env{WAYLAND_DISPLAY} so wlroots uses the nested
backend).  Diagnose failures from @code{journalctl -b | grep -iE
'gdm-wayland-session|cmacs|gowl|wlr|backend'}.

@subsection Running X11 Applications (XWayland)
@cindex XWayland
@cindex X11 applications

gowl runs its own rootless XWayland server, so unmodified X11
applications --- including non-Wayland toolkits and raylib/GLFW apps
such as @samp{gst --lrg=2d} --- run inside the session.  On startup the
compositor sets @env{DISPLAY} to gowl's own X server (e.g.@: @samp{:1}),
distinct from any parent session's.

@code{gowl-spawn} (and the launch commands built on it) point spawned
X11 clients at gowl's XWayland by setting @env{DISPLAY} to
@code{gowl_compositor_get_xwayland_display}, and set Wayland toolkit
hints so GTK/Qt/SDL/Electron prefer Wayland.  X11 windows are managed
like native ones (tiled, tagged, focusable).

X11 @emph{override-redirect} surfaces --- popup menus, combo dropdowns,
tooltips, and app confirmation dialogs (e.g.@: Zoom's ``End meeting /
Leave'') --- take a dedicated @emph{unmanaged} path: they float at their
own position with no border, take keyboard focus if they request it, and
hold an exclusive grab so the compositor will not steal focus back or
deactivate their parent window while they are up.  Without this a menu
would lose its grab the instant focus moved and dismiss itself after a
fraction of a second.  Set @env{GOWL_DEBUG_XWAYLAND=1} in the session
environment to log each X11 surface as it maps (override-redirect vs
managed, with class and title).

This needs the @command{Xwayland} binary (Fedora:
@code{xorg-x11-server-Xwayland}) and a gowl built with
@code{BUILD_XWAYLAND=1} (the default); otherwise @env{DISPLAY} is left
unset and only Wayland-native clients run.

@subsection Input Capture / KVM (deskflow)
@cindex deskflow
@cindex InputCapture
@cindex RemoteDesktop
@cindex KVM

Software-KVM tools such as deskflow (and input-leap / Synergy) share one
keyboard and mouse across machines by moving the pointer off a screen edge.
On Wayland this needs the freedesktop @emph{InputCapture} portal (to
capture local input when sharing out) and the @emph{RemoteDesktop} portal
(to inject input when sharing in).  Only GNOME's Mutter-backed portal
implements InputCapture for the wlroots world, which is why deskflow works
under GNOME but logs @samp{CreateSession failed} under a plain wlroots
compositor.

gowl implements both portals itself, so deskflow works under
@command{cmacs --gowl} for both the server (capture) and client (inject)
roles.  gowl core carries a D-Bus-free capture state machine driven over a
private Wayland protocol, and a standalone @command{xdg-desktop-portal-gowl}
binary (built when @code{libeis} is present) owns the
@code{org.freedesktop.impl.portal.desktop.gowl} D-Bus name and runs the EIS
server deskflow connects to.  The full architecture is documented in gowl's
@file{docs/input-capture.org}.

To enable it: install @code{libeis-devel}, then @code{make install} installs
the portal binary, its @file{.portal} registration, a @file{portals.conf}
routing InputCapture/RemoteDesktop to gowl (ScreenCast still goes to
@command{xdg-desktop-portal-wlr}), and a D-Bus @file{.service}.  The session's
@env{XDG_CURRENT_DESKTOP} must contain @code{gowl}; @command{install-wm} sets
@code{DesktopNames=CMacs:gowl} accordingly.

Input capture sees every keystroke and injection can drive any application;
access is gated by the @command{xdg-desktop-portal} permission model.  The
private Wayland protocol trusts only its single client (the portal binary,
which the compositor launches) and is not a public interface.

Troubleshooting: if deskflow still logs @samp{CreateSession failed}, the
backend is not routed---confirm @code{gowl} is in @env{XDG_CURRENT_DESKTOP},
the @file{gowl.portal} file is installed, and @code{libeis} was present at
build time (run @command{xdg-desktop-portal} with @env{G_MESSAGES_DEBUG=all}
to see the selected backend).  If instead the pointer reaches the edge but
never crosses (deskflow loops activating and releasing the capture), check the
deskflow side: its screen layout must have a neighbour on that edge.  Running
@command{deskflow-core server} standalone does @emph{not} translate the
deskflow GUI's screen grid into links, so launch deskflow via its GUI or set
@code{externalConfig=true} in its config; deskflow's debug log
(@code{[log] level=7}) shows @samp{no neighbor on @var{side}} when the link is
missing.  The deeper gowl-side cause of that loop---a missing EIS device
region---is fixed; see the Troubleshooting section of gowl's
@file{docs/input-capture.org}.  If the cursor crosses fine but typing on the
remote spams @samp{failed to open x11 default display}, deskflow (a Flatpak) is
denied X11: it still uses an X11 display to translate keycodes even on Wayland,
and its default @code{fallback-x11} permission withholds X11 while Wayland is
present.  gowl runs XWayland (@env{DISPLAY=:0}), so grant access with
@code{flatpak override --user --socket=x11 org.deskflow.deskflow} and restart
deskflow.  This is a deskflow/Flatpak limitation, not a gowl bug.

@section Window Management

@table @code
@item cmacs-gowl-spawn-command COMMAND
Launch a Wayland client.
@item cmacs-gowl-focus-window
Interactively select and focus a client window.
@item cmacs-gowl-close-window
Interactively select and close a client window.
@item cmacs-gowl-list-windows
Display all managed windows in a help buffer.
@item cmacs-gowl-view-tag TAG
Switch to a tag (workspace) 1--9 (full view: re-tiles and refocuses).
@item cmacs-gowl-switch-tag
Pick a tag from a list annotated with what's open, and switch to it.
@item cmacs-gowl-switch-to-app
Pick any open window (across tags/monitors) and jump to it.
@item cmacs-gowl-send-to-tag TAG
Move focused client to a tag 1--9.
@item cmacs-gowl-toggle-tag TAG
Toggle a tag's visibility 1--9.
@item cmacs-gowl-assign-monitor-tags
Give each monitor its own tag (monitor 1 @result{} tag 1, 2 @result{}
tag 2, @dots{}).  Re-runnable after hotplugging a monitor.
@item cmacs-gowl-launch-in-tag TAG
Pick an application (Emacs completion over @file{.desktop} files) and
launch it on @var{TAG}.
@item cmacs-gowl-bemenu-in-tag TAG
Like the above, but pick via bemenu (dmenu mode).
@item cmacs-gowl-spawn-in-tag COMMAND TAG
Spawn @var{COMMAND} as a client placed directly on @var{TAG}.
@item cmacs-gowl-set-layout LAYOUT
Set the tiling layout (tile, monocle, float).
@item cmacs-gowl-toggle-vsplit
Toggle the vsplit tile orientation on the focused monitor.  With vsplit
on, the master row is on top and the stack row on the bottom (the
existing window stays on top, new windows underneath); with it off, the
normal left/right split is restored.
@end table

@section Tags and Workspaces
@anchor{Tags and Workspaces}

@cindex tags
@cindex workspaces

Gowl uses the dwm/dwl @dfn{tag} model: nine tags (1--9), each a bit in
a per-monitor bitmask.  Viewing a tag shows the clients tagged with it
and hides the rest.  Emacs's own frame is an ordinary tiled client on
tag 1, so switching to an empty tag hides Emacs (classic dwm
behaviour); @kbd{Super+1} brings it back.  This complements client
embedding: @code{gowl-embed} runs an app @emph{inside} an Emacs window,
whereas the launch-into-tag commands run an app as an independent
client in another workspace.

@subsection Switching from M-x

Everything works without keybinds:

@table @code
@item cmacs-gowl-view-tag TAG
Switch the focused monitor to @var{TAG}.  Performs a full view ---
shows/hides clients, re-tiles, and moves focus, exactly like
@kbd{Super+N}.
@item cmacs-gowl-switch-tag
A picker listing the nine tags, each annotated with what's open on it;
select one to switch to it.
@item cmacs-gowl-switch-to-app
A window picker listing every open window across all tags and monitors
(title, app-id, and its tag).  Selecting one reveals that window's tag
on its monitor and focuses it --- the way to reach an app open in
another tag or on another monitor.  Embedded clients are excluded
(reach those with @kbd{C-x b}).
@end table

@subsection Multi-monitor

Tags are @emph{per-monitor}: each output has its own independent tag
set, so each tag can live on its own monitor.  On mode start (when
@code{cmacs-gowl-monitor-tags-on-launch} is non-@code{nil}, the
default) each monitor is given its own initial tag --- the main
(first) monitor views tag 1, the second views tag 2, and so on, up to
nine monitors.  Re-run @code{cmacs-gowl-assign-monitor-tags} after
hotplugging a monitor.

The tag-action keybinds act on the @emph{focused} monitor: focus one
with @kbd{Super+,} / @kbd{Super+.}, then @kbd{Super+N} switches that
monitor's tags.  Each monitor's bar shows its own tag state.  Launching
into a tag follows the tag to its monitor:
@code{cmacs-gowl-spawn-in-tag} places the client on the monitor
currently showing the target tag, so launching on tag 2 lands on the
monitor whose workspace is tag 2.

@subsection Restored keybindings

Under @option{--gowl} the compositor starts with an empty keybind
table, so @code{cmacs-gowl-mode} installs the standard dwm-style set on
enable (controlled by @code{cmacs-gowl-default-keybindings}, default
on).  The compositor evaluates these before forwarding keys to the
focused surface, so they work globally even while Emacs or an embedded
client holds focus.

@table @kbd
@item Super+Return
Launch terminal (@code{cmacs-gowl-terminal-command}).
@item Super+p
Launch menu (@code{cmacs-gowl-menu-command}, wofi).
@item Super+Shift+p
Run dialog (@code{cmacs-gowl-run-command}, wofi).
@item Super+Shift+c
Kill the focused client.
@item Super+j / Super+k
Focus next / previous in the stack.
@item Super+h / Super+l
Shrink / grow the master area.
@item Super+i / Super+d
Increment / decrement the master count.
@item Super+Shift+Return
Zoom (promote to master).
@item Super+t / f / m
Tile / float / monocle layout.
@item Super+v
Vsplit on: master row on top, stack row on the bottom.
@item Super+Shift+v
Vsplit off: restore the normal left/right split.
@item Super+space
Toggle floating; @kbd{Super+Shift+space} toggles fullscreen.
@item Super+1..9
View tag N; add @kbd{Shift} to move the focused client to it, @kbd{Ctrl}
to toggle its visibility, @kbd{Shift+Ctrl} to toggle it on the client.
@item Super+0
View all tags; @kbd{Super+Shift+0} tags the client to all.
@item Super+, / Super+.
Focus previous / next monitor (@kbd{Shift} moves the client).
@item Super+Shift+q
Quit the compositor.  @strong{Under @option{--gowl} this quits Emacs.}
@item Super+Shift+r
Reload config.
@end table

The compositor interprets a tag-action keybind argument as a raw tag
@emph{bitmask} (@code{atoi(arg) & TAGMASK}), not a 1-based tag number,
and an argument of @code{"0"} is a no-op.  @code{cmacs-gowl-mode}
therefore binds tag N to the bitmask @code{(ash 1 (1- N))} and ``all''
to @code{(1<<9)-1} = 511.  (gowl's shipped @file{default-config.c}
passes plain @code{"1".."9"/"0"}, which is only correct for tags 1 and
2; the cmacs installer passes real bitmasks.)

@subsection Launching applications into a tag

@code{cmacs-gowl-spawn-in-tag} registers the spawned PID with the
compositor via @code{gowl-pretag-pid}, so the client adopts the tag's
bitmask the instant it maps --- flash-free and race-free.
@code{cmacs-gowl-bemenu-in-tag} runs a dmenu-mode picker (reading the
app list on stdin) so cmacs owns the spawn and thus the PID, and shows
the picker UI inside gowl by pointing @env{WAYLAND_DISPLAY} at the
compositor socket.  The picker binary is
@code{cmacs-gowl-dmenu-command} (default @code{"bemenu"}), deliberately
separate from the @kbd{Super+p} launcher (@code{cmacs-gowl-menu-command},
default @code{"wofi --show drun"}): the picker needs a dmenu-mode binary
(stdin to stdout), not a drun/run launcher.  @kbd{Super+p} keeps the
classic behaviour (the launcher runs the chosen app itself, on the
current tag).

@section Monitor Management

@cindex monitor
@cindex output
@cindex resolution
@cindex scale
@cindex transform
@cindex HiDPI

Gowl provides full control over connected monitors (outputs): listing,
mode changes, scale adjustments, transforms, and enable/disable.  All
operations work on both physical monitors (DRM backend) and nested
Wayland outputs.

@subsection Listing Monitors

@code{cmacs-gowl-list-monitors} displays a table of all outputs:

@example
Name    Mode          Position  Scale  Status   Transform
WL-1    1280x720@@60   0,0      1.0    enabled  normal
DP-1    2560x1440@@144  1280,0  1.0    enabled  normal
@end example

@noindent
From the shell:

@example
bacon> cmacsgi monitor list
WL-1
DP-1

bacon> cmacsgi monitor info WL-1
name=WL-1
mode=1280x720@@60
position=0,0
scale=1.000000
enabled=t
transform=0
@end example

@subsection Changing Resolution

@code{cmacs-gowl-set-resolution} prompts with the monitor's advertised
modes.  For outputs with no advertised modes (common for nested Wayland
sessions), specify a custom mode directly.

@example
;; From Elisp:
(gowl-set-monitor-mode 1920 1080 0 (gowl-find-monitor "WL-1"))

;; From the shell:
bacon> cmacsgi monitor set-mode WL-1 1920x1080
bacon> cmacsgi monitor set-mode DP-1 2560x1440@@144000
@end example

@noindent
The refresh rate is in millihertz (60000 = 60@dmn{Hz}).  Use 0 to match
any refresh rate, or omit the @code{@@RATE} suffix.

@subsection Scaling

Scale controls the output DPI@.  A scale of 2.0 doubles the pixel
density, halving the logical resolution.

@example
;; HiDPI (2x):
(gowl-set-monitor-scale 2.0 (gowl-find-monitor "WL-1"))

;; Lower density for more screen real-estate:
(gowl-set-monitor-scale 0.8 (gowl-find-monitor "WL-1"))
@end example

@example
bacon> cmacsgi monitor scale WL-1 2.0
bacon> cmacsgi monitor scale WL-1 0.8
bacon> cmacsgi monitor scale WL-1 1.0
@end example

@noindent
At scale 0.8 on a 1280x720 output the logical area grows to 1600x900;
at scale 2.0 it shrinks to 640x360.  Clients automatically resize to
fill the new logical area.

@subsection Transform (Rotation/Flip)

Transform values follow the Wayland @code{wl_output_transform} enum:

@multitable @columnfractions .15 .25 .6
@headitem Value @tab Name @tab Effect
@item 0 @tab normal @tab No rotation
@item 1 @tab 90 @tab Rotate 90@textdegree{} clockwise
@item 2 @tab 180 @tab Rotate 180@textdegree{}
@item 3 @tab 270 @tab Rotate 270@textdegree{} clockwise
@item 4 @tab flipped @tab Horizontal flip
@item 5 @tab flipped-90 @tab Flip + rotate 90@textdegree{}
@item 6 @tab flipped-180 @tab Flip + rotate 180@textdegree{}
@item 7 @tab flipped-270 @tab Flip + rotate 270@textdegree{}
@end multitable

@example
bacon> cmacsgi monitor transform WL-1 1   @r{;; rotate 90@textdegree{}}
bacon> cmacsgi monitor transform WL-1 0   @r{;; back to normal}
@end example

@subsubsection Compositor: lock + screensaver wallpaper

The @code{gowl} command group covers session lock/unlock and the animated
libregnum screensaver wallpaper.  (Lock/unlock are exposed over cmacsgi,
D-Bus, and @command{emacsctl}, but deliberately @emph{not} over MCP.)

@example
bacon> cmacsgi gowl lock
bacon> cmacsgi gowl screensaver blackhole-cool
bacon> cmacsgi gowl screensaver-stop
bacon> cmacsgi gowl screensaver-configs
@end example

@subsubsection Persisting Transform (and Other Monitor Settings) via YAML

For a machine where a display always wants the same orientation,
scale, or position (a tablet with a portrait-default panel, an
external monitor placed left of the laptop, a HiDPI screen at
non-default scale), put the override in
@file{~/.config/gowl/config.yaml} under the @code{monitors:} key so
it applies on every boot:

@example
monitors:
  eDP-1:                 @r{# mobile screen: boot already-rotated}
    transform: 90
  HDMI-A-1:              @r{# external 1440p at 144 Hz, placed right}
    width: 2560
    height: 1440
    refresh: 144.0
    x: 1920
    y: 0
    scale: 1.0
    enabled: true
@end example

Every field is independently optional --- set only what you want to
override.  Unset fields are left at the compositor's defaults, so a
@code{transform: 90} line on its own is valid.  @code{transform}
accepts either the integer code (0--7) or one of the canonical names
(@code{normal}, @code{90}, @code{180}, @code{270}, @code{flipped},
@code{flipped-90}, @code{flipped-180}, @code{flipped-270}).

Discover output names via @kbd{M-x cmacs-gowl-list-monitors} or
@code{cmacsgi monitor list}.

Changes take effect live on @kbd{M-x cmacs-gowl-reload-config},
@code{cmacsgi compositor reload}, or the @kbd{Mod+R} keybind --- no
compositor restart needed.

@subsection Enable / Disable

@example
bacon> cmacsgi monitor disable DP-2
bacon> cmacsgi monitor enable DP-2
@end example

@noindent
Or interactively: @kbd{M-x cmacs-gowl-toggle-monitor}.

@subsection Closed Laptop Lid / Off Displays

gowl automatically powers off the laptop's built-in panel (an
@code{eDP}/@code{LVDS}/@code{DSI} connector) while the lid is shut
@emph{and} at least one external display is connected.  The selected
monitor and any windows on the internal panel migrate to the external
output, and the internal panel is removed from the layout so the cursor
and newly launched windows never land on the dark screen.  This is what
you want when docking a closed laptop to a single external monitor:
gowl renders only on the attached display.

The behaviour is live and ordering-independent:

@itemize
@item
Booting @code{cmacs --gowl} with the lid already shut leaves the
internal panel off from the first frame (the initial state is read from
@file{/proc/acpi/button/lid}).
@item
Closing or opening the lid while running toggles the panel via the
libinput lid switch.
@item
Single-display safety: if the lid is shut and there is @emph{no}
external display, the internal panel stays on---gowl never blanks the
only screen.  Unplugging the external while the lid is shut brings the
internal panel back automatically.
@item
Idempotent (clamshell boot): if the DRM driver already powered the panel
down (the enable commit for a shut lid fails, so it comes up
@code{enabled=nil} but still mapped), gowl still removes it from the
output layout, so a ``half-disabled'' panel never lingers there stealing
the cursor and newly launched windows.  gowl does @emph{not} destroy the
disabled output's @code{wlr_scene_output}: a disabled output stops
emitting frame events, so its scene output never commits again.  (An
earlier version destroyed it and hit a use-after-free in the render
thread.)
@end itemize

This is controlled by the @code{manage-lid} compositor setting
(default on).  Turn it off at runtime to keep driving a closed-lid
panel:

@lisp
(gobject-set (gowl-config-object) "manage-lid" nil)
@end lisp

@subsection Interactive Commands

@table @code
@item cmacs-gowl-list-monitors
Show a table of all outputs with mode, position, scale, and status.
@item cmacs-gowl-monitor-info
Show detailed info for a selected monitor.
@item cmacs-gowl-set-resolution
Interactively choose a monitor and set its resolution.
@item cmacs-gowl-set-scale SCALE
Set the scale factor on a selected monitor.
@item cmacs-gowl-set-transform
Interactively choose a transform for a selected monitor.
@item cmacs-gowl-toggle-monitor
Enable or disable a selected monitor.
@end table

@subsection How It Works

When a mode, scale, or transform change is committed via
@code{wlr_output_commit_state()}, gowl updates the monitor geometry by
querying @code{wlr_output_layout_get_box()}, which returns the correct
logical dimensions accounting for scale and transform.  This matches the
approach used by dwl.

The compositor then calls @code{gowl_compositor_arrange()}, which
re-tiles all clients on the affected monitor.  Fullscreen clients
(including the Emacs frame in standalone @option{--gowl} mode) are
resized to fill the full monitor area.  Normal tiling clients are
re-laid-out by the active layout function (tile, monocle, etc.).

On the Wayland backend (nested compositors),
@code{on_layout_change()} typically fires synchronously during the
output commit.  As a safety net, each setter also queries the layout
box and calls @code{arrange()} directly, ensuring correct behavior
even if the asynchronous path is delayed.

@section Touchpad and Pointer Input
@cindex touchpad
@cindex tap to click
@cindex natural scrolling
@cindex gestures

gowl applies GNOME-like defaults to libinput @emph{touchpads} as they
connect (@code{create_pointer} in @file{gowl-compositor.c}):

@itemize
@item
@emph{Tap-to-click} and @emph{tap-and-drag} enabled.
@item
@emph{Natural (reverse) scrolling} --- two fingers up scrolls the content
up, matching GNOME.  Applied only to touchpads, so a plain mouse keeps its
normal wheel direction.
@item
@emph{Two-finger scroll} and @emph{disable-while-typing}.
@end itemize

A device is treated as a touchpad when libinput reports a non-zero tap
finger count (mice report zero).

@emph{Gestures.}  Swipe, pinch, and hold events are relayed to the focused
client through the pointer-gestures protocol
(@code{zwp_pointer_gestures_v1}), so applications get three-finger swipe,
pinch-to-zoom, etc.  Two-finger scrolling is ordinary pointer-axis
scrolling and works everywhere.

These are compiled-in defaults (no per-device config knob yet).  Because
the static @file{libgowl.a} now calls @code{libinput_device_config_*}
directly, cmacs links @code{libinput} (the @code{CMACS_GOWL_DEPS}
pkg-config list in @file{configure.ac}).

@section Client Embedding

Wayland clients can render @emph{inside} Emacs buffer windows.  The
client's scene node is reparented into Emacs's compositor scene tree
and positioned over the window body area.  The compositor renders the
client directly---no pixel copying, no GPU readback.  Input is routed
by focus-follows-mouse.

@example
M-x gowl-embed RET foot RET
@end example

@noindent
This spawns @command{foot} and embeds it in the current Emacs window.
The modeline, fringes, and header line remain visible around the
embedded client.

@subsection Embedding Commands

@table @code
@item gowl-embed COMMAND
Spawn @var{COMMAND} and embed it in the current Emacs window.
@item gowl-embed-client CLIENT
Embed an existing gowl client in the current window.
@item gowl-unembed
Release the embedded client back to normal tiling.
@end table

@subsection How It Works

@enumerate
@item
@code{gowl-embed} spawns a Wayland client and pre-registers its PID
so the compositor floats and hides it on map.
@item
A 100ms timer polls @code{gowl-list-clients} to match the PID.
@item
Once matched, the client is positioned on the compositor's OVERLAY
layer at the Emacs window's monitor-absolute coordinates
(@code{gowl-position-embedded}).  The frame's compositor position
(from @code{gowl-client-info} geometry) is added to the
frame-relative window coordinates to account for the bar height
and vanitygaps.
@item
@code{window-configuration-change-hook} keeps the overlay in sync
when windows resize, split, or switch buffers.
@item
A 1-second health timer detects client exit (by PID comparison) and
cleans up the placeholder buffer.
@end enumerate

@subsection Lifecycle

@table @strong
@item Client exits
Health timer detects it gone from @code{gowl-list-clients} (compared
by PID), kills the placeholder buffer.
@item Buffer killed
@code{kill-buffer-hook} closes the Wayland client.
@item Window hidden
Overlay is hidden.  Switching back to the buffer re-shows it.
@end table

@subsection Keyboard Focus (ESC Escape-Hatch)

Embedded clients receive keyboard input directly from the compositor.
An ESC-based escape hatch lets you switch between the embedded client
and Emacs:

@table @strong
@item ESC (single press)
Returns keyboard focus to Emacs.  Subsequent keys are processed by
Emacs (e.g.@: Evil normal-mode commands).
@item ESC ESC (double press)
Sends a single ESC to the embedded client (useful for apps that use
ESC, like vim or bash with @code{set -o vi}).
@item i / a (Evil normal state)
Gives keyboard focus back to the embedded client.  Also works with
@kbd{I}, @kbd{A}, @kbd{o}, @kbd{O}, and @kbd{RET}.
@item Click
Clicking on the embedded area also returns keyboard focus to the
embedded client.
@end table

The escape hatch uses a 200@dmn{ms} timer to distinguish single from
double ESC@.  Pressing ESC followed by another key within the timeout
sends the second key to Emacs.

The underlying primitive @code{gowl-embed-focus} can be called from
Elisp to programmatically give keyboard focus to any embedded client.

