@subsection Clipboard Sync

@cindex clipboard sync
@cindex kill-ring, Wayland sync

@code{cmacs-gowl-clipboard-sync-mode} provides bidirectional
clipboard synchronization between the Wayland compositor and the
Emacs kill-ring.

@defun cmacs-gowl-clipboard-sync-mode &optional arg
Global minor mode.  When enabled:
@itemize @bullet
@item
Text copied by Wayland clients is pushed to the kill-ring.
@item
Text killed in Emacs is placed on the Wayland clipboard.
@item
@kbd{C-y} checks the Wayland clipboard before the kill-ring.
@end itemize
@end defun

@noindent
Enable in your config:

@example
(cmacs-gowl-clipboard-sync-mode 1)
@end example

@noindent
The @code{GowlSeat} object emits two signals for clipboard
monitoring:

@table @code
@item clipboard-changed
Fired when a Wayland client sets the clipboard.
@item primary-selection-changed
Fired when a Wayland client sets the primary selection.
@end table

@noindent
These signals only fire for client-initiated changes, not when
Emacs sets the clipboard via @code{gowl-clipboard-set}, so no
infinite loop occurs.

@subsubsection Implementation notes

Clipboard reads from client-owned sources (including clients
embedded via @code{gowl-embed-app}) are asynchronous.  The signal
handler schedules an fd watch via
@code{gowl_seat_read_clipboard_async} and returns immediately; the
wl dispatch loop then flushes the @code{wl_data_source.send} event
to the owning client, the client writes to a pipe, and a
@code{WL_EVENT_READABLE} callback drains the data and hands it to
the Emacs main thread via @code{g_idle_add}.  A synchronous read
inside the signal handler would see EOF immediately because the
event hasn't been flushed to the client yet.

The sync @code{gowl-clipboard-get} DEFUN still exists for
peeking at the current clipboard state; it works reliably for
compositor-owned sources (text set via @code{gowl-clipboard-set})
and is used by @code{interprogram-paste-function}.

