@c ===================================================================
@node Podomation Automation
@chapter Podomation Automation

@cindex podomation
@cindex automation engine

Podomation is a GObject-native, event-driven automation engine with 80+
modules, a custom DSL, REPL, admin API, and state persistence.  CMacs
embeds it via @code{pod_engine_start_embedded()}, running on the existing
GMainContext without a separate event loop.

Enable with @option{--with-cmacs-podomation} at configure time.

@menu
* Podomation Architecture::     Embedding model and design.
* Podomation Configuration::    Customize variables and .pod files.
* Podomation DSL::              The event-binding language.
* Podomation REPL::             Interactive evaluation.
* Podomation Hook Bridge::      Emacs hooks as podomation events.
* Podomation Modules::          Built-in cmacs and gowl modules.
* Podomation Plugin Modules::   80+ loadable .so plugins.
* Podomation DEFUNs::           Elisp primitives.
* Podomation Elisp Layer::      Convenience commands and functions.
* Podomation Bacon Builtin::    The pod shell command.
* Podomation GI Access::        GObject Introspection.
* Podomation Examples::         DSL recipes.
* Podomation File Reference::   Source file listing.
@end menu

@node Podomation Architecture
@section Architecture

@subsection Embedding Model

CMacs uses @code{PodRepl} to create the engine internally.  The REPL's
engine (obtained via @code{pod_repl_get_engine()}) is the engine ---
REPL and DEFUNs share the same module manager and pod namespace.

The engine runs on the default @code{GMainContext} integrated into
Emacs's @code{pselect()} loop via @file{cmacs-glib-loop.c}.  No
separate thread or event loop is needed.

@subsection Built-in Modules

Two @code{PodModule} subclasses are compiled into @command{temacs}
(not loaded as @file{.so} plugins).  They are registered with
@code{pod_module_manager_register()} during
@code{cmacs-podomation-start}, after the plugin @file{.so} files load:

@table @code
@item cmacs
Bridges Emacs events and eval into the DSL.
@item gowl
Bridges compositor events and commands (requires
@option{--with-cmacs-gowl}).
@end table

Both are DUAL modules implementing @code{PodEventSource} and
@code{PodEventHandler} interfaces via
@code{G_DEFINE_FINAL_TYPE_WITH_CODE} with
@code{G_IMPLEMENT_INTERFACE}.

@subsection Lisp Eval Safety

The cmacs module's @code{eval} and @code{eval_async} handlers use
@code{g_idle_add()} to post work to the GMainContext.  This avoids the
@code{waiting_for_input} SIGABRT crash that would occur if Lisp were
evaluated synchronously from a GLib dispatch callback.

The gowl module's handlers call @code{cmacs_dispatch_gowl_*()} functions
directly --- no idle dispatch needed since they don't evaluate Lisp.

@subsection pdumper Safety

The @code{PodEngine} is never created during C init.
@code{init_cmacs_podomation()} is intentionally empty.  The engine is
created from Elisp only, making it pdumper-safe.

@node Podomation Configuration
@section Configuration

@defvar cmacs-podomation-auto-start
When non-nil, start the engine automatically during init.
Default: @code{nil}.
@end defvar

@defvar cmacs-podomation-pod-files
List of @file{.pod} files to load when the engine starts.
@end defvar

@defvar cmacs-podomation-module-dirs
Additional directories to scan for podomation module @file{.so} files.
@end defvar

Start the engine manually:

@lisp
(cmacs-podomation-start)
@end lisp

Or with full config (starts engine, installs hook bridge, loads pod
files):

@lisp
(cmacs-podomation-start-with-config)
@end lisp

@node Podomation DSL
@section DSL Reference

The podomation DSL connects event sources to handlers.

@subsection Pod Declaration

@example
pod name = module_name->new(arg1, arg2);
@end example

@subsection Event Binding

@example
name->event_name => handler->function(args);
@end example

@subsection Result Capture

@example
res result := name->event => handler->function(args);
res persist result := name->event => handler->function(args);
@end example

@code{persist} results survive across event dispatches.

@subsection Pipe Chain

@example
name->event => handler1->func(args) | handler2->func("@{pipe->field@}");
@end example

Each handler's output becomes the next handler's @code{pipe} scope.

@subsection Where Guard

@example
name->event where result->field == "value" => handler->func(args);
@end example

Comparison operators: @code{==}, @code{!=}, @code{<}, @code{>},
@code{<=}, @code{>=}, @code{=~} (regex), @code{!~} (negated regex).

@subsection Conditional

@example
if result->field == "value" @{
    name->event => handler->func(args);
@} else @{
    name->event => handler->other(args);
@}
@end example

@subsection Retry with Circuit Breaker

@example
name->event => retry(3, backoff: "exponential") @{
    res r := handler->func(args)
        circuit_breaker(threshold: 5, reset: "1m");
@} on_failure => log->write_error("failed: @{error@}");
@end example

@subsection Parallel Block

@example
name->event => parallel @{
    res a := handler1->func(args);
    res b := handler2->func(args);
@};
@end example

@subsection Interpolation

@multitable @columnfractions .45 .55
@headitem Syntax @tab Meaning
@item @code{@{result->field@}} @tab Property access on a result
@item @code{@{result->field[key]@}} @tab Nested dict key lookup
@item @code{@{pipe->field@}} @tab Current pipe stage value
@item @code{@{pipe[N]->field@}} @tab Nth pipe stage (0-based)
@item @code{$@{ENV_VAR@}} @tab Environment variable
@item @code{$(command)} @tab Shell substitution (stdout)
@item @code{@{field|regex("pat","rep")@}} @tab Inline regex substitution
@end multitable

@subsection Regex Operators

@code{=~} matches against PCRE patterns.  After a match, capture groups
populate @code{rematch->} scope:

@example
source->on_event
    where event->message =~ "(?P<action>Accepted|Failed) .* for (?P<user>\\w+)"
    => log->write_info("@{rematch->action@} for @{rematch->user@}");
@end example

@multitable @columnfractions .35 .65
@headitem Scope @tab Meaning
@item @code{rematch->0} @tab Full match
@item @code{rematch->1} @tab First capture group
@item @code{rematch->name} @tab Named group
@item @code{rematch->count} @tab Number of groups
@item @code{rematch->matched} @tab @code{"true"} or @code{"false"}
@end multitable

@subsection Event Name Patterns

@example
name->on_*               glob: matches on_tick, on_message, ...
name->on_t?ck            glob: single wildcard char
name->/on_(get|post)/    regex: enclosed in /.../ delimiters
@end example

@subsection Operators Summary

@multitable @columnfractions .1 .35 .55
@headitem Op @tab Use @tab Example
@item @code{->} @tab Member access / call
  @tab @code{timer->new(1000)}
@item @code{=>} @tab Event binding
  @tab @code{t->on_tick => log->write(...)}
@item @code{=} @tab Pod declaration only
  @tab @code{pod t = timer->new(...)}
@item @code{:=} @tab Result capture
  @tab @code{res r := source->event => ...}
@item @code{|} @tab Pipe to next handler
  @tab @code{h1->f() | h2->g(...)}
@item @code{==} @tab String equality guard
  @tab @code{where r->status == "200"}
@item @code{!=} @tab Inequality
  @tab @code{where r->code != "0"}
@item @code{=~} @tab Regex match (PCRE)
  @tab @code{where e->path =~ "^/api"}
@item @code{!~} @tab Negated regex match
  @tab @code{where e->ua !~ "bot"}
@end multitable

Evaluate DSL text from Elisp:

@lisp
(cmacs-podomation-eval-dsl
 "pod t = timer->new(5000); t->on_tick => print->string(\"tick\");")
@end lisp

Load a @file{.pod} file:

@lisp
(cmacs-podomation-load "~/.config/cmacs/auto.pod")
@end lisp

@node Podomation REPL
@section REPL

Open an interactive REPL buffer with @code{M-x podomation-repl}.
The REPL uses @code{comint-mode} with tab completion.

From the bacon shell, use @code{pod repl} to enter an interactive
session.

Built-in REPL commands start with @code{:}:

@table @code
@item :modules
List loaded modules.
@item :pods
List active pods.
@item :vars
List result variables.
@item :undef <name>
Remove a pod (stops it) or variable.
@item :load <file>
Load and execute a @file{.pod} file.
@item :reset
Reset engine state.
@item :describe <module>
Full module help text.
@item :help
Show help.
@item :quit
Exit the REPL.
@end table

DSL text entered at the prompt is parsed and executed immediately.
Multi-line input is supported (the prompt changes to @code{...>}).
Tab completion works for module names, event names, handler names,
and REPL commands.

@node Podomation Hook Bridge
@section Hook Bridge

The Elisp layer bridges Emacs hooks to podomation events in
@code{snake_case}.  Hooks are installed by
@code{cmacs-podomation--install-hooks} (called automatically by
@code{cmacs-podomation-start-with-config}).

@subsection Default Hook Mappings

@multitable @columnfractions .35 .3 .35
@headitem Emacs Hook @tab Podomation Event @tab Event Data
@item @code{after-save-hook}
  @tab @code{on_buffer_save}
  @tab @code{buffer_name}, @code{file_name}, @code{major_mode}
@item @code{kill-buffer-hook}
  @tab @code{on_buffer_kill}
  @tab @code{buffer_name}
@item @code{after-init-hook}
  @tab @code{on_after_init}
  @tab (none)
@item @code{post-command-hook}
  @tab @code{on_post_command}
  @tab @code{command_name}
@end multitable

@subsection Dynamic Hook Registration

Register any Emacs hook as a podomation event source:

@lisp
(cmacs-podomation-hook "compilation-finish-functions")
@end lisp

This fires an @code{on_hook} event with @code{hook_name} data
whenever the named hook runs.  Use from DSL with a @code{where}
guard:

@example
pod c = cmacs->new();
c->on_hook
    where event->hook_name == "compilation-finish-functions"
    => gotify->send("Compilation finished");
@end example

