@node Print to cmacs
@chapter Print to cmacs

@cindex print to cmacs
@cindex cups
@cindex virtual printer
@cindex pdf import

@quotation
This chapter mirrors @file{doc_org/cmacs/print.org}; that file is the
primary interactive reference.
@end quotation

@section Overview

``Print to cmacs'' is a CUPS virtual printer that delivers print jobs
into a running cmacs instance as multi-page annotatable org documents.
Modelled after Microsoft OneNote's ``Print to OneNote'' --- print from
any application, get an annotation canvas in your editor.

A small CUPS backend (@file{cmacs/print/cmacs-print}) staged at
@file{/usr/lib/cups/backend/cmacs-print} (root:root, mode 0700, as
CUPS requires) accepts print jobs over stdin and atomically writes
them to @file{/tmp/cmacs-print-<uid>/<TS>-job<ID>-<title>.pdf}.
Cmacs (when @file{cmacs-print.el} is loaded) @code{file-notify}
-watches that directory and runs @code{cmacs-print-import-pdf} in its
own @code{unconfined_t} security context.

This pull-from-spool architecture deliberately avoids three SELinux
dead ends in stock Fedora policy that all silently deny without an
AVC entry (suppressed by @code{dontaudit} rules):

@itemize @bullet
@item
@code{cupsd_t} writing to @code{user_tmp_t} (= @file{/run/user/$UID/}).
@item
@code{cupsd_t} writing to @code{user_home_t} (the user's home dir).
@item
@code{cupsd_t} running @command{su} / @command{runuser} / @command{gdbus}
to talk to the user's session.
@end itemize

@file{/tmp/} has the @code{tmp_t} label, which @code{cupsd_t} can
manage freely.

A periodic @code{cmacs-print-poll-interval} drain (default 30 s) is the
belt-and-suspenders fallback for daemon-without-frame setups where
@code{file-notify} events can lag.  If cmacs is not running when a
print arrives, the PDF sits in @file{/tmp/cmacs-print-<uid>/} until
cmacs starts; the load-time drain picks it up then.

@code{cmacs-print-import-pdf} rasterises the PDF into per-page PNGs
(@command{pdftocairo -png -r 150}) and writes a generated
@file{index.org} with one @samp{* Page N} heading per page, each
followed by an inline @code{[[file:...]]} image link.

Annotation rides on the existing cmacs-ink subsystem (see
@ref{Org-Ex Interactive Widgets}) plus the user's Doom annotation
plugins:

@table @asis
@item @code{cmacs-ink-region}
Pixel-anchored overlay strokes that float on top of the rendered page
image as a transparent layer.  Primary ``draw on the page'' path.
@item @code{org-remark}
Persistent text-anchored highlights and notes.
@item @code{org-noter}
Side-by-side notes paired against the original PDF.
@item @code{pdf-tools}
Used to view the original PDF directly.
@item @code{org-roam}
Links the imported doc into the user's note graph.
@end table

@section Installation

There are two install modes.  Pick based on whether @file{/usr} is
writable on your system.

@subsection System mode (mutable Linux)

Stages a CUPS backend at @file{/usr/lib/cups/backend/cmacs-print}
(root:root, mode 0700, as CUPS requires) and registers a CUPS queue
named @samp{cmacs}.

@example
make install-cmacs-printer    # prompts for sudo
@end example

On immutable / OSTree systems (Fedora Silverblue, Atomic, Bluefin,
Bazzite, Universal Blue, NixOS), the installer detects
@file{/run/ostree-booted} or a non-writable @file{/usr} and redirects
you to the per-user install with a single message rather than
failing.  Override with @code{CMACS_PRINT_FORCE_SYSTEM=1} after
@command{sudo bootc usroverlay} or for layered RPM builds.

@subsection Per-user mode (immutable systems / no-sudo)

Runs @command{ippeveprinter} (ships with CUPS) as a per-user systemd
service.  The printer advertises via mDNS; @command{cups-browsed}
discovers it and surfaces @samp{cmacs} in every app's print dialog.
No @file{/usr} writes, no sudo:

@example
make install-cmacs-printer-user
@end example

The installer drops:

@itemize @bullet
@item
@file{~/.local/libexec/cmacs/cmacs-print-handler} --- per-job handler.
@item
@file{~/.config/systemd/user/cmacs-print.service} --- service unit
with absolute paths substituted in.
@end itemize

Then @code{systemctl --user enable --now cmacs-print.service}.

If @command{cups-browsed} is inactive on your system, the installer
prints the one-time @code{sudo systemctl enable --now cups-browsed}
needed to make discovered printers visible to applications.  That is
the @emph{only} sudo step on any OSTree variant.

@subsection Verify and remove

@example
lpstat -p cmacs               # should report "idle"
make check-cmacs-printer      # reports both modes + immutability

# remove:
make uninstall-cmacs-printer        # system mode
make uninstall-cmacs-printer-user   # per-user mode
@end example

@section Configuration

All defcustoms live in @file{lisp/cmacs/cmacs-print.el}:

@table @code
@item cmacs-print-target-dir
Output directory.  Default
@file{~/Documents/notes/03_resources/cmacs-print}.
@item cmacs-print-output-formats
Which artefacts to produce per print job.  A list containing any of
@code{org} (rasterise the PDF and generate an annotatable org doc with
inline page images) and @code{pdf} (keep a full copy of the PDF).
Default @code{(org pdf)}.
@item cmacs-print-dpi
@command{pdftocairo -r} value.  Default @code{150}.
@item cmacs-print-max-pages
Confirmation-prompt threshold for very large PDFs.  Default @code{200}.
@item cmacs-print-auto-open
If non-nil, open the imported document on success.
@item cmacs-print-rasterizer-program
External rasteriser.  Default @code{"pdftocairo"}.
@item cmacs-print-pdfinfo-program
External metadata reader.  Default @code{"pdfinfo"}.
@item cmacs-print-debug
Log every step to @samp{*cmacs-print-log*}.  Default @code{nil}.
@end table

Output is one self-contained directory per print job under
@code{cmacs-print-target-dir}:

@example
20260502-142300-receipt/
  index.org              # if 'org in formats
  source.pdf             # if 'pdf in formats
  page-001.png           # if 'org (one per page)
  page-002.png
20260502-150412-other-doc/
  ...
@end example

The org file references its siblings by bare basename
(@code{page-001.png}, @code{source.pdf}) so the whole directory is
self-contained --- copy or move it anywhere and the links keep working.

@section Usage

Pick @samp{cmacs} as the printer in any application's print dialog,
or invoke programmatically:

@example
M-x cmacs-print-import-pdf-interactive
@end example

@noindent
or from the shell:

@example
gdbus call --session \
  --dest=org.cmacs.Editor1 \
  --object-path=/org/cmacs/Editor \
  --method=org.cmacs.Editor1.Eval \
  '(cmacs-print-import-pdf "/tmp/test.pdf" "test")'
@end example

@noindent
or to drain prints that arrived while cmacs was down:

@example
M-x cmacs-print-reprocess-inbox
@end example

@section File Reference

@table @file
@item cmacs/print/cmacs-print
System CUPS backend (POSIX shell, runs as root).
@item cmacs/print/cmacs-print.ppd
PPD declaring @code{application/pdf} passthrough.
@item cmacs/print/install-cmacs-printer.sh
System installer.  Auto-redirects to user-mode on immutable systems.
@item cmacs/print/uninstall-cmacs-printer.sh
System uninstaller.
@item cmacs/print/cmacs-print-handler
Per-user IPP handler (POSIX shell).
@item cmacs/print/cmacs-print.service.in
systemd user-unit template.
@item cmacs/print/install-cmacs-printer-user.sh
Per-user installer.  No sudo, no @file{/usr} writes.
@item cmacs/print/uninstall-cmacs-printer-user.sh
Per-user uninstaller.
@item lisp/cmacs/cmacs-print.el
Elisp module: @code{cmacs-print-import-pdf} entry point + helpers.
@item test/cmacs/cmacs-print-tests.el
ERT tests for sanitisation, PNG dimension parsing, org generation,
page renaming.
@end table

@c ===================================================================
@node CMacs Video
@chapter CMacs Video: Inline GStreamer Playback

@cindex video
@cindex GStreamer
@cindex RTSP
@cindex RTSPS
@cindex unifi
@cindex security camera

The @code{cmacs-video} subsystem embeds GStreamer-decoded video
inline in Emacs buffers via a Cairo post-glyph paint hook.  It
supports local files, HTTP(S), RTSP, and RTSPS via
@code{playbin3}, and is the foundation for third-party packages
such as a Unifi Protect camera viewer.

@strong{Compositor independence.}  cmacs-video paints inside
@code{pgtk_handle_draw}, which fires whenever cmacs is rendering
through GTK --- regardless of which outer compositor hosts the
client.  This subsystem works under mutter, kwin, sway, hyprland,
weston, gowl, and X11+pgtk alike.  It does @strong{not} require
@code{--with-cmacs-gowl} nor running cmacs in @code{--gowl}
compositor mode.

@section Requirements

System packages:

@table @asis
@item Fedora
@code{gstreamer1-devel gstreamer1-plugins-base-devel
gstreamer1-plugins-good gstreamer1-plugins-bad-free
gstreamer1-plugins-bad-free-extras gstreamer1-plugins-ugly-free
gstreamer1-libav}
@item Debian / Ubuntu
@code{libgstreamer1.0-dev libgstreamer-plugins-base1.0-dev
gstreamer1.0-plugins-@{good,bad,ugly@} gstreamer1.0-libav}
@item Arch
@code{gstreamer gst-plugins-@{base,good,bad,ugly@} gst-libav}
@item macOS (Homebrew) / FreeBSD
See @code{install-deps} for the exact package names.
@end table

The @code{plugins-bad} family is non-negotiable: @code{rtspsrc}
(RTSP and RTSPS) lives there.  @code{gst-libav} provides
software H.264 / H.265 fallback for cameras whose codecs don't
match a hardware decoder.

@strong{Fedora caveat.}  Fedora's @code{gstreamer1-libav} is
built against @code{ffmpeg-free}, which strips patent-encumbered
codecs --- including H.264 Main and High profile (the encoding
nearly all IP cameras use).  Without RPM Fusion's full
@code{ffmpeg} swap, streams will reach @code{paused} but never
produce frames.  See @file{doc_org/cmacs/cmacs-video.org} for
the swap recipe and the SRTP / @code{?enableSrtp} workaround for
Unifi Protect.

Build with @code{--with-cmacs-video}.  Configure also requires
@code{--with-cmacs-glib} (bus dispatch piggybacks on the cmacs
GMainContext); without @code{--with-pgtk} the subsystem still
builds but cannot display frames (decode side works for
snapshot / transcode use cases).

@section Block syntax (@code{#+BEGIN_VIDEO})

@example
#+BEGIN_VIDEO :src "rtsps://nvr.lan:7441/abc?enableSrtp" :width 640 :height 360 :insecure t :latency 200
Front-door camera                ; body is reserved for caption / notes
#+END_VIDEO
@end example

@table @code
@item :src
URI.  Required.  @code{file://}, @code{http(s)://}, @code{rtsp://},
@code{rtsps://}, @code{srt://}, @code{udp://}, @code{rtp://}.
@item :width @var{W}
Display width in pixels (default @code{cmacs-video-default-width}).
@item :height @var{H}
Display height in pixels (default @code{cmacs-video-default-height}).
@item :autoplay
Default @code{t}.
@item :loop
Restart on EOS (file URIs only).  Default @code{nil}.
@item :start
Initial seek in seconds.
@item :audio
Default @code{nil} (muted, no audio sink).
@item :volume
@code{0.0..1.0}, default @code{1.0}.
@item :insecure
RTSPS only: skip TLS validation.  See @code{cmacs-video-rtsps-insecure-by-default}.
@item :latency
RTSP jitterbuffer in milliseconds.  Default
@code{cmacs-video-default-latency-ms} (200, appropriate for LAN).
@end table

Render every block in the current buffer with
@code{M-x cmacs-video-org-render-buffer}.  Tear down with
@code{M-x cmacs-video-org-unrender-buffer}.

@section Standalone @code{cmacs-video-mode}

@itemize @bullet
@item @code{M-x cmacs-video-open-file @key{RET} PATH @key{RET}}
@item @code{M-x cmacs-video-open-url  @key{RET} URI  @key{RET}}
@end itemize

Both pop a new buffer in @code{cmacs-video-mode} with the video
filling the window.  Keybindings:

@table @kbd
@item SPC
Toggle play / pause.
@item q
Kill the buffer (also stops the stream).
@item s
Prompt for a seek position in seconds.
@item f / b
Step one frame forward / backward.
@item , / .
Seek -5 / +5 seconds.
@item < / >
Seek -30 / +30 seconds.
@item 0
Seek to start.
@item m
Toggle mute.
@item + / -
Volume up / down by 0.05.
@item p
Save a snapshot to a PNG file.
@item i
Echo state, position, frame size in the minibuffer.
@end table

@section Lisp API

@deffn Function cmacs-video-supported-p
Return non-@code{nil} if @code{cmacs-video} was built in and
GStreamer initialised successfully.
@end deffn

@deffn Function cmacs-video-open uri &rest plist
Open @var{uri} and return an opaque integer handle.  See the
docstring of the underlying primitive @code{cmacs-video--open-1}
for the full keyword option set.  This wrapper layers on
@code{cmacs-video-rtsps-insecure-by-default} and
@code{cmacs-video-default-latency-ms}.
@end deffn

@deffn Function cmacs-video-close handle
Stop and destroy stream @var{handle}.  Blocks ~100--500 ms for
RTSP/RTSPS streams (TEARDOWN handshake) because the pipeline's
state transition to @code{NULL} must complete before streaming
threads unwind.  Idempotent.
@end deffn

@deffn Function cmacs-video-play handle
@deffnx Function cmacs-video-pause handle
@deffnx Function cmacs-video-stop handle
Set state to @code{PLAYING} / @code{PAUSED} / @code{READY}.
@end deffn

@deffn Function cmacs-video-seek handle position-seconds
@deffnx Function cmacs-video-step handle n
Seek to @var{position-seconds} or step by @var{n} frames
(negative goes backwards via approximate keyframe seek).
@end deffn

@deffn Function cmacs-video-state handle
Return one of: @code{initializing}, @code{buffering},
@code{playing}, @code{paused}, @code{stalled},
@code{reconnecting}, @code{eos}, @code{error}, @code{closed}.
@end deffn

@deffn Function cmacs-video-position handle
Return @code{(POS-NS . DUR-NS)} or @code{nil}.  For live streams
@code{DUR-NS} is @code{nil}.
@end deffn

@deffn Function cmacs-video-frames-decoded handle
@deffnx Function cmacs-video-frame-size handle
Frame counter and native @code{(W . H)} dimensions.
@end deffn

@deffn Function cmacs-video-set-volume handle volume
@deffnx Function cmacs-video-set-mute handle mute
Audio knobs.
@end deffn

@deffn Function cmacs-video-attach-buffer handle marker
@deffnx Function cmacs-video-attach-frame handle frame x y w h
@deffnx Function cmacs-video-detach handle
Anchor stream @var{handle} either to a buffer marker (inline
overlay) or to a frame's pixel rectangle (standalone mode).
@end deffn

@deffn Function cmacs-video-snapshot-to-file handle path
Write the current frame to @var{path} as PNG.
@end deffn

@deffn Function cmacs-video-add-state-handler handle fn
@deffnx Function cmacs-video-remove-state-handler handle fn
Register an observer called as @code{(@var{fn} @var{handle}
@var{state-sym} @var{&optional detail})} on every state change.
Always invoked on the main Emacs thread via @code{safe-calln} ---
errors are logged to @code{*Messages*} without unwinding the
stream.
@end deffn

@deffn Function cmacs-video-list
Return a list of all live stream handles.
@end deffn

@deffn Function cmacs-video-promote-hardware-decoders
Bump priority of VAAPI / V4L2 / NVDEC H.264 + H.265 decoders so
@code{playbin3} prefers them over @code{libav}.  Saves 8--15% CPU
per 720p stream on capable hardware; may expose flaky vendor
drivers (black frames).  Off by default; opt in by setting the
defcustom of the same name.
@end deffn

@section RTSPS guide

The TLS validation flags follow @code{GTlsCertificateFlags}:

@table @code
@item :insecure t
Accept any certificate.  Sets @code{tls-validation-flags=0}.
Use only during initial setup of self-signed NVRs; pin the cert
afterwards.
@item default
Accept @code{UNKNOWN_CA}, @code{BAD_IDENTITY}, @code{INSECURE}.
Strict on @code{EXPIRED} / @code{REVOKED}.
@end table

Unifi Protect feeds look like
@code{rtsps://NVR-IP:7441/<random-key>?enableSrtp}.  The
@code{enableSrtp} query parameter triggers SDP-driven SRTP key
exchange.  Set @code{:insecure t} for first connection, then
extract the cert with @code{openssl s_client -connect
NVR:7441 -showcerts} and switch to a pinned cert via
@code{tls-database}.

The stall watchdog (default 5 s threshold + 3 s grace) is
mandatory for live streams: older @code{gst-plugins-good}
(<1.22) reports only @code{WARNING} on UDP packet loss while
the pipeline stays in @code{PLAYING} indefinitely.  Without the
watchdog the symptom is a frozen frame; with it, the stream
transitions @code{stalled} → @code{reconnecting} and recovers
via exponential backoff.

@section Building a third-party viewer

The Lisp API is designed for third-party packages to wrap.  A
minimal @code{unifi-cam.el} sketch:

@example
(defun unifi/open (name uri)
  (let ((buf (get-buffer-create (format "*cam: %s*" name))))
    (with-current-buffer buf
      (cmacs-video-mode)
      (let ((h (cmacs-video-open uri :width 640 :height 360
                                 :on-state #'unifi/state-cb)))
        (setq cmacs-video--standalone-handle h)))
    (display-buffer buf)))
@end example

See @file{doc_org/cmacs/cmacs-video.org} for an extended recipe
covering certificate pinning, multi-camera grid layout, snapshot
thumbnails, and reconnect behaviour.

@section Troubleshooting

@table @asis
@item @strong{Black frame forever}
Missing @code{plugins-bad}.  Install it; restart cmacs.
@item @strong{``Internal data stream error''}
Codec missing.  Install @code{libav} or the relevant decoder
plugin and retry.
@item @strong{TLS handshake failure}
For self-signed NVRs, set @code{:insecure t} first.  Pin the
certificate afterwards via @code{tls-database}.
@item @strong{No audio with @code{:audio t}}
@code{autoaudiosink} silently falls back to @code{fakesink} when
no session bus is reachable.  Verify @code{XDG_RUNTIME_DIR} /
@code{PULSE_SERVER} are set.
@item @strong{State stuck in @code{reconnecting}}
Network reachable but the camera or NVR is denying the session.
Check the camera's RTSP path and credentials.
@end table

