@node CMacs Piper
@chapter CMacs Piper: Offline Text-to-Speech

@cindex cmacs-piper
@cindex piper
@cindex text to speech
@cindex TTS

@quotation
This chapter mirrors @file{doc_org/cmacs/cmacs-piper.org}; that file
is the canonical interactive reference.
@end quotation

@section Overview

cmacs-piper invokes Piper as a subprocess via @code{g_subprocess_new}:

@example
piper --model VOICE.onnx --output_raw < text > pcm
@end example

The S16LE PCM stream is fed back into
@code{cmacs-audio--playback-open-pcm-1} for live playback, or written
to a WAV via @code{with-temp-file} for archival.  Spawning uses
@code{g_subprocess_communicate_async} so polling happens on the cmacs
GMainContext — no extra thread.

@section Installing Piper

cmacs uses the @uref{https://github.com/OHF-Voice/piper1-GPL,
OHF-Voice piper1-GPL fork} — the active community fork after the
original @code{rhasspy/piper} went quiet.  It ships as a Python
package on PyPI that installs a @code{piper} console-script:

@example
/usr/bin/python3 -m pip install --user piper-tts       # preferred
# or
pipx install --python /usr/bin/python3 piper-tts       # see below
@end example

Prefer plain @code{pip --user} over @code{pipx}: piper-tts's
scikit-build-core wheel triggers a pipx metadata-readback bug when
pipx uses @code{uv} as its backend, surfacing as
@code{Pipx Internal Error: cannot find package 'piper-tts' metadata.}
even though the wheel installed cleanly.  Plain @code{pip --user}
sidesteps the whole pipx/uv layer.

Runtime dep: @code{espeak-ng} (phonemization backend; pulled in by
@code{install-deps}).

The @code{deps/piper/} submodule is kept for reference + test
fixtures but is NOT built from source — the CMake file in that tree
builds a Python C extension, not the standalone @code{piper} CLI.

configure probes for @code{piper} on PATH at configure time
(@code{AC_PATH_PROG}).  If absent, configure still succeeds and the
macro @code{CMACS_PIPER_DEFAULT_BINARY} falls back to literal
@code{"piper"} — the binary is resolved at runtime via
@code{g_find_program_in_path}, so installing Piper post-build works
without reconfigure.

@section Voice Search Path

Voice files are resolved through @code{cmacs-piper-voices-search-path},
which by default looks in:

@enumerate
@item
@file{~/.local/share/cmacs/piper-voices/} — user dir.
@item
@file{/usr/share/cmacs/piper-voices/} — system dir populated by
container image builds.  The default English voice
(@code{en_US-amy-low.onnx} + @code{.json} sidecar, ~64 MB) is
bundled here by @code{./build-container}.
@end enumerate

User-installed voices take precedence.  Each voice's
@code{.onnx.json} sidecar carries the native sample rate;
cmacs-piper reads it to size the playback stream.

@subsection Overriding the bundled voice

@example
PIPER_VOICE_NAME=en_GB-alba-medium.onnx \
PIPER_VOICE_DIR=en/en_GB/alba/medium \
  ./build-container
@end example

More voices at @uref{https://huggingface.co/rhasspy/piper-voices/tree/main,
rhasspy/piper-voices}.

@section Quick Start

@example
;; Speak a literal string
(cmacs-piper-speak "Hello, cmacs.")

;; Speak the active region (or sentence-at-point dwim)
M-x cmacs-piper-speak-region        ;; C-c v s
M-x cmacs-piper-stop                ;; C-c v S
@end example

@section PGTK Right-Click Context Menu

@code{lisp/cmacs/cmacs-piper-context-menu.el} adds an entry to
@code{context-menu-functions} (the standard Emacs 28+ hook used by
PGTK's @kbd{mouse-3}) that shows:

@itemize
@item @samp{Speak selection (Piper)} when a region is active
@item @samp{Speak sentence (Piper)} when there's no region
@item @samp{Stop speaking} while playback is live
@end itemize

Auto-enables @code{context-menu-mode} unless
@code{cmacs-piper-auto-enable-context-menu} is set to nil.

@section Defcustoms

@table @code
@item cmacs-piper-voices-directory
User-writable voices dir (default
@file{~/.local/share/cmacs/piper-voices/}).

@item cmacs-piper-system-voices-directory
System voices dir populated by image builds (default
@file{/usr/share/cmacs/piper-voices/}).

@item cmacs-piper-voices-search-path
Ordered list of directories searched for voice files; default
@code{(cmacs-piper-voices-directory cmacs-piper-system-voices-directory)}.

@item cmacs-piper-default-voice
Default voice filename (e.g. @code{en_US-amy-low.onnx}).

@item cmacs-piper-default-rate
Fallback sample rate if the voice's @code{.onnx.json} sidecar is
absent (default 22050 Hz).

@item cmacs-piper-auto-enable-context-menu
Auto-enable @code{context-menu-mode} when cmacs-piper loads (t).
@end table

@section Troubleshooting

@subsection Piper not found

@code{(cmacs-piper-supported-p)} returns @code{nil}; speak commands
raise @code{user-error}.  Install piper-tts (see @ref{CMacs Piper})
and re-run; cmacs resolves the binary at runtime via
@code{g_find_program_in_path}, so no rebuild is needed.

@subsection @code{pipx install piper-tts} fails: "cannot find package metadata"

Symptom:

@example
Pipx Internal Error: cannot find package 'piper-tts' metadata.
@end example

This appears when @code{pipx} has been built (or auto-detected) to
use @code{uv} as its backend.  piper-tts ships a scikit-build-core
wheel that compiles a C extension wrapping piper-phonemize; uv's
pip-install of that wheel finishes the install but doesn't
populate the @code{PKG-INFO} / @code{METADATA} files in the layout
pipx scans afterwards.  pipx then deletes the venv and raises this
error even though the wheel was fine.

The smoking gun in @code{pipx --verbose} output is:

@example
Backend resolved to uv (source: auto-path)
@end example

right before @code{installing piper-tts...}.

@strong{Fix:} skip pipx and use plain pip:

@example
/usr/bin/python3 -m pip install --user piper-tts
@end example

CPython's own pip runs the wheel install synchronously and
populates metadata in the expected places.  The @code{piper}
console-script lands at @file{~/.local/bin/piper} — make sure
that's on @code{PATH}.

@strong{Why @file{/usr/bin/python3} explicitly?}  On systems where
Homebrew / conda / asdf has put a non-distro Python first on
@code{PATH}, pipx may have cached a venv path that points into a
stale brew prefix (e.g.
@file{/home/linuxbrew/.linuxbrew/opt/python@@3.14/libexec/bin/python}).
After a brew upgrade reshuffles that path, every subsequent
@code{pipx install} dies with @code{No interpreter found at path}
before even reaching the backend.  Using the system interpreter is
more stable across brew updates.

@subsection @code{No interpreter found at path}

@example
× No interpreter found at path
│ `/home/linuxbrew/.linuxbrew/opt/python@@3.14/libexec/bin/python`
@end example

pipx remembers the Python it was first set up with, baked into its
internal venvs metadata.  When Homebrew updates @code{python@@3.14}
the @file{libexec/bin/python} symlink moves and every @code{pipx}
invocation fails before doing any actual work.

Fixes, in order of preference:

@enumerate
@item
Bypass pipx entirely: @*
@code{/usr/bin/python3 -m pip install --user piper-tts}.
@item
Reset pipx to the system Python: @*
@code{PIPX_DEFAULT_PYTHON=/usr/bin/python3 pipx reinstall-all}.
@item
Nuke pipx state and start over: @*
@code{rm -rf ~/.local/share/pipx ~/.local/pipx && pipx ensurepath}.
@end enumerate

@subsection Choppy or wrong-pitch playback

Sample-rate mismatch.  Confirm the voice's sidecar:

@example
cat ~/.local/share/cmacs/piper-voices/en_US-amy-low.onnx.json | jq .audio.sample_rate
@end example

If absent, set @code{cmacs-piper-default-rate} to the right value.

