@node CMacs Libregnum
@chapter CMacs Libregnum: Embedded 3D Scene Buffers

@cindex libregnum
@cindex 3D scenes
@cindex raylib

@quotation
This chapter mirrors @file{doc_org/cmacs/cmacs-libregnum.org}.
@end quotation

@code{cmacs-libregnum} embeds the libregnum game engine (a
GObject wrapper over raylib via graylib) as a first-class cmacs
subsystem.  Each @code{cmacs-libregnum-mode} buffer owns a 3D
scene whose framebuffer is blitted across the whole window area
on every redisplay.  The buffer's text is a YAML-ish description
of the scene; saving the buffer snapshots the camera state into
the text and reopening the file rebuilds the scene.

Besides the built-in scene visualisers, a buffer can also host a
full libregnum @emph{game} loaded as a module; see @emph{Game module
hosting} below.

@section Architecture

cmacs does not drive libregnum through a native @code{GtkGLArea}
child widget.  raylib's @code{CORE.Window} globals make external
GL contexts infeasible.  Instead, cmacs acquires a single
process-shared @emph{hidden} raylib window
(@code{FLAG_WINDOW_HIDDEN} set before @code{InitWindow}); each
view owns a @code{RenderTexture2D} (FBO) plus a double-buffered
Cairo @code{ARGB32} surface.  A redraw tick draws the scene to
the FBO and reads it back with a single @code{glReadPixels} in
@code{GL_BGRA} straight into the back surface (which matches
Cairo's byte order, so there is no CPU channel-swap loop and no
per-frame allocation; the bottom-up GL origin is flipped for free
by the paint hook's Cairo matrix), then swaps front/back.  The
view then invalidates the GTK widget of each frame showing the
buffer with @code{gtk_widget_queue_draw}, which re-runs
@code{pgtk_handle_draw} to blit the front surface.  The pattern
matches @code{cmacs-video}'s GStreamer overlay path.

@section Rendering pipeline and performance

Three choices make scene interaction (orbit/zoom) real-time:

@enumerate
@item
@emph{No hidden-window presentation.}  The render path does not
call @code{lrg_window_begin_frame}/@code{lrg_window_end_frame}.
Those wrap raylib's @code{BeginDrawing}/@code{EndDrawing}, and
@code{EndDrawing} presents and @emph{paces} the hidden window:
@code{glfwSwapBuffers} (vsync block), @code{WaitTime} (the
@code{SetTargetFPS(60)} cap, up to ~16@dmn{ms} of sleep per
call), and @code{glfwPollEvents}.  For an offscreen FBO that is
pure latency.  Offscreen rendering needs only
@code{BeginTextureMode}/@code{EndTextureMode} plus a current GL
context.

@item
@emph{Targeted overlay refresh, not full redisplay.}
@code{notify_frame_ready} calls @code{gtk_widget_queue_draw} on
the frames showing the buffer rather than @code{force-window-update}.
The latter triggers a full @code{redisplay_internal} every frame,
profiled at ~35@dmn{ms}/frame (capping ~20 FPS even though the
render was only ~10--15@dmn{ms}).  The targeted invalidate re-runs
just @code{pgtk_handle_draw}, restoring 40--60 FPS.  It is correct
because the buffer text is static and fully hidden behind the
blit, so Emacs's backing surface is already up to date.

@item
@emph{Double buffering.}  The renderer fills the back surface and
swaps under a mutex held only for the pointer swap, so the paint
hook never blocks on an in-flight render.
@end enumerate

Static scenes render on demand.  For animated content, switch a
view into animation mode with @code{cmacs-libregnum-toggle-animation}
(or the @code{cmacs-libregnum-set-animated} primitive); a shared
timer then re-renders at @code{cmacs-libregnum-target-fps}
(default 60) while the buffer stays on-screen, and stops when it
is hidden (tracked via a paint-stamp generation counter).

Rendering stays on the main thread.  An off-thread GL worker
(GLFW context handoff plus double-buffered PBO async readback) was
implemented and reverted: the worker-to-main per-frame handoff and
GL-driver cross-thread contention regressed frame rate, because
after the FPS-cap removal the render was never the main-thread
blocker.  The experiment is parked on branch
@code{libregnum-offthread-wip}.

@section Translation-unit firewall

raylib declares @code{Color} as a struct; cmacs's
@file{pgtkgui.h} declares @code{Color} as @code{void *}.  These
conflict.  Only @file{cmacs-libregnum-render.c} pulls in raylib;
all other translation units talk to it through the plain-C
@file{cmacs-libregnum-render.h} API.

@section Interactive commands

@deffn Command cmacs-libregnum-demo
Open an empty scene buffer.  Useful as a smoke test for the
render pipeline.
@end deffn

@deffn Command cmacs-libregnum-project-tree &optional root
Open a @emph{navigable} 3D directory tree under @var{root}
(defaults to @code{project-current} root, then
@code{default-directory}).  Directories are spheres, files are cubes
(height = log file size, hue = extension), laid out as a tidy
hanging tree (depth on @math{Y}, siblings spread on @math{X} by
subtree size) joined to their parent by edge lines.  Directory and
selected-node names are labelled in the scene.  See the
@emph{Navigating the project tree} section below for click and
keyboard interaction.
@end deffn

@deffn Command cmacs-libregnum-gobject-graph &optional namespace
Open a libregnum scene visualising the live GObject class
hierarchy.  With a prefix arg, prompts for a leading type-name
prefix (e.g. @code{Gtk}, @code{Lrg}) to scope the graph.  Depth
maps to @math{Y}, siblings fan out on @math{X}; hue derives from
the first uppercase prefix in the type name.
@end deffn

@deffn Command cmacs-libregnum-mind-map org-file
Parse @var{org-file}'s heading tree and render it as a 3D radial
mind map.  Each heading is a node, parent/child relationships
are @code{LrgLine3D} edges, layout is deterministic.
@end deffn

@deffn Command cmacs-libregnum-toggle-animation
Toggle continuous animation for the current view.  When on, a
shared timer re-renders the view at @code{cmacs-libregnum-target-fps}
(default 60) while its buffer is on-screen; when off, the view
renders only on demand.  The underlying primitive is
@code{cmacs-libregnum-set-animated} (@var{buffer} @var{flag}
&optional @var{target-fps}).
@end deffn

@section Navigating the project tree

The project-tree scene is interactive.  Every node is recorded in a
node table that drives picking, in-scene labels, and a keyboard
navigation model (exposed to Lisp via
@code{cmacs-libregnum-tree-nodes}).

Mouse: left-click a file to open it (@code{find-file}); left-click a
directory to drill in (the scene re-roots there).  Left-drag still
orbits, right-drag pans, scroll zooms; a click is told from a drag by
whether the pointer moved.

@subsubheading Click dispatch and the loading invariant

Viewport clicks are handled in C: the click ray-picks a node
synchronously, then @emph{defers} a dispatch onto the cmacs GMainContext
to one of four Elisp entry points
(@code{cmacs-libregnum--node-clicked} for scenes + gnuseye, and the
editor's @code{--on-select} / @code{--drop} / @code{--context-menu}).
Two invariants follow.  First, @emph{the dispatcher's file must be
loaded before a view can exist}: the dispatch goes through
@code{safe_call}, which mutes a @code{void-function} error -- the click
dies silently (the only trace is an ``Error muted by safe_call'' line
in @file{*Messages*}).  @file{cmacs-gnuseye.el} requires
@code{cmacs-libregnum} for exactly this reason; the editor's
dispatchers live in the same file as its entry command; the MCP
@code{lrg_editor_open} tool requires the file first.  Second,
@emph{numeric node ids go stale across scene rebuilds} (gnuseye's
smooth tick renumbers them every ~0.4 s), so gnuseye resolves clicks
by the pick-time @emph{path string} (its entity id); the editor is safe
by construction -- its node table changes only on explicit edits and
the engine-side selection happens synchronously at click time.

Keyboard (a highlighted selection cursor; the camera eases to frame
it and its name is echoed):

@table @kbd
@item @key{up} / k
select parent
@item @key{down} / j
select first child
@item @key{left} / h
select previous sibling
@item @key{right} / l
select next sibling
@item @key{RET}
open file / drill into directory
@item u (or ^)
go up one directory (re-root to parent)
@end table

Picking is a ray-cast (raylib @code{GetScreenToWorldRayEx} +
@code{GetRayCollisionBox} against each node's AABB) on the main
thread; the open/drill action is deferred onto the cmacs
GMainContext so no Lisp runs inside the GTK event handler.

@section Game module hosting

Beyond the built-in scene visualisers, a @code{cmacs-libregnum}
buffer can host a @emph{full libregnum game}.  A game built with the
@code{LRG_DEFINE_GAME_MODULE} macro produces a host-agnostic shared
module (@file{.so}); cmacs loads it, drives it each frame, and
forwards input to it, so the game runs inside an Emacs buffer.

@code{M-x cmacs-libregnum-play} prompts for a game module and opens a
buffer that renders and plays it.  Both @code{cmacs-libregnum-play} and
@code{cmacs-libregnum-load-game} take an optional @var{ARGV} --- a list of
strings passed verbatim to the module as a @code{main()}-style argument
vector, applied through libregnum's @code{LrgConfigurable} interface
(@code{lrg_game_template_apply_args}) before startup.  A rejected argument
warns but does not abort the load.  The screensaver subsystem uses this to
drive configured variants of one module (@pxref{CMacs Screensaver}).

@subsection How it works

The game is driven through libregnum's host abstraction
@code{LrgGameHost}.  cmacs implements @code{CmacsFboGameHost}, which
lends the game cmacs's already-running engine and renders it into the
view's FBO -- the same offscreen path the scene visualisers use -- so
no extra window is created.  Each frame, @code{render_to_bgra}
advances the game (@code{lrg_game_template_update}, with a monotonic
delta since the FBO path never presents a backbuffer) and draws it
(@code{lrg_game_template_render}), then reads the FBO back into the
buffer's surface.

Because the host owns no @code{LrgWindow}, an embedded game never
grabs the real cursor (an FPS enabling mouse-look is a no-op on the
cursor) and never starts or stops the shared engine (engine startup
is reference-counted in libregnum, so the host and the game share
one).

@subsection Input

@itemize @bullet
@item @emph{Mouse} is routed by the C layer: in a game buffer, the
@code{cmacs-libregnum-handle-motion}/@code{-button}/@code{-scroll}
handlers forward the pointer and buttons to the game instead of
orbiting the scene camera.
@item @emph{Keyboard} is forwarded by the
@code{cmacs-libregnum-game-mode} minor mode (enabled automatically by
@code{cmacs-libregnum-play}).  Emacs delivers no key-@emph{release}
events, so a held key is emulated from keyboard auto-repeat: the
first press is sent immediately, each auto-repeat re-arms a release
timer, and when the repeats stop the key is released.  Tune
@code{cmacs-libregnum-game-key-release-delay} (default @code{0.4}) a
little above your system's key-repeat delay to keep a held key from
stuttering.
@end itemize

Letters, digits, space, the arrows, common editing/function keys and
1:1 punctuation map to graylib @code{GrlKey} codes.  Quit with
@kbd{C-c C-q} (@code{cmacs-libregnum-game-quit}), which releases held
keys, unloads the game, and kills the buffer.

Under Doom/Evil the buffer stays in Evil emacs state so these keys reach
the game; the @kbd{C-w} window-command prefix is preserved, so window
management (@kbd{C-w v}, @kbd{C-w s}, @dots{}) still works while playing.

@subsection Host-agnostic modules

A game module links @emph{no} libregnum of its own: its libregnum
symbols are resolved at load time from whichever host loads it.  The
same @file{.so} runs unchanged under the standalone
@command{lrg-launcher} shim (which provides libregnum via its shared
library) and under cmacs (which static-links libregnum into Emacs with
@option{--whole-archive --export-dynamic}; see @emph{Building}).  This
keeps exactly one copy of the engine and the GType registry in the
process.

@subsection Game-hosting commands

@table @code
@item cmacs-libregnum-play
open a buffer hosting a game module
@item cmacs-libregnum-load-game
load a module into an existing view
@item cmacs-libregnum-unload-game
unload the hosted game
@item cmacs-libregnum-game-loaded-p
@code{t} if the buffer is hosting a game
@item cmacs-libregnum-game-mode
minor mode: forward keyboard to the game
@item cmacs-libregnum-game-quit
(@kbd{C-c C-q}) unload the game and kill the buffer
@end table

@section Default key bindings

@table @kbd
@item q
@code{kill-this-buffer}
@item g g
@code{cmacs-libregnum-redraw-current}
@item g r
@code{cmacs-libregnum-reload-from-buffer}
@item g s
@code{cmacs-libregnum-save-to-buffer}
@item a
@code{cmacs-libregnum-toggle-animation}
@end table

The project-tree navigation keys (arrows / @kbd{hjkl} / @key{RET} /
@kbd{u}) are documented above under @emph{Navigating the project
tree}.

@section Buffer text format

A minimal YAML subset.  Comments start with @code{#}; blank
lines are ignored.  Recognised keys: @code{scene_type},
@code{project_root}, @code{namespace}, @code{org_file},
@code{position}, @code{target}, @code{fov}.  The
@code{position}/@code{target}/@code{fov} triple is updated on
save via @code{before-save-hook} and re-applied on next open.

@section Building

Pass @option{--with-cmacs-libregnum} to @command{configure}.
The block requires @option{--with-cmacs-glib} and
@option{--with-pgtk}.  Configure probes for a system
@file{libregnum-1.0.pc}; otherwise it falls back to the bundled
@file{deps/libregnum} submodule.  Emacs links the libregnum static
archive with @option{-Wl,--whole-archive} and
@option{-Wl,--allow-multiple-definition}: the whole-archive pull-in
lets a buffer host an arbitrary game module (a host-agnostic module
resolves its libregnum symbols from Emacs, so every symbol -- not
just the ones cmacs itself calls -- must be present and, via
@option{--export-dynamic}, exported), and
@option{--allow-multiple-definition} absorbs raylib's inline raymath,
which the headers emit without @code{static} into multiple objects.

@section Known limitations (v1)

@itemize @bullet
@item Multi-window: each on-screen view now auto-resizes its framebuffer
to its window (a debounced @code{window-size-change} hook), so a single
view is no longer stretched on resize; the same buffer in two windows of
@emph{different} sizes still shares one framebuffer.
@item No text labels yet on the GObject / mindmap scenes.
@item Click-to-jump is not yet wired; tracked as follow-up work.
@end itemize

@node Editor / Level Authoring
@section Editor / Level Authoring

@cindex level authoring
@cindex LrgLevel
@cindex LrgNode
@cindex LrgEditor

@subsection Overview

libregnum now ships an IDE-hostable editor runtime alongside its game engine.
The relationship mirrors Unreal Engine@tie{}@arrow{}@tie{}Unreal Editor: the
@emph{editor runtime lives entirely inside libregnum} and is host-agnostic;
cmacs is one frontend and a standalone engine-drawn editor is another.  For
the full architecture and implementation sequencing, see the design plan at
@file{~/.claude/plans/we-recently-added-support-stateless-rossum.md}.

The level model (@code{LrgLevel} / @code{LrgNode}, serialized as
@file{.rlevel} YAML files) is a new @dfn{authoring layer} sitting above the
existing runtime layers:

@table @code
@item LrgLevel / LrgNode
The editable document (authoring layer; @file{deps/libregnum/src/editor/}).
@item LrgScene / LrgSceneObject
The renderer's geometry model (existing; produced by
@code{lrg_level_to_scene}).
@item LrgWorld / LrgGameObject
The ECS runtime (existing; produced by @code{lrg_level_instantiate} for
play-in-editor).
@end table

Neither bridge mutates the source @code{LrgLevel}, so editing, baked
previews, and play-in-editor can coexist safely.

@subsection Engine-side editor types (implemented)

The types in this section are part of the libregnum library and are present in
the @code{Libregnum} GIR typelib.  Because cmacs loads the typelib via the
@code{gi} subsystem, all of them are reachable from Elisp through
@code{cmacs-gi-require}, @code{cmacs-gi-call}, and
@code{cmacs-gi-method-call} today.

@subsubsection LrgLevel --- the level document

@code{LrgLevel} (@file{src/editor/lrg-level.h}) is a final GObject owning a
root @code{LrgNode} whose descendants form the editable scene tree plus
level-wide settings (name, @code{default-2d} flag).  It emits three signals so
a host IDE can keep its panels in sync:

@itemize @bullet
@item @code{node-added} (@var{node}: @code{LrgNode *})
@item @code{node-removed} (@var{node}: @code{LrgNode *})
@item @code{node-changed} (@var{node}: @code{LrgNode *})
@end itemize

Key API: @code{lrg_level_new}, @code{lrg_level_get_root},
@code{lrg_level_add_node}, @code{lrg_level_remove_node},
@code{lrg_level_find_node}, @code{lrg_level_notify_node_changed}.

@subsubsection LrgNode --- the scene-tree element

@code{LrgNode} (@file{src/editor/lrg-node.h}) is a @emph{derivable} GObject
--- the single unifier of 2D and 3D content.  Every node carries:

@itemize @bullet
@item a stable @code{guid} string (survives save/load round-trips);
@item a local TRS transform: @code{location} / @code{rotation} (Euler radians) /
  @code{scale} as @code{GrlVector3} values; world transform is computed lazily
  as @math{parent * T * R * S}; 2D nodes live at @math{Z=0} with
  @code{is-2d} set as a gizmo/camera hint;
@item an optional @code{LrgNodeVisual} payload;
@item ordered @code{GPtrArray}s of @code{LrgComponentDesc} and
  @code{LrgScriptBinding} records;
@item @code{visible} / @code{locked} flags.
@end itemize

Custom node kinds may subclass @code{LrgNode} and override the
@code{instantiate} virtual method.

@subsubsection LrgNodeVisual --- visual payload

@code{LrgNodeVisual} (@file{src/editor/lrg-node-visual.h}) is a tagged record
discriminated by @code{LrgNodeVisualKind}:

@table @code
@item LRG_NODE_VISUAL_NONE
Empty / group node.
@item LRG_NODE_VISUAL_PRIMITIVE
Built-in @code{LrgPrimitiveType} + @code{LrgMaterial3D}.
@item LRG_NODE_VISUAL_MESH_ASSET
External mesh asset (gltf/obj/glb) by reference + material.
@item LRG_NODE_VISUAL_SPRITE
2D texture asset reference.
@item LRG_NODE_VISUAL_TILEMAP
Tileset asset reference.
@item LRG_NODE_VISUAL_LIGHT
Kind-specific params (intensity, colour, @dots{}).
@item LRG_NODE_VISUAL_CAMERA
Kind-specific params (fov, near/far, @dots{}).
@item LRG_NODE_VISUAL_AUDIO_EMITTER
Audio asset reference + positional params.
@item LRG_NODE_VISUAL_PREFAB_INSTANCE
Prefab guid + property overrides.
@end table

Kind-specific scalars live in a typed @code{GValue} parameter bag
(@code{lrg_node_visual_set_param}, @code{get_param},
@code{set_param_double}, @code{get_param_double}).

@subsubsection LrgEditor --- the editor runtime core

@code{LrgEditor} (@file{src/editor/lrg-editor.h}) is a final GObject and the
central C API contract.  It owns the @code{LrgLevel} being edited, a selection
model (@code{LrgEditorSelection}), and a command stack: all mutating
operations go through reversible @code{LrgEditorCommand} objects.

Mutating operations: @code{lrg_editor_add_node}, @code{delete_node},
@code{reparent_node}, @code{set_node_transform} (full TRS in one command),
@code{set_node_property} (any @code{GObject} property).

Undo/redo: @code{lrg_editor_can_undo} / @code{can_redo} /
@code{undo} / @code{redo}.

Active tool: @code{lrg_editor_get_tool} / @code{set_tool} with
@code{LrgEditorTool} values (@code{SELECT}, @code{TRANSLATE},
@code{ROTATE}, @code{SCALE}).

Snap: @code{lrg_editor_set_snap} / @code{get_snap} (translate / rotate /
scale increments; 0 disables).

Create with @code{lrg_editor_new ()}: the new editor immediately has an empty
level ready to use.

@subsubsection LrgEditorHost --- the embedding contract

@code{LrgEditorHost} (@file{src/editor/lrg-editor-host.h}) is a GInterface
mirroring @code{LrgGameHost}: the environment the editor borrows from its
host.  A host IDE implements @code{get_engine},
@code{get_render_size}, @code{begin_frame} / @code{clear} / @code{end_frame},
@code{get_frame_delta}, @code{get_input_source},
@code{get_viewport_camera}, @code{is_2d_mode}, and @code{request_redraw}.
@code{LrgEditorSoftwareHost} is a simple concrete implementation that stores
the borrowed environment; it is used by IDE-driven embedding and by tests.

@subsubsection Bake and instantiate bridges

@code{lrg_level_to_scene (@var{level})} bakes the renderable (primitive-visual)
nodes of a level into a new @code{LrgScene}.
@code{lrg_level_instantiate (@var{level}, @var{world}, @var{engine}, @var{error})}
materializes the level into a runtime @code{LrgWorld} of
@code{LrgGameObject} instances with their components, for play-in-editor.
Neither bridge mutates the source @code{LrgLevel}.

@code{lrg_level_from_scene} (@file{src/editor/lrg-scene-import.h}) opens
existing Blender @file{.yaml} scene exports in the editor.

@subsubsection LrgProject and LrgAssetDatabase

@code{LrgProject} (@file{src/editor/lrg-project.h}) describes a game project
on disk: a root directory plus a @file{project.ryaml} manifest.

@code{LrgAssetDatabase} (@file{src/editor/lrg-asset-database.h}) scans the
project's asset directories, classifies assets by extension, assigns stable
guids, and exposes a queryable list suitable for an asset browser.

@subsubsection Other editor types

@table @code
@item LrgComponentDesc
Serializable component description: a registry type name plus a
@code{GHashTable} of property values; materialized at instantiate time.
@item LrgScriptBinding
A @{language, script_path_or_guid, enabled@} record.  @code{language} is a
@code{LrgScriptLanguage} enum value (@code{NONE}, @code{LUA}, @code{PYTHON},
@code{GJS}, @code{CRISPY}).
@item LrgPrefab
A @file{.rprefab} file is a saved one-node subtree with an exported-property
manifest; @code{lrg_prefab_save}, @code{load}, and @code{instantiate} manage
the round-trip.
@end table

Full engine API reference: @file{deps/libregnum/docs/editor/}.

@subsection Reaching the editor from cmacs today (via the gi subsystem)

The editor types are GI-annotated and present in the @code{Libregnum} typelib.
Using @code{cmacs-gi-require} / @code{cmacs-gi-call} /
@code{cmacs-gi-method-call} (or MCP @code{eval}) gives full access to the
level-authoring API from Elisp right now.

The general approach for headless level authoring from Elisp:

@lisp
;; Ensure the Libregnum typelib is loaded
(cmacs-gi-require "Libregnum")

;; Create a new level and editor
(let ((editor (cmacs-gi-call "Libregnum" "editor_new"))
      (level  (cmacs-gi-call "Libregnum" "level_new" "My Level")))

  ;; Add a node with a primitive visual
  (let* ((node   (cmacs-gi-call "Libregnum" "node_new" "Cube"))
         (visual (cmacs-gi-call "Libregnum" "node_visual_new"
                                (cmacs-gi-enum-value "Libregnum"
                                                     "NodeVisualKind"
                                                     "PRIMITIVE"))))
    (cmacs-gi-method-call visual "set_primitive"
                          (cmacs-gi-enum-value "Libregnum"
                                               "PrimitiveType" "CUBE"))
    (cmacs-gi-method-call node "set_visual" visual)
    (cmacs-gi-method-call editor "add_node" node nil))

  ;; Save to a .rlevel file
  (cmacs-gi-method-call editor "save_level"
                        "/tmp/my-level.rlevel" nil))
@end lisp

The snippet above illustrates the approach.  Exact GI function names follow
the C symbols with the @code{lrg_} prefix and class-name portion stripped per
typelib convention.  When in doubt, use
@code{(cmacs-gi-list-functions "Libregnum")} to enumerate what is available,
or @code{(cmacs-gi-describe "Libregnum" "editor_new")} to inspect a specific
function's signature.

Play-in-editor is reachable from Elisp too: @code{lrg_level_instantiate}
produces a @code{LrgWorld} and the existing game @file{.so} hosting
(see the game-module hosting section) can run a game against that world in a buffer.

@subsection Native editor frontend

The core native editor is implemented: an @code{cmacs-libregnum-editor-mode}
viewport buffer hosting an engine @code{LrgEditor}, with the level baked into
the scene drawables so the existing FBO/Cairo blit pipeline, ray-picking and
@code{cmacs-libregnum-tree-nodes} node model double as the editor viewport and
outliner.  Objects move by mouse drag or keyboard nudge with optional grid
snapping.  A property inspector, asset-browser drag-and-drop, rotate/scale
gizmo handles and play-in-editor remain planned.

@subsubsection Implemented

@table @asis
@item C layer
An editor host path @emph{inside} @file{cmacs-libregnum-render.c} (no new
translation unit, hence no @command{configure} change), reusing the
scene-drawable pipeline rather than a separate gizmo renderer.  It exposes a
plain-C opaque-pointer API (@code{cmacs_libregnum_render_ctx_editor_*}) across
the raylib firewall, wrapped by DEFUNs in @file{cmacs-libregnum-defuns.c}:
@code{cmacs-libregnum-editor-new}/@code{-open}/@code{-save},
@code{-active-p}, @code{-add-primitive} (returns a node id),
@code{-delete}/@code{-select}, @code{-set-position} (one undoable edit),
@code{-undo}/@code{-redo}/@code{-can-undo-p}, and @code{-node-guid}; plus
@code{-node-location} and @code{-selected-id} (the engine selection shared by
mouse and keyboard), @code{-focus} (frame a node), @code{-set-snap} (translate
grid), the @code{-drag-begin}/@code{-drag-update}/@code{-drag-end} mouse-move
pipeline (view pixels → ground-plane move, one coalesced undo step per drag),
@code{-node-rotation}/@code{-node-scale} + @code{-set-rotation}/@code{-set-scale}
(each one undoable transform edit), @code{-reparent} (re-nest a node),
@code{-add-visual} (mesh-asset/sprite/light/camera/audio nodes; MESH_ASSET loads
+ draws the real model), @code{-attach-script}/@code{-node-script-count},
@code{-play}/@code{-stop}/@code{-playing-p}/@code{-play-tick} (play-in-editor),
@code{-set-tool}/@code{-get-tool} + @code{-gizmo-begin}/@code{-gizmo-drag}/
@code{-gizmo-end} (on-screen transform gizmo), and @code{cmacs-libregnum-project}
(world @math{->} view pixels).  Plus @code{cmacs-libregnum-snapshot}
(BUFFER PATH), which
renders the view synchronously to a PNG via engine FBO read-back (works
headless; used by the render/move ERT tests).
@code{cmacs-libregnum-editor-set-shading}/@code{-shading-p}/@code{-toggle-shading}
enable/query/flip the Blinn-Phong shader pass for mesh geometry;
@code{cmacs-libregnum-editor-look-through} (@var{buffer} @var{node-id}) renders
the viewport from a CAMERA node's transform,
@code{cmacs-libregnum-editor-look-through-off} restores the edit camera, and
@code{cmacs-libregnum-editor-look-through-p} queries the state.

@item Elisp layer
In @file{lisp/cmacs/cmacs-libregnum.el}: @code{cmacs-libregnum-editor} (entry
command; empty level, or a @file{.rlevel} with a prefix arg);
@code{cmacs-libregnum-editor-mode} (derived from @code{cmacs-libregnum-mode})
with keymap @kbd{c}/@kbd{b}/@kbd{y}/@kbd{n} (add cube/sphere/cylinder/plane),
arrows or @kbd{hjkl} (nudge on the ground X/Z), @kbd{J}/@kbd{K} and PageUp/Down
(nudge vertically Y), @kbd{[}/@kbd{]} (rotate yaw; shifted for pitch),
@kbd{+}/@kbd{-} (scale), @kbd{s} (cycle snap grid), @kbd{f} (frame
selection), @kbd{m} (type exact coordinates), @kbd{x}/@kbd{<delete>} (delete),
@kbd{u}/@kbd{C-r} (undo/redo), @kbd{p} (palette), @kbd{o} (outliner), @kbd{i}
(inspector), @kbd{C-x C-s} (save-as) --- numeric prefixes multiply the
move/rotate/scale step; also @kbd{A} (asset browser), @kbd{L} (attach script),
@kbd{<f5>} (play/stop).  A left-column @strong{palette}
(@code{cmacs-libregnum-palette-mode}) in three sections --- Primitives (all
eleven: Cube/Sphere/IcoSphere/Cylinder/Cone/Torus/Plane/Circle/Grid/Rect2D/
Circle2D), Objects (Light/Camera/Audio), Assets (Mesh.../Sprite..., prompt for a
file) --- over the node-tree @strong{outliner}
(@code{cmacs-libregnum-outliner-mode}: @kbd{RET} selects, @kbd{m} marks then
@kbd{P} reparents under point, @kbd{r} to root); a right-column @strong{inspector}
(@code{cmacs-libregnum-inspector-mode}, key @kbd{i}): a @code{widget.el} form of
the selection's position/rotation/scale whose @kbd{C-c C-c} (or @emph{Apply})
routes edits through the undoable command stack; and an @strong{asset browser}
(@code{cmacs-libregnum-assets-mode}, key @kbd{A}) that lists model/image files to
click-place as mesh/sprite nodes.  Under Evil the palette/outliner/assets are
read-only @code{motion}-state panels (so @kbd{hjkl} navigates); the viewport and
inspector stay in @code{emacs} state.

@strong{Transforming objects:} click a shape to select it (outliner + inspector
follow); an @strong{on-screen gizmo} draws over it --- @kbd{w} translate (drag
the coloured arrows), @kbd{e} rotate (drag the rings), @kbd{r} scale (drag the
cube-tipped handles), @kbd{t} hides it.  Dragging a handle is axis-constrained;
or @emph{drag the body} to free-move on the ground plane, or @emph{nudge} with
arrows / @kbd{hjkl} (@kbd{[}/@kbd{]} rotate, @kbd{+}/@kbd{-} scale).  The bake
applies each node's rotation + scale, so all transforms show live.  @kbd{s}
cycles a snap grid honoured by drag/gizmo/nudge, @kbd{f} frames the camera.
Dragging empty space orbits; one drag (handle or body) is a single undo step.
Edits go through the engine's undoable @code{LrgEditorCommand} stack; levels
round-trip to @file{.rlevel}.
@end table

@subsubsection Now also implemented

@itemize
@item @strong{Real mesh + sprite rendering} --- MESH_ASSET loads + draws a
glTF/OBJ model; SPRITE draws its texture as a flat textured quad via a unit-plane
@code{GrlModel} (the mesh/VAO path composites in the editor FBO batch, unlike
raylib's @code{rlgl} textured immediate mode / @code{DrawBillboard}).
@item @strong{On-screen gizmo handles} (@kbd{w}/@kbd{e}/@kbd{r}) ---
axis-constrained translate/rotate/scale, one coalesced undo per drag.
@item @strong{2D camera mode} (@kbd{v}) --- top-down orthographic view;
picking/projection honour the projection.
@item @strong{Running script logic} --- the crispy backend is enabled
(@file{deps/libregnum} built @code{CRISPY=1}; cmacs already links
@code{libcrispy}), so a crispy script attached to a node compiles + runs when
the level plays.  @code{lrg_level_instantiate} materialises each node's
@code{LrgScriptBinding} as an @code{LrgScriptComponent}.
@item @strong{Play rendering} --- @code{play-tick} reflects the running world's
object positions back onto the baked scene, so script motion shows live.
@item @strong{Drop-at-point} --- arm a palette/asset item (@kbd{D}) then click a
viewport point; the input layer ray-casts to the ground plane and drops it.
@item @strong{Mouse drag-and-drop} --- two paths: (a) Emacs's own
@code{drag-mouse-1} event for intra-frame drops from palette/asset rows to the
viewport (no upstream edit); (b) a GTK-native GDK drag-source
(@code{[down-mouse-1]} → @code{cmacs-libregnum-dnd-arm} →
@code{gtk_drag_begin_with_coordinates}) for cross-process DnD to external GTK
apps.  The latter requires two thin @code{pgtkterm.c} hunks described in the
Upstream Changes chapter.
@item @strong{Right-click context menu} --- @strong{right-click} an entity in the
viewport to pop a real GTK menu of actions on it (a @strong{right-drag} still
pans, @code{scroll} still zooms).  It is Emacs's own @code{x-popup-menu}, which
the pgtk build renders as a native @code{GtkMenu}, so it matches every other
right-click menu in Emacs with no raw GTK code.  The C input layer ray-picks the
node, captures the ground point, and defers
@code{cmacs-libregnum-editor--context-menu}, which pops the menu from the command
loop (a 0-delay timer) --- never from the GLib dispatch, where a nested GTK menu
loop must not run during the pselect wait.  The picked node is selected first, so
the chosen action operates on it.  Items vary by the node's @emph{kind}: every
node gets the common actions (Rename, Set position, a Rotate submenu, Reset
transform, Duplicate, Toggle visible/locked, an Attach script submenu, Inspector,
Focus camera, Reparent to root, Save as prefab, Delete); @emph{mesh/sprite/audio}
add Open asset file; @emph{light} adds Set light range/color; @emph{camera} adds
Set camera FOV; @emph{audio} adds Set audio range + Play audio; @emph{tilemap}
adds Set tilemap brush.  Two items open real nested GTK submenus: @emph{Rotate}
offers ±45° and ±90° about each axis (X / Y / Z) plus Reset rotation;
@emph{Attach script} offers New script@dots{} (scaffold a starter file in the
chosen language, attach, and open it) and Existing script@dots{}.
Right-clicking empty space pops an @emph{Add} menu (the palette's Primitives /
Objects / Assets sections) that places the chosen item at the clicked ground
point.  A subsystem can append its @emph{own} actions per-buffer through the
buffer-local @code{cmacs-libregnum-editor-extra-menu-items} (appended after the
built-ins), keeping the editor core subsystem-agnostic --- the CAD model viewer
uses it for its print section (Export G-code, Slicer settings, @dots{}).

@emph{Rename} showcases the new name in the outliner: each row reads
@code{Type (Name)} once a node is renamed (e.g.@: @code{Cube (Player)}) and just
@code{Type} otherwise, so the type stays visible and the custom name stands out.
Renaming goes through @code{cmacs-libregnum-editor-set-name}, which re-bakes the
level so the outliner's bake-time-cached labels refresh (a plain GObject
@code{name} set would not); the concrete shape label comes from
@code{cmacs-libregnum-editor-node-primitive}.

The menu is data-driven and easy to extend: it is built from
@code{cmacs-libregnum-editor-context-menu-items}, whose entries are plists ---
@code{(:label STRING :kinds KINDS :action FN)} for a command,
@code{(:label STRING :kinds KINDS :submenu SYMBOL)} for a static submenu (SYMBOL
names a list of sub-items in the same shape),
@code{(:label STRING :kinds KINDS :items-fn FN)} for a @emph{dynamic} submenu
(@code{FN} is called as @code{(FN BUFFER ID)} to build the item list at pop
time --- used by the scripts submenu to reflect the node's current attachment
list), or @code{(:sep)} for a divider.  Entries may carry @code{:enable PRED}
(a predicate called as @code{(PRED BUFFER ID)} to grey out inapplicable items)
and @code{:keys STRING} to show a keyboard accelerator.  @code{KINDS} is
@code{t} (all kinds) or a list of node-kind symbols (@code{group},
@code{primitive}, @code{mesh}, @code{sprite}, @code{tilemap}, @code{light},
@code{camera}, @code{audio}, @code{prefab}); action @code{FN} is called as
@code{(FN BUFFER ID)}.  Adding an item or a whole submenu is a one-line edit.
The menu header shows the right-clicked node's label.  The same menu is
available by right-clicking a row in the @strong{outliner} panel, not only in
the 3-D viewport.  The menu is a keymap (so submenus are native), popped with
@code{x-popup-menu} and resolved with @code{lookup-key}.  The node kind comes
from the @code{cmacs-libregnum-editor-node-kind} DEFUN (an int matching the
@code{cmacs-libregnum-visual-*} constants; @code{1} = a primitive, @code{nil} =
a group node).  Like the @code{drag-mouse-1} path, it needs no upstream pgtk
change --- it rides the existing libregnum button hook.

@emph{Expanded common items (all kinds)} --- the set of actions every node
receives was enlarged:

@itemize @minus
@item @strong{Scale @U{25B8}} submenu --- @emph{2@U{00D7}}, @emph{@U{00BD}@U{00D7}},
@emph{Reset scale}, @emph{Set scale@dots{}} (prompted).
@item @strong{Drop to ground} --- snaps the node's Y position to the ground
plane.
@item @strong{Snap to grid} --- rounds the position to the current grid size.
@item @strong{Reset position} --- zeroes the node's translation.
@item @strong{Tool @U{25B8}} submenu --- switch the active gizmo to
@emph{Translate}, @emph{Rotate}, @emph{Scale}, or @emph{Select}.
@item @strong{Cut} / @strong{Copy} / @strong{Paste} --- clipboard operations
via a temporary prefab; @strong{Copy GUID} copies the node's identifier string.
@item @strong{Reparent under@dots{}} --- interactively pick a new parent node
(complements the existing @emph{Reparent to root}).
@item @strong{Add child @U{25B8}} --- add a new primitive or group under this
node.
@item @strong{Add empty group} --- inserts a bare group node as a child.
DEFUNs: @code{cmacs-libregnum-editor-node-parent},
@code{cmacs-libregnum-editor-add-empty}.
@item @strong{Select @U{25B8}} submenu --- @emph{Add to selection},
@emph{Remove from selection}, @emph{Clear selection}, @emph{Select parent} ---
backed by @code{LrgEditorSelection} and
@code{cmacs-libregnum-editor-select-add} /
@code{-select-remove} / @code{-select-clear} / @code{-selected-ids}.
@item @strong{Rotate in place} --- rotation about all three axes (the gizmo
and the @emph{Rotate} submenu) pivots about the node's own bounding-box centre,
and the selection box tracks the rotated extents, so a part turns in place
instead of swinging about the world origin.  The model draw composes the full
Euler XYZ orientation (quaternion @arrow{} axis-angle), not just yaw.
@item @strong{Per-window framebuffer fit} --- a debounced
@code{window-size-change} hook keeps each on-screen view's framebuffer sized to
its window, so the 1:1 overlay blit never stretches content as windows resize;
@code{cmacs_libregnum_render_ctx_resize} keeps the old framebuffer if a
reallocation fails, so it never blanks the view.
@end itemize

@emph{Appearance (primitive/mesh)} --- a @strong{Material @U{25B8}} submenu
lets you @emph{Set color} (@code{cmacs-libregnum-editor-set-color}),
@emph{Set roughness} (@code{-set-roughness}), and @emph{Set metallic}
(@code{-set-metallic}), written through the node's @code{LrgMaterial3D}.
The bake now honours a node's authored material colour instead of a fixed
per-kind colour; @code{cmacs-libregnum-editor-node-color} reads it back.
@emph{Toggle wireframe} authors a @code{"wireframe"} visual param rendered via
@code{grl_model_draw_wires_ex}; @emph{Toggle cast shadow} authors a
@code{"cast_shadow"} param (persisted in the @file{.rlevel}; shadow mapping
is not yet implemented).

@emph{Duplicate} now performs a native engine deep-clone (fresh GUIDs for the
entire subtree) placed under the original node's parent, replacing the older
temp-prefab path.  DEFUN: @code{cmacs-libregnum-editor-duplicate-node}.

@emph{Manage scripts @U{25B8}} --- a dynamic submenu that lists each script
currently attached to the node with @emph{Edit} and @emph{Detach} sub-actions
(@code{cmacs-libregnum-editor-node-scripts} / @code{-detach-script}), plus
@emph{Attach new@dots{}} and @emph{Attach existing@dots{}}.

@emph{Per-kind additions:}

@itemize @minus
@item @strong{light} --- @emph{Set intensity} and a @strong{Light type @U{25B8}}
submenu to switch between @emph{Point}, @emph{Spot}, and @emph{Directional}.
@item @strong{camera} --- @emph{Align to current view}
(@code{cmacs-libregnum-camera-state}) snaps the camera node to the live
viewport camera pose; @emph{Look through this camera}
(@code{cmacs-libregnum-editor-look-through}) renders the viewport from that
camera until @code{cmacs-libregnum-editor-look-through-off} is called.
@item @strong{audio}, @strong{mesh}, @strong{sprite} --- @emph{Replace asset}
(@code{cmacs-libregnum-editor-set-node-asset}) swaps the underlying asset file
without recreating the node.
@item @strong{tilemap} --- @emph{Enter paint mode}, @emph{Clear}, and
@emph{Resize} (in addition to the existing @emph{Set tilemap brush}).
@item @strong{prefab} --- @emph{Unpack}
(@code{cmacs-libregnum-editor-unpack-prefab}) expands the prefab instance into a
plain node subtree in place.
@end itemize

@emph{Multi-selection} --- the engine @code{LrgEditorSelection} is exposed as
@code{cmacs-libregnum-editor-select-add} / @code{-select-remove} /
@code{-select-clear} / @code{-selected-ids}.  Several nodes can be selected
simultaneously (each draws its own wireframe); the menu offers @emph{Delete
selected} and @emph{Group selected} when a multi-selection is active.
@item @strong{Tilemaps --- paint, render, persist} --- @kbd{T}
(@code{cmacs-libregnum-editor-tilemap-create}) makes a TILEMAP node from a
tileset image + tile/map dimensions; @code{cmacs-libregnum-editor-tilemap-paint}
arms a sticky brush so each viewport click paints the cell under the cursor
(@code{...-tilemap-set-brush}, finish with @code{...-stop-paint}).  The tile grid
lives in the node's visual params (@code{tw}/@code{th}/@code{cols}/@code{mw}/@code{mh}/@code{tiles}
CSV) so it round-trips in the @file{.rlevel}.  Each non-empty cell renders the
actual tileset art: the tile sub-rect is sliced
(@code{grl_image_from_region}@tie{}+@tie{}@code{grl_texture_new_from_image}) into
a textured unit-plane @code{GrlModel} drawn on the ground (the mesh path that
composites in the FBO batch), cached per tile index, with a colour-sampled cell
fallback.  DEFUNs: @code{...-tilemap-config}/@code{-set-tile}/@code{-info}.
@item @strong{Dedicated MCP tools} --- @file{cmacs/mcp/cmacs-mcp-tools-libregnum.c}
registers @code{lrg_editor_object_tree}/@code{_add_primitive}/@code{_add_visual}/
@code{_move}/@code{_select}/@code{_delete}/@code{_open}/@code{_save}/@code{_play}/
@code{_stop}.
@item @strong{Introspection-driven inspector} --- alongside the undoable
transform fields, the inspector enumerates the selected node's GObject
properties (name/visible/locked/is-2d/@dots{}) through a node@tie{}GObject bridge
(@code{cmacs-libregnum-editor-node-object}) +
@code{gobject-list-properties}/@code{-property-info}/@code{-get}/@code{-set};
widgets are chosen per @code{GParamSpec} type.
@item @strong{Prefabs} --- @kbd{C-c p} saves the selected subtree to a
@file{.rprefab}; @kbd{C-c P} instantiates one under the selection/root.
@item @strong{Scene import} --- @kbd{C-c i} imports a Blender-exported
@file{.yaml} scene as the current level.
@item @strong{Multi-language scripts (Lua / Python / Gjs / crispy)} --- the
@kbd{L} picker lists the backends libregnum was built with (runtime
@code{lrg_scripting_manager_get_available}).  With the scripting @code{-devel}
packages installed (@code{luajit-devel}, @code{python3-devel}, @code{gjs-devel};
see @file{install-deps}) all four are available; cmacs links the system
@code{libluajit}/@code{libpython}/@code{libgjs}+@code{libmozjs}.  Each degrades
out cleanly when its @code{-devel} is absent (crispy is always present).
@item @strong{Script hot-reload} --- attaching via @kbd{L} watches the script
file; saving it restarts play so the script recompiles.
@item @strong{Projects + asset database} --- @code{cmacs-libregnum-project-new}
scaffolds a project (levels/assets/scripts + @file{project.ryaml} + a starter
level); @code{-project-open} loads a project's default level
(@code{LrgProject}); @code{cmacs-libregnum-assetdb-scan} classifies a directory
via @code{LrgAssetDatabase}.
@item @strong{Lights / cameras / audio --- viz + playback} --- the viewport
draws each light's range as a wireframe sphere in its authored colour, each
camera as a frustum at its FOV, and each audio emitter as a range sphere (live
from the node's visual params).  Author with @code{cmacs-libregnum-editor-set-light}
/ @code{-set-camera-fov} / @code{-set-audio-range}, and play an emitter's sound
with @kbd{C-c a} (@code{cmacs-libregnum-editor-play-audio}, via the cmacs
GStreamer audio subsystem when @option{--with-cmacs-audio} is present, falling
back to @code{play-sound-file}).  The context menu exposes @emph{Stop audio}
and @emph{Set volume}; volume persists as a node param.
@item @strong{GObject Introspection (gjs + Python-GI)} --- the cmacs build
auto-builds @code{Libregnum-1.typelib} (when @code{g-ir-scanner} is present) so
the editor/authoring types (@code{LrgLevel}, @code{LrgNode}, @code{LrgEditor},
@code{LrgProject}, @code{LrgScriptingManager}, the enums, …) are usable over GI.
With @code{GI_TYPELIB_PATH} set (the Justfile @code{run} recipe does so), gjs
(@code{imports.gi.Libregnum}) and Python (@code{from gi.repository import
Libregnum}, needs @code{python3-gobject}) fully import and drive them --- this is
what gives a libregnum game's gjs/Python scripting backends typed engine access.
cmacs's own @code{gi} subsystem loads it via @code{(cmacs-gi-require
"Libregnum")}.  The internal GI scripting bridges are excluded from the scan so
the typelib has no unresolvable @code{GIRepository-2.0} dependency.  (Caveat: a
gjs/python script run @emph{embedded inside} cmacs still conflicts with cmacs's
own @code{libgirepository} --- two GI runtimes in one process --- which is
deferred; see the Design notes.)
@item @strong{Real-time scene shading} --- an opt-in toggle
(@code{cmacs-libregnum-editor-toggle-shading}; getter/setter
@code{cmacs-libregnum-editor-set-shading}/@code{-shading-p}) loads a
Blinn-Phong GLSL shader and illuminates MESH_ASSET geometry from the scene's
LIGHT nodes (position, colour, intensity).  Imported glTF/OBJ models are lit in
the editor viewport.  Scope: only the shaderable VAO/mesh path; primitive
immediate-mode shapes and editor gizmos stay flat by design; no shadow mapping
yet.
@item @strong{Look-through camera} --- @emph{Look through this camera} in the
right-click context menu renders the viewport from the selected camera node's
transform (position, yaw/pitch, and its @code{fov} param).  Implemented by
overriding the single camera chokepoint so picking, projection, and gizmos stay
consistent; orbit/pan/zoom are suspended while looking through.
DEFUNs: @code{cmacs-libregnum-editor-look-through} (@var{buffer} @var{node-id}),
@code{cmacs-libregnum-editor-look-through-off} (@var{buffer}),
@code{cmacs-libregnum-editor-look-through-p} (@var{buffer}).
@item @strong{Audio volume + stop} --- audio nodes are now played through the
cmacs GStreamer audio subsystem (async, with a pipeline handle) so @emph{Stop
audio} and @emph{Set volume} work in the context menu; volume persists as a
node param.  Falls back to @code{play-sound-file} when
@option{--with-cmacs-audio} is absent.
@item @strong{Wireframe per node} --- @emph{Toggle wireframe} in the context
menu authors a @code{"wireframe"} visual param honoured by the render loop
(mesh assets via @code{grl_model_draw_wires_ex}).  @emph{Toggle cast shadow}
authors a @code{"cast_shadow"} param persisted in the @file{.rlevel}; actual
shadow mapping remains deferred.
@end itemize

@subsubsection Planned

The following items remain for future renderer work:

@itemize @bullet
@item @strong{Shadow mapping} --- real-time shadows from LIGHT nodes; the
@code{"cast_shadow"} node param is authored now but not yet consumed by a
shadow pass.
@item @strong{Shading of primitive immediate-mode shapes} --- the Blinn-Phong
shader covers the mesh/VAO path; primitive shapes drawn via immediate-mode
calls stay flat.
@item @strong{Spatial/3D audio attenuation} --- audio range is visualised and
the volume knob persists as a param, but distance-based attenuation is not yet
wired in the GStreamer pipeline.
@end itemize

@subsubsection Context-menu items formerly deferred --- now implemented

The following four items were carried as deferred in earlier versions and are
now implemented:

@itemize @bullet
@item @strong{Real-time scene shading} --- Blinn-Phong GLSL shader for
mesh/model geometry.
@item @strong{Look-through camera} --- viewport renders from the selected
camera node.
@item @strong{Audio volume / stop} --- GStreamer pipeline handle; volume
persists as a node param; fallback to @code{play-sound-file} when absent.
@item @strong{Wireframe / cast-shadow flags} --- @code{"wireframe"} and
@code{"cast_shadow"} visual params authored per node; shadow mapping remains
deferred.
@end itemize

@subsubsection Design notes & potential improvements

@file{doc_org/cmacs/cmacs-libregnum-design-notes.org} catalogues the deliberate
scope boundaries (with rationale + an improvement path each).  In brief:

@itemize @bullet
@item @strong{Remaining renderer work} --- shadow mapping (the @code{"cast_shadow"}
param is authored, not yet consumed), shading of primitive immediate-mode shapes
(only mesh/VAO geometry is lit), spatial audio attenuation.
@item @strong{Component authoring} --- the inspector edits node GObject props +
attaches scripts, but there is no in-editor "Add Component" + property grid for
@code{LrgComponentDesc} (components still instantiate at play + persist).
@item @strong{Project build-and-play} --- @code{project-new}/@code{-open} + load
work; compiling the project's game @code{.so} from the editor (build-then-play)
is not wired.
@item @strong{Python / Gjs runtime} --- enabled, linked, listed; Lua is
smoke-tested running, Python/Gjs use the same path but aren't yet run-verified.
@item @strong{Minor / by design} --- asset browser uses a directory scan (not
@code{LrgAssetDatabase}); the standalone editor app cycles selection + omits
viewport rotation; prefabs are fresh copies (no override link); tilemaps persist
inline (no standalone @code{.rtilemap}); GIR generation stays off; some new
features lack dedicated ERT tests.
@item @strong{Renderer constraint} --- raylib @code{rlgl} textured immediate
mode does not composite in the editor FBO batch, so sprites/tiles use textured
@code{GrlModel} planes (mesh/VAO path) and ranges/frustums use untextured lines.
@end itemize

