@c ===================================================================
@node CMacs Gsurf
@chapter CMacs Gsurf

This chapter mirrors @file{doc_org/cmacs/cmacs-gsurf.org}.

@code{cmacs-gsurf} embeds gsurf --- a GLib/GObject port of suckless
@command{surf} --- as a first-class subsystem.  Each
@code{cmacs-gsurf-mode} buffer owns a live WebKitGTK view (a
@code{GsurfView}) parented into the buffer's pgtk frame and clipped to
the window showing it (xwidget-style live embed): the window body
@emph{is} the browser.

gsurf is library-first, so cmacs links @file{libgsurf.a} statically and
drives it directly.  surf's ``patches'' are gsurf @dfn{modules}
(runtime-loaded @file{.so} plugins) that apply to the embedded views:
search engines, ad-block, vim-style modal navigation with link hints,
history, dark mode, and more.

@section Opening a browser buffer

@deffn Command cmacs-gsurf &optional url
Open a @code{cmacs-gsurf-mode} buffer at @var{url} (prompted if nil).
Bare words become a search via @code{cmacs-gsurf-search-url}.
@end deffn

@deffn Command cmacs-gsurf-browse-url url &rest _
Open @var{url} in a gsurf buffer.  Suitable as
@code{browse-url-browser-function}.
@end deffn

@section Focus model

The window body is a live GTK widget; if it held keyboard focus
permanently, Emacs (and evil) would never see your keys.  So
cmacs-gsurf manages focus explicitly.  A gsurf buffer @emph{starts under
Emacs/evil control} (the page is shown but does not capture keys), so
all normal Emacs and evil keys work --- window management (@kbd{C-w v}),
motions, @kbd{M-x}, etc. --- plus the browsing commands.  To type
into/scroll the page, give it focus with @kbd{RET}/@kbd{i},
@code{cmacs-gsurf-focus-page} (@kbd{C-c C-f}), or a click.  To return to
Emacs, press @kbd{Escape} (pulls focus back and drops evil to normal
state), @code{cmacs-gsurf-release-focus} (@kbd{C-c C-g}), or select
another window (clicking another Emacs window releases page focus
automatically).

Two things work @emph{without} focusing the page (the buffer text is a
hidden placeholder, so these are rebound from evil motions):
@kbd{h}/@kbd{j}/@kbd{k}/@kbd{l} (and @kbd{C-d}/@kbd{C-u} half-page,
@kbd{C-f}/@kbd{C-b} full-page, @kbd{gg}/@kbd{G} top/bottom) scroll the
live page, and @kbd{f} (@code{cmacs-gsurf-follow}) pops vimium-style
link hints --- type the chord to click a link.  @kbd{SPC} is left
unbound so the evil/Doom leader (@kbd{SPC b b}, etc.) works.

The rest of the @code{cmacs-gsurf-mode} keymap: @kbd{RET}/@kbd{i} focus
page (type), @kbd{r} reload, @kbd{R} reload-nocache, @kbd{H}/@kbd{B}
back, @kbd{L}/@kbd{F} forward, @kbd{o}/@kbd{C-c C-o} open URL,
@kbd{+}/@kbd{-}/@kbd{0} zoom, @kbd{/}/@kbd{s} find, @kbd{q} quit.  Under
evil these are bound in normal+motion state (so evil motions don't
shadow them) and @kbd{C-w} is left to evil's window map.  Once the page
has focus, gsurf's own @code{modal} module (its own vim keys, @kbd{f}
link hints, @kbd{i} insert) takes over until you press @kbd{Escape}.

@section Caret mode

@cindex caret browsing
@code{cmacs-gsurf-caret-mode} is a minor mode (on by default, see
@code{cmacs-gsurf-caret-mode-default}) that gives the live browser
buffer an in-page @emph{text cursor} you move with Emacs/evil motions,
so a page reads like a normal buffer.  It is driven entirely from Emacs
by injecting JavaScript that moves the DOM selection
(@code{getSelection().modify}) and paints a fixed-position caret
overlay; the page is @emph{never focused}, so the @kbd{SPC} leader,
@kbd{C-w} and @kbd{M-x} keep working.  (It is deliberately not a gsurf
input-handler module --- those only fire when the page holds GTK
keyboard focus, which the focus model forbids.)

@table @kbd
@item h / l
Move the caret left / right by a character.
@item j / k
Move the caret down / up by a line.
@item w / b / e
Move the caret by a word.
@item 0 / $
Move the caret to the line start / end.
@item gg / G
Move the caret to the document top / bottom.
@item v
Start a highlight (subsequent motions extend it).
@item y, M-w
Copy the highlight to the kill-ring and system clipboard.
@item RET
Activate (click) the link or button under the caret.
@item /
Search the page and jump the caret to the match (via
@code{window.find}).
@item n / N
Jump to the next / previous search match.
@end table

The page auto-scrolls to keep the caret in view.  @kbd{C-d}/@kbd{C-u}
(half page), @kbd{C-f}/@kbd{C-b} (full page) and @kbd{C-e}/@kbd{C-y}
(one line, vim-style) all scroll, @kbd{f} pops link hints, and
@kbd{SPC} stays free for the leader.  While caret mode is on, @kbd{0}
and @kbd{$} move the caret; zoom-reset stays on @kbd{+} / @kbd{-} /
@kbd{=}.

@deffn Command cmacs-gsurf-run-javascript-async buffer script callback
Run @var{script} in @var{buffer}'s view and call @var{callback} with
the script's value as a string (JSON for non-strings).  Unlike
@code{cmacs-gsurf-run-javascript} (fire-and-forget) this is the result
return channel the caret @kbd{y} command and gsurf-lite use.
@end deffn

@deffn Command cmacs-gsurf-add-user-script buffer script &optional at-end
Inject @var{script} into every page of @var{buffer}'s view (persists
across navigation).  Used to install the caret engine once per view.
@end deffn

@section Page events and hooks

Each live view bridges its WebKit signals to Emacs abnormal hooks.  In
addition to the navigation hooks
(@code{cmacs-gsurf-@{load,uri,title@}-changed-functions}), these fire:

@table @code
@item cmacs-gsurf-hovered-uri-changed-functions
@code{(BUFFER URI)} --- the link under the pointer; shown in the echo
area by default (@code{cmacs-gsurf-show-hovered-uri}).
@item cmacs-gsurf-progress-changed-functions
@code{(BUFFER FRACTION)} --- drives a @code{[NN%]} mode-line indicator.
@item cmacs-gsurf-crashed-functions
@code{(BUFFER)} --- the web process died; a header-line notice appears
(reload with @kbd{r}, or set @code{cmacs-gsurf-auto-reload-on-crash}).
@item cmacs-gsurf-favicon-changed-functions
@code{(BUFFER)} --- refresh the header line.
@item cmacs-gsurf-download-changed-functions
@code{(ID URI DEST RECEIVED TOTAL STATE)} --- download lifecycle.
@item cmacs-gsurf-permission-request-functions
@code{(BUFFER ORIGIN TYPE)} --- a denied permission request.
@end table

The title hook renames the buffer to @samp{*gsurf: TITLE*} (unless
@code{cmacs-gsurf-rename-buffers} is nil).  Popups (@code{target=_blank},
@code{window.open}, middle-click) open in a new gsurf buffer displayed
per @code{cmacs-gsurf-popup-display-function}.

@section Render backends (pgtk vs --lrg)

gsurf has two render backends in one @file{libgsurf}, selected @emph{at
runtime by the Emacs frame type}; every @code{cmacs-gsurf-*} command drives
whichever backend the frame uses (there is no separate Elisp surface).

@table @asis
@item GTK3 / WebKitGTK (pgtk)
The default.  The live @code{WebKitWebView} widget is parented into the pgtk
frame's @code{GtkFixed} and clipped to the window body.

@item libregnum / WebKit (@command{emacs --lrg})
Built with @option{--with-cmacs-gsurf-lrg} (requires
@option{--with-cmacs-libregnum}).  Under the lrgterm display backend there
is no GTK widget tree, so the page is rendered to a libregnum
@code{GrlTexture} and composited into the buffer's window rectangle by
lrgterm (@code{lrg_paint_libregnum_window}), like a cmacs-libregnum 3D
buffer.  Raylib input is routed back into the page
(@file{cmacs/gsurf/cmacs-gsurf-lrg.c}): pointer over the window body, and,
while the page holds focus, the keyboard --- Escape always returns control
to Emacs.  @code{cmacs-gsurf-focus-page} or a click focuses the page;
@code{cmacs-gsurf-release-focus}, Escape, or switching windows release it.
@end table

The web engine for the @option{--lrg} path is selected at build time: today
it is offscreen WebKitGTK (reusing the same @code{webkit2gtk-4.1} the pgtk
embed links --- two WebKit libraries cannot coexist in one process), with
WPE WebKit reserved as a future fully-GTK-free engine.  Only @strong{2D}
render mode is implemented (a flat textured quad); under @command{emacs
--lrg=3d} the same flat texture is captured onto the 3D panel automatically.
@code{cmacs-gsurf-lrg-supported-p} reports whether this backend was built.

A standalone @command{gsurf --lrg[=2d]} (the @file{deps/gsurf} binary) is a
full browser in its own raylib window with an address/progress/hover chrome,
sharing the same backend code.

@section Downloads

Downloads auto-save into @code{cmacs-gsurf-download-directory} (default
@file{~/Downloads}), de-duplicating colliding names.

@deffn Command cmacs-gsurf-downloads
Open the @code{*gsurf-downloads*} manager (a @code{tabulated-list} with
live progress): @kbd{g} refresh, @kbd{c} cancel, @kbd{RET}/@kbd{o} open
the file, @kbd{&} open externally, @kbd{d} remove the row.
@end deffn

@deffn Command cmacs-gsurf-download-cancel id
Cancel the in-flight download with integer @var{id}.
@end deffn

Downloads are process-wide and outlive the buffer that started them.

@section Permissions

Permission requests (geolocation, notifications, camera/mic, clipboard,
@dots{}) are answered without blocking from a per-origin policy table.
An origin with no saved policy is denied, and
@code{cmacs-gsurf-permission-request-functions} fires.

@deffn Command cmacs-gsurf-allow-origin origin type
@deffnx Command cmacs-gsurf-deny-origin origin type
Record a permission decision for @var{origin}
(@samp{scheme://host:port}) and @var{type} (@samp{geolocation},
@samp{notification}, @samp{media}, @samp{clipboard}, @samp{device-info},
@samp{pointer-lock}).  Updates @code{cmacs-gsurf-permission-policy} for
the session; customize that variable to persist it.
@end deffn

@section Snapshot, print and pipe

@deffn Command cmacs-gsurf-snapshot buffer file &optional callback full-page
Save a PNG snapshot of @var{buffer}'s view to @var{file} (the whole
document when @var{full-page}, else the visible region).  Asynchronous:
@var{callback} (or nil) receives the path on success, nil on failure.
@end deffn

@deffn Command cmacs-gsurf-print-to-pdf buffer file &optional callback
Print @var{buffer}'s page to a PDF at @var{file} with no dialog
(asynchronous).
@end deffn

@deffn Command cmacs-gsurf-pipe command &optional html
Pipe the current page's text (HTML with a prefix argument) through the
shell @var{command} into @code{*gsurf-pipe*}.  Bound to @kbd{|}.
@end deffn

@deffn Command cmacs-gsurf-play-external &optional url
Open @var{url} (default: the current page URL) in
@code{cmacs-gsurf-external-player} (default @command{mpv}).  Bound to
@kbd{&}.
@end deffn

@section AI: summarize and ask

With cmacs-ai configured, these read the page text (no page focus needed)
and answer in an Org side window.

@deffn Command cmacs-gsurf-summarize
Summarize the current page (@kbd{C-c C-s}).
@end deffn

@deffn Command cmacs-gsurf-ask question
Answer @var{question} about the current page (@kbd{C-c C-a}).
@end deffn

Page text is truncated to @code{cmacs-gsurf-ai-max-chars}.  Over MCP,
compose @code{gsurf_extract_text} with the @code{ai_*} tools instead.

The new MCP tools are @code{gsurf_download_list},
@code{gsurf_download_cancel}, @code{gsurf_snapshot},
@code{gsurf_print_pdf} and @code{gsurf_permission_policy}.  Browser
activity is also emitted as podomation events (@code{on_gsurf_navigate},
@code{on_gsurf_download}, @code{on_gsurf_permission},
@code{on_gsurf_crash}).

@section Native inspector

@deffn Command cmacs-gsurf-inspect
Open @code{*gsurf-dom*}, a navigable DOM tree of the live page
(@kbd{C-c C-i}).  The walk stamps a @code{data-cmacs-node-id} on each
element.  On a node: @kbd{h}/@kbd{RET} outline it in the page and scroll
to it, @kbd{c} show its computed style in @code{*gsurf-css*}, @kbd{g}
re-walk.  Depth is @code{cmacs-gsurf-inspector-depth}.
@end deffn

@deffn Command cmacs-gsurf-console
Open @code{*gsurf-console*} (@kbd{C-c C-l}): streams
@code{console.log/warn/error} from the page (through the JS bridge below)
and evaluates a JS expression with @kbd{e}.
@end deffn

@section Bookmarks

@deffn Command cmacs-gsurf-bookmark-add url title tags
Save a tagged bookmark (@kbd{m}; defaults from the current page) to
@code{cmacs-gsurf-bookmarks-file}.
@end deffn

@deffn Command cmacs-gsurf-bookmarks
Open @code{*gsurf-bookmarks*} (@kbd{C-c C-b}), a @code{tabulated-list}:
@kbd{RET}/@kbd{o} open in gsurf, @kbd{d} delete, @kbd{e} edit tags,
@kbd{/} filter by tag, @kbd{a} add.
@end deffn

@section JS to Emacs bridge

The @code{js_bridge} gsurf module (in @file{cmacs/gsurf/modules/}, so
gsurf itself stays elisp-free) exposes a page-to-Emacs @emph{data}
channel.  Page or user-script JS calls
@code{window.cmacs.send("channel", @{any: "json"@})} and the C host bridge
resolves the originating buffer and runs the abnormal hook
@code{cmacs-gsurf-js-message-functions} with @code{(BUFFER CHANNEL
PAYLOAD)}.  The payload is decoded JSON --- data only, never evaluated.
The console capture rides on this channel; user scripts injected with
@code{cmacs-gsurf-add-user-script} can define their own channels.

@section gsurf-lite

@cindex gsurf-lite
@cindex eww
@code{gsurf-lite} renders a page with gsurf/WebKit @emph{offscreen}
(its JavaScript runs, and logins/sessions persist in WebKit's shared
cookie context) and dumps the post-JS DOM into a @emph{real Emacs text
buffer} rendered with @code{shr} --- like @code{eww}, but the HTML is
WebKit's live @code{document.documentElement.outerHTML} instead of a raw
@code{url-retrieve} fetch.  The result is a normal, navigable,
copy/search/yank-able buffer that works on JavaScript-only / single-page
sites and stays logged in.

@deffn Command cmacs-gsurf-lite &optional url
Open @var{url} (prompted if nil) as eww-style text rendered from an
offscreen gsurf view.  Links navigate the hidden view in place; the
load-finish hook re-extracts and re-renders.
@end deffn

A hidden @code{GsurfView} is attached to the lite buffer headless: its
WebKit widget is parented into the frame's @code{GtkFixed} at a full
viewport size but parked far below the visible area (@code{y=100000}),
where GTK clips it away.  It is realized -- so WebKit spawns its web
process and runs JavaScript with a real viewport -- yet never visible,
and never resized to a window rectangle (@code{cmacs-gsurf-place} /
@code{-hide} are no-ops for it, and @code{cmacs-gsurf-offscreen-p}
reports it), so the live page can never cover the rendered text.  (A
pure @code{GtkOffscreenWindow} does @emph{not} start the web process --
the JavaScript callback never fires -- which is why this parked-in-frame
approach is used.)  @kbd{RET} follows the link at point (real links
navigate the hidden view; SPA/@code{onclick} links are clicked by a
@code{data-cmlite-id} stamped during extraction).  @kbd{g} reloads,
@kbd{B}/@kbd{F} go back/forward in the hidden view's history, @kbd{o}
opens a URL, and @kbd{&} hands the page off to a full live
@code{cmacs-gsurf} buffer.  The MCP tools @code{gsurf_lite_open} and
@code{gsurf_extract_text} expose this to external agents and cmacs-ai.

Forms and logins work.  Form controls render as editable items
(@code{Username: [ ]}, @code{Password: [ ]}, @code{[ ] remember},
@code{[ Log In ]}); move between them with @kbd{TAB} / @kbd{S-TAB}.
Text, password and textarea fields are editable @emph{inline} -- just
type into the box; characters self-insert in place, @kbd{DEL} erases,
and passwords echo as bullets, with edits pushed into the live DOM on a
short idle debounce (and right before any submit/click).  Or press
@kbd{RET} on a field to read the value in the minibuffer instead
(passwords without echo); the value is never written into the extracted
HTML either way.  Toggle a
checkbox or click a button with @kbd{RET}, and submit with @kbd{RET} on
the submit button or @kbd{C-c C-c}.  Filling pushes the value into the
live DOM via the native value setter plus @code{input}/@code{change}
events (React-safe), and submitting clicks the real control, so
cookies, CSRF tokens, hidden fields and the session ride along exactly
as a browser would send them -- a real login works from the lite
buffer.  (File-upload inputs are out of scope.)  Many sign-in / SPA
pages render their form only after the load-finished event, so
gsurf-lite re-extracts the DOM at each delay in
@code{cmacs-gsurf-lite-resettle-times} (default 0.6/1.6/3.0@tie{}s);
raise those for very slow pages.

A click or submit (e.g.@: @emph{Log In}) often kicks off work that
finishes later than one settle and may navigate @emph{client-side} (a
@code{pushState} route change, a @code{window.location} bounce, or a
fetch-then-update), firing the load event late or not at all.  After any
click/submit gsurf-lite therefore re-extracts at each delay in
@code{cmacs-gsurf-lite-action-watch-times}
(default 0.3/0.8/1.6/3.0/4.5/6.5@tie{}s) and follows the page wherever it
lands, so the buffer updates to the post-login page even when no load
event fires.  Lengthen the tail for very slow sign-in flows.

@section Elisp API

Primitives operate on a browser buffer: @code{cmacs-gsurf-load-uri},
@code{cmacs-gsurf-reload}, @code{cmacs-gsurf-stop},
@code{cmacs-gsurf-back}, @code{cmacs-gsurf-forward},
@code{cmacs-gsurf-get-uri}, @code{cmacs-gsurf-get-title},
@code{cmacs-gsurf-get-progress}, @code{cmacs-gsurf-set-zoom},
@code{cmacs-gsurf-get-zoom}, @code{cmacs-gsurf-run-javascript},
@code{cmacs-gsurf-find}, @code{cmacs-gsurf-find-next}, and the focus
commands @code{cmacs-gsurf-focus-page} /
@code{cmacs-gsurf-release-focus} / @code{cmacs-gsurf-page-focused-p}.

The view's URI/title/load signals run the abnormal hooks
@code{cmacs-gsurf-uri-changed-functions} @code{(BUFFER URI)},
@code{cmacs-gsurf-title-changed-functions} @code{(BUFFER TITLE)}, and
@code{cmacs-gsurf-load-changed-functions} @code{(BUFFER EVENT)} where
@var{event} is @code{started}, @code{committed} or @code{finished}.

@section Configuration

cmacs-gsurf does @emph{not} read gsurf's own
@file{~/.config/gsurf/config.yaml} (or @file{config.c}) by default; it
is configured from Emacs and tells gsurf to ignore YAML entirely
(@code{cmacs-gsurf-ignore-yaml}, default @code{t}).  Because gsurf
modules are disabled unless a config enables them, this is also how the
vim @code{modal} module (@kbd{hjkl} scroll, @kbd{f} link hints) is
turned on.

The primary knob is @code{cmacs-gsurf-modules}, an alist of
@code{(MODULE-NAME . PLIST)} pushed onto the gsurf config through its
programmatic C-config setters (@code{gsurf_config_set_module_enabled} /
@code{_set_module_option}) before the modules load --- no YAML file.
@code{:enabled BOOL} toggles a module (precedence over any YAML); any
other @code{:KEY VALUE} is a module option.  The default enables the
windowless essentials @code{modal}, @code{search_engines},
@code{history}:

@example
(setq cmacs-gsurf-modules
      '(("modal"          :enabled t :scroll_step 100)
        ("search_engines" :enabled t)
        ("history"        :enabled t)
        ("adblock"        :enabled t)))
@end example

To also read gsurf's own files set @code{cmacs-gsurf-load-user-config}
to @code{t}; to load a specific file set @code{cmacs-gsurf-config-file}
(YAML) or @code{cmacs-gsurf-config-c-file} (a crispy-compiled C config,
e.g. @file{~/.config/cmacs/init.c}, whose @code{gsurf_config_init()} may
call any @code{gsurf_config_*} setter).  Primitives:
@code{cmacs-gsurf-config-set-module-enabled} / @code{-set-module-option}
/ @code{-set-homepage} / @code{cmacs-gsurf-set-ignore-yaml}, and the
loaders @code{cmacs-gsurf-load-config-data} / @code{-file} /
@code{-c-file}; re-apply with @code{M-x cmacs-gsurf-reload-config}.

@section Modules

gsurf modules load from these directories (first match by filename
wins, like @env{PATH}): @env{CMACS_GSURF_MODULE_DIR} (exported by
@command{just run}), @env{GSURF_MODULE_PATH},
@file{cmacs/gsurf/modules/} (cmacs custom modules),
@file{deps/gsurf/build/release/modules/} (the stock modules), and the
installed @file{$libdir/cmacs/gsurf/modules/}.  Which modules are
@emph{enabled} comes from the config (see above), not the search path.
Inspect and toggle at runtime with @code{cmacs-gsurf-modules-list} and
@code{cmacs-gsurf-module-set-enabled}.

@section cmacsgi / introspection

@code{init_cmacs_gsurf} prepends the bundled @file{Gsurf-0.1.typelib}
directory to the GObject-Introspection search path, so
@code{(gi-require "Gsurf" "0.1")} and the Bacon @code{cmacsgi} builtin
work with no @env{GI_TYPELIB_PATH} setup.

@section MCP and cmacs-ai

With @option{--with-cmacs-mcp}, the tools @code{gsurf_open},
@code{gsurf_navigate}, @code{gsurf_back}, @code{gsurf_forward},
@code{gsurf_reload}, @code{gsurf_stop}, @code{gsurf_get_uri},
@code{gsurf_get_title}, @code{gsurf_current}, @code{gsurf_list},
@code{gsurf_eval_js}, @code{gsurf_set_zoom},
@code{gsurf_modules_list}, @code{gsurf_lite_open} (offscreen render to a
text buffer) and @code{gsurf_extract_text} (read a lite buffer's text)
are exposed.  @code{^gsurf_} is in the default
@code{cmacs-ai-mcp-bridge-allowlist}, so cmacs-ai chat buffers can drive
the browser out of the box.

@section Building

Pass @option{--with-cmacs-gsurf} (alias @option{--with-gsurf}) to
@command{configure}; requires @option{--with-cmacs-glib} and
@option{--with-pgtk}.  gsurf is built @code{STATIC=1 BUILD_GIR=1
GTK_BACKEND=gtk3} and @file{libgsurf.a} is whole-archived into temacs;
because it merges crispy + yaml-glib objects it is run through the
dedup machinery (@code{_gsurf_strip}), never @option{-z muldefs}.  It
reuses the same WebKitGTK/GTK3 stack as @option{--with-xwidgets}, so no
new system packages are needed.

@strong{Signal note.}  WebKit's JavaScriptCore pauses threads for GC
using a POSIX signal that defaults to @code{SIGUSR1} (signal@tie{}10).
That is the same signal podomation's @code{engine} source
(@code{on_sigusr1}), libreclaw's thread-dump, and Emacs's @code{[sigusr1]}
use, so JSC steals it.  cmacs moves JSC to a free real-time signal
(@math{40 = @code{SIGRTMIN}+5} on Linux) in C at the top of @code{main()}
via JSC's private @code{JSConfigureSignalForGC()}, so both @code{./src/emacs}
and @code{just run} are handled automatically.  @code{SIGUSR2}/@code{SIGHUP}
are unaffected.  cmacs deliberately does @emph{not} set the
@code{JSC_SIGNAL_FOR_GC} environment variable: it works, but JSC's option
parser also scans every @code{JSC_*} var and rejects it with @samp{ERROR:
invalid option: JSC_SIGNAL_FOR_GC=40}.  If you see that error, unset the
variable in your environment.

@c ===================================================================
@node CMacs CAD
@chapter CMacs CAD

@code{cmacs-cad} (@option{--with-cmacs-cad}) turns cmacs into a
parametric CAD environment.  A part is a text file in one of two
languages, both evaluating through cad-glib (Manifold mesh CSG +
OpenCASCADE B-rep + SolveSpace constraint sketches) into one document
model: @file{.cad} is the s-expression DSL
(@code{cmacs-cad-mode}); @file{.ccad} is crispy --- ordinary C with a
@code{main()} calling the flat @code{cad_*} API
(@code{cmacs-cad-crispy-mode}).

@example
(defparam thickness 4.0 :min 2 :max 10)
(defpart bracket
  (difference
    (box 40 20 thickness)
    (translate (10 10 -1) (cylinder :r 2.5 :h (+ thickness 2)))))
@end example

Geometry is exact B-rep by default (@code{:backend mesh} opts into
Manifold), parameters re-bind without editing source, and every
feature carries its source span, so viewers can map a clicked face
back to the expression that made it.  Parts render in scenes as
libregnum @code{CAD_PART} nodes.

Both modes share one evaluation loop: @kbd{C-c C-c} (or saving, when
@code{cmacs-cad-eval-on-save}) re-evaluates the buffer asynchronously
and single-flight; failures land in Flymake at the reported source
span.  @kbd{C-c C-p} echoes parameters, @kbd{C-c C-i} mass properties
(volume, area, watertightness), @kbd{C-c C-e} exports
STL/OBJ/STEP/IGES.  Completion and ElDoc come from the live modeling
vocabulary (@code{cmacs-cad-dsl-symbols}).

The Lisp surface (errors signal @code{cmacs-cad-error} with the
evaluator's message and location): @code{cmacs-cad-doc-open},
@code{cmacs-cad-set-source}, @code{cmacs-cad-eval},
@code{cmacs-cad-eval-async}, @code{cmacs-cad-params},
@code{cmacs-cad-part-names}, @code{cmacs-cad-feature-tree},
@code{cmacs-cad-inspect} (volume, area, bbox, center of mass,
watertightness, triangle count), @code{cmacs-cad-section} (plane to a
list of line segments) and @code{cmacs-cad-export}.

@kbd{C-c C-M-i} shows the full mass properties in a help buffer;
@kbd{C-c C-m} (@code{cmacs-cad-measure}) reports the distance and
per-axis delta between two points; @kbd{C-c C-s}
(@code{cmacs-cad-section-at}) slices the part with an X/Y/Z plane and
reports the section's segment count and perimeter.  Visiting an
@file{.stl}, @file{.obj}, @file{.step}, @file{.iges} or @file{.3mf} file
enters @code{cmacs-cad-model-mode}, a print-prep workbench: a narrow
settings sidebar and a libregnum viewport.  The geometry imports through
cad-glib (binary STL and B-rep STEP alike) and renders as a CAD_PART,
turned Z-up to Y-up so it stands upright
(@code{cmacs-cad-model-orient-z-up}) on a configurable build-plate plane
(@code{cmacs-cad-model-bed-x}/@code{-bed-y}).  The sidebar lists the
model's facts and the print settings as @emph{clickable values} (bed
size, layer and first-layer height, infill, walls, supports with
grid/snug/organic style, brim, slicer override) --- click one to change
it and it applies at once.  @kbd{G} (@code{cmacs-cad-model-export-gcode})
slices the model with those settings and opens the toolpath viewer,
always in the file's @emph{original} orientation (the upright display
turn never reaches the exported geometry).  The viewer holds one or more
parts: @kbd{i}, the @samp{Add STL@dots{}} button, right-click
@samp{Insert}, dragging a file onto the viewport, or just opening another
model file all add it @emph{on} the plate and off to one side (never
under), beside the existing parts; @kbd{x}/@key{DEL} delete the selected
one (all work from either pane).  @kbd{b}/@kbd{B} set/toggle the bed.
Opening a
@file{.gcode} file enters @code{cmacs-cad-gcode-mode}: the raw text
stays in the buffer and the toolpath renders as a 3-D extruded-bead mesh
through the libregnum editor (via cad-glib's OBJ import + the CAD_PART
bake path), with @kbd{]}/@kbd{[} to scrub the top visible layer and the
slicer-footer time/filament estimates in the mode-line.

Visiting a @file{.cad}/@file{.ccad} file automatically opens the
workbench (set @code{cmacs-cad-auto-workbench} to nil for bare text):
the libregnum-rendered 3-D view is the primary way to see a part, with
the text buffer as its source.  @kbd{C-c C-v} (@code{cmacs-cad-workbench})
opens or re-focuses it: the source buffer and a 3D viewport side by side, with a feature-tree panel (RET jumps to and
pulses the source span that made the feature) and an interactive CAD
Parameters section in the inspector --- each @code{defparam} gets a
field plus @kbd{-}/@kbd{+} nudge buttons that set a @code{cad:} override
on the node and re-bake live (parameters re-bind without editing
source), undoable through the editor's own undo stack and clamped to the
param's declared range.  Re-evaluating the source pushes the (possibly
unsaved) buffer text into the viewport and re-bakes the geometry, so
edits show immediately without saving; untouched params re-seed to the
new source default while nudged params keep their value.  The editor's CAD palette section
offers @emph{New Part}, @emph{Place Part} and @emph{Import STL/STEP} ---
the last wraps an external @file{.stl}/@file{.obj}/@file{.step}/@file{.iges}
file in a generated @code{(import "@dots{}")} part so imported geometry
renders through the same @code{CAD_PART} path and can be drilled,
combined or measured.  The @code{import} form (and its crispy twin
@code{cad_import}) resolves relative paths against the part file's own
directory.

Real-world STL/OBJ files are frequently not 2-manifold/watertight (open
shells, T-junctions) yet are printable and viewable.  @code{import} tries a
true manifold solid (full CSG), retries after a tolerance vertex-merge, and
if the mesh is still non-manifold falls back to a @emph{display-only} mesh
solid (reported @code{watertight: no}) so the geometry is shown as FreeCAD
shows it, rather than failing with ``could not import''.  A display-only
solid supports tessellation, bounding box, volume/area estimates and rigid
transforms; CSG/boolean, hull and fillet/chamfer/shell/offset on it return
@code{CAD_ERROR_NEEDS_BREP}.

@code{cmacs-cad-slice-part} exports the current part to STL and slices it
to G-code with an external slicer; @code{cmacs-cad-slicer-settings} edits
the common parameters.  The default backend is PrusaSlicer, found in this
order (first wins): a native @code{prusa-slicer} binary, then the
@code{com.prusa3d.PrusaSlicer} flatpak in the system then the per-user
(@code{flatpak --user}) installation --- overridable with
@code{cmacs-cad-slicer-prusa-program}.  It merges a small overrides ini
(layer and first-layer height, infill, perimeters, supports with a
grid/snug/organic @code{support_material_style}, brim) on top of your
base profile so only the parameters you set change.  Slicing is async
with a job buffer; estimates come from the G-code footer; staging stays
under @file{$HOME} for the flatpak.  @code{cmacs-cad-printer-upload} sends the
result to a networked printer (OctoPrint, Moonraker, PrusaLink, or a
local @code{gcode} directory sink) over @code{curl}; the API key comes
from auth-source and is fed via stdin so it never appears in the argv.
@code{cmacs-cad-printer-start} is the only path that begins a print and
requires an explicit confirmation (interactive @code{yes-or-no-p} or a
programmatic @code{:confirm t}).

With @option{--with-cmacs-mcp}, a "vibe CAD" tool set lets an AI agent
model conversationally: the inner loop is @code{cad_set_source} /
@code{cad_patch_source} (edit, re-evaluate, report), surrounded by
@code{cad_open}, @code{cad_inspect}, @code{cad_feature_tree},
@code{cad_section}, @code{cad_export}, @code{cad_snapshot} (inline PNG),
@code{cad_slice}, @code{cad_printers} and @code{cad_print} (which starts a
print only when both @code{start} and @code{confirm} are set).  Org Babel
@code{cad} (s-expression) and @code{ccad} (crispy) source blocks execute
a part body: no output yields a mass-property table, @code{:file} inlines
a snapshot, @code{:export} writes an artifact, and @code{:var} supplies
parameter overrides.

@kbd{C-c C-k} (@code{cmacs-cad-sketch}) opens an interactive 2D
constraint sketcher rendered as an SVG canvas and driven by cad-glib's
SolveSpace solver: place points (click or @kbd{p}), connect lines
(@kbd{l}) and circles (@kbd{c}), select entities and apply constraints
(@kbd{H}orizontal, @kbd{V}ertical, @kbd{P}arallel, perpe@kbd{N}dicular,
@kbd{E}qual, @kbd{T}angent, @kbd{C}oincident, @kbd{M}idpoint, @kbd{F}ixed;
@kbd{D}/@kbd{R}/@kbd{A} add a dimension).  The header-line shows the
remaining degrees of freedom (green 0 / yellow >0 / red over-constrained);
@kbd{C-c C-c} writes a @code{(defsketch @dots{})} form back into the part
(or, from a @file{.ccad} buffer, to the kill-ring).

@code{cmacs-cad-new-part} / @code{cmacs-cad-new-project} scaffold parts
and projects; @code{cmacs-cad-part-browser} lists a project's parts with
content-hashed thumbnails; and @code{cmacs-cad-diff} compares two git
revisions of a part by parameter changes and feature-tree structure.

@section Sweep, loft, holes, and assemblies

Beyond extrude/revolve the DSL adds @code{(sweep SKETCH PATH@dots{})}
(sweep a profile along a 3D polyline), @code{(loft (SK z) @dots{} :ruled #f)}
(loft through section sketches), and @code{(hole SOLID :face (top) :dia D
:depth H)}.  A hole's face is named by a @emph{topological selector}
(@code{(top)}, @code{(cyl-largest)}, @code{(face-near x y z)}, @dots{})
that re-resolves from geometry on each recompute, so the hole follows its
face when a parameter changes.

A @code{(defassembly NAME @dots{})} form places named part instances and
relates them with mates (coincident/concentric/distance/rigid/@dots{})
and drivable joints (revolute/slider/@dots{}); a deterministic
composition solver grounds the @code{:grounded} instances and places the
rest.  From a part buffer the @kbd{C-c C-a} family inspects and drives
it: @kbd{C-c C-a b} bill of materials, @kbd{C-c C-a i} interference
report, @kbd{C-c C-a s} state/DOF, @kbd{C-c C-a j} drive a joint, @kbd{C-c
C-a J} animate a joint, @kbd{C-c C-a a} open the 3-D workbench.  Driving a
joint re-solves the @emph{live} assembly without re-evaluating the
source, so the mechanism does not reset.  The @code{cmacs-cad-assembly-*}
primitives and the @code{cad_assembly_info} / @code{cad_assembly_bom} /
@code{cad_assembly_interference} / @code{cad_assembly_set_joint} MCP tools
expose the same surface; an Org Babel @code{cad} block with @code{:bom}
emits the bill of materials as a table.

See @file{doc_org/cmacs/cmacs-cad.org} for the living document.

@c ===================================================================
@node CMacs Screensaver
@chapter CMacs Screensaver: libregnum Wallpaper, Lock Screen & Buffer

The @code{cmacs-screensaver} subsystem renders the GPU screensavers from the
@file{deps/screensavers} dependency (@code{blackhole}, @code{singularity},
@code{helios} --- libregnum game modules) and shows them as an animated
@strong{gowl wallpaper}, an animated @strong{lock-screen background}, or
@strong{in a buffer}.  Enable with @option{--with-cmacs-screensaver} (needs
@option{--with-cmacs-libregnum}; wallpaper and lock also need
@option{--with-cmacs-gowl}).  Off by default.

@section Architecture: an out-of-process renderer

The wallpaper and lock sinks render in a dedicated child process,
@command{cmacs-screensaver-render}, @emph{not} on Emacs's main thread (buffer
mode still renders in-process).  This avoids editor lag (the per-frame
@code{glReadPixels} GPU readback no longer blocks redisplay), avoids hanging the
gowl dispatch thread (Emacs holds gowl's lock only for the brief frame push,
never across a render), and removes the in-process raylib(GLX)/gowl(EGL)
context conflict that caused constant @code{EGL_BAD_ACCESS} errors.  A
@emph{process}, not a thread, is required: raylib has one GL context per process
shared by the wallpaper, gnuseye and buffer playback, so only a separate process
gets its own context.

The child renders each @code{(sink,monitor)} target to an FBO, reads it back as
top-down @code{BGRA}, and publishes it into a per-target shared-memory frame
ring (a sealed @code{memfd} passed to Emacs over @code{SCM_RIGHTS}, with a
triple-buffered lock-free seqlock so Emacs always reads the newest complete
frame without tearing).  The control channel is a @code{SOCK_SEQPACKET}
socketpair carrying one JSON message per datagram
(@file{cmacs/screensaver/cmacs-screensaver-proto.h}).  Emacs pushes the
shared-memory slot straight to gowl
(@code{gowl_compositor_push_@{wallpaper,lock@}_frame}); gowl copies it and has
@emph{no} dependency on libregnum/graylib/raylib/GL.  Two @code{nm} build guards
enforce that the Emacs-side screensaver objects link zero @code{lrg_*}/@code{grl_*}
symbols, the child is a standalone libregnum host with no Emacs symbols, and gowl
stays libregnum-free (@file{cmacs/screensaver/render/tests/guard-symbols.sh}).

Emacs supervises the child: lazy spawn, crash-restart with backoff (giving up
after a crash loop and revealing the static wallpaper), a heartbeat watchdog
that restarts a wedged child, and @code{PR_SET_PDEATHSIG} so the child dies with
Emacs.  A hung child can never hang Emacs --- every main-thread interaction is
non-blocking, except a bounded wait at start
(@code{cmacs-screensaver-start-timeout}) so a bad module's error is reported
synchronously.

@section Named configurations

Screensavers are chosen from @code{cmacs-screensaver-configs}, an alist of
@code{(NAME . (:module M :args ARGS))}.  @code{:module} is a key of
@code{cmacs-screensaver-modules-alist} (symbol @result{} @file{.so} base name)
or an absolute path; @code{:args} is a list of raw CLI argument strings passed
verbatim to the module (the same flags its standalone binary takes).  Keep as
many variants of a module as you like and pick between them.

@example
(setq cmacs-screensaver-configs
      '((default        . (:module blackhole :args nil))
        (blackhole-cool . (:module blackhole :args ("--profile" "cool" "--orbit-radius" "60" "--infall" "2")))
        (helios-trinary . (:module helios    :args ("--stars" "3")))))
@end example

Use the module's own CLI flags (see the standalone saver's @code{--help});
a module that gets an unknown flag or a missing value (e.g. @code{--infall}
takes an integer) warns and falls back to its defaults.

Knobs: @code{cmacs-screensaver-default-config}, @code{cmacs-screensaver-fps}
(default 30), @code{cmacs-screensaver-pause-when-covered} (default @code{t};
skips a monitor hidden by a fullscreen window), and
@code{cmacs-screensaver-lock-config} (below).

@section Commands

@table @code
@item M-x cmacs-screensaver-play
Pick a config and play it in a buffer.
@item M-x cmacs-screensaver-set-wallpaper
Pick a config and set it as the animated gowl wallpaper.
@item M-x cmacs-screensaver-stop-wallpaper
Stop the animated wallpaper, restoring the static one.
@item M-x cmacs-screensaver-status
Echo / return the render child's status plist (@code{:running :pid :fps
:paused :gave-up :targets :wallpaper :lock :last-error}).
@item M-x cmacs-screensaver-restart
Kill and respawn the render child (recovery).
@item M-x cmacs-screensaver-pause / -resume
Pause/resume rendering (the child stops drawing; the GPU idles).
@item M-x cmacs-screensaver-set-fps
Change the target frame rate (1--240) live.
@end table

The control commands (@code{status}, @code{restart}, @code{pause},
@code{resume}, @code{set-fps}) are exposed on every cmacs surface: Elisp,
@code{cmacsgi gowl screensaver-*}, @code{emacsctl compositor screensaver-*}, the
D-Bus @code{org.cmacs.Editor1.Compositor} interface, and MCP
(@code{screensaver_status}/@code{_restart}/@code{_pause}/@code{_resume}/@code{_set_fps}).

@section Lock screen

Set @code{cmacs-screensaver-lock-config} to a config name and ordinary
@code{gowl-lock} shows that screensaver as the lock background, with the
password box and the typing indicator composited on top while the animation
keeps running.  Leave it @code{nil} for the unchanged static lock.  The
animation stops automatically when the session unlocks.

@section Module discovery and install

Modules are searched for in @env{CMACS_SCREENSAVER_MODULE_DIR} (a dev override
that @command{just run}/@command{just gowl} set to
@file{deps/screensavers/build/release}) and then in
@file{$(libdir)/cmacs/screensaver-modules} (where @code{make install} places
them).  An absolute @code{:module} path bypasses discovery.

When gowl is enabled the build also produces the
@command{cmacs-screensaver-render} helper (a standalone GLib/GObject binary that
links libregnum but no Emacs objects).  Emacs finds it via
@env{CMACS_SCREENSAVER_RENDER_BIN} (a dev override @command{just run}/@command{just
gowl} set to @file{src/cmacs-screensaver-render}) then
@file{$(libexecdir)/cmacs/cmacs-screensaver-render} (where @code{make install}
puts it).  It requires @code{memfd_create} (Linux >= 3.17, checked at configure
time) and has its own unit tests + symbol guards under
@file{cmacs/screensaver/render/tests/}.

See @file{doc_org/cmacs/cmacs-screensaver.org} for the living document.

@c ===================================================================
@node CMacs Lrgterm
@chapter CMacs Lrgterm: Independent libregnum/raylib Display Backend

@code{output_lrg} is a third Emacs display backend in cmacs, a peer to the
terminal backend (@code{emacs -nw}) and the graphical @emph{pgtk} backend.
Where pgtk renders through GTK and Cairo, @code{output_lrg} renders the
@emph{entire} Emacs UI --- every glyph, cursor, fringe, region and modeline
--- through the @emph{libregnum} engine and its GObject raylib wrapper
@emph{graylib} (raylib 6.0 on GLFW/X11).

It is opt-in: plain @code{emacs} still starts pgtk.  The backend is selected
only by the @code{--lrg[=MODE]} command-line flag (or by setting
@code{initial-window-system} to @code{lrg}).  raylib owns the OS window and
input loop; Emacs's @code{pselect} loop is unchanged and is woken by a timer
file descriptor.  @code{(window-system)} returns @code{lrg} on these frames.

@example
./src/emacs --lrg          # == --lrg=2d (the default render mode)
./src/emacs --lrg=3d       # reserved -- errors politely (not yet implemented)
@end example

Enable at build time with @code{--with-cmacs-lrgterm} (requires
@code{--with-cmacs-libregnum} and @code{--with-cmacs-glib}; it reuses
libregnum's archive and @code{Lrg*} surface/atlas types).  It is part of
the standard flag set, so @command{just bootstrap} and the container build
include it.

@command{make install} (and the container build) install two extra
application launchers next to the usual @emph{Emacs} / @emph{Emacs
(Client)} ones: @emph{Emacs Libregnum}, which runs @code{emacs --lrg}
(@file{etc/emacs-libregnum.desktop}), and @emph{Emacs Libregnum (Client)},
which runs @code{emacsclient --lrg}
(@file{etc/emacsclient-libregnum.desktop}).  Both set
@code{StartupWMClass=cmacs}.  The plain @emph{Emacs} launchers are
unchanged and still start pgtk.

The client launcher mirrors @file{emacsclient.desktop} (an @code{sh -c}
wrapper: with files it runs @code{emacsclient --lrg "$@@"}, with none
@code{emacsclient --lrg --create-frame} so a bare icon click opens a frame
instead of erroring with ``file name or argument required'').  It
deliberately does @emph{not} pass an empty @option{--alternate-editor=} the
way the pgtk client does: that would make @command{emacsclient} auto-start a
generic @code{emacs --daemon} (without @option{--lrg}).  A daemon @emph{can}
host the lrg window on demand now (the @code{-lrg} request calls
@code{cmacs-lrg-attach}; see below), but only when it was started inside a
graphical session---so the launcher relies instead on @option{--lrg}'s
built-in fallback to @code{emacs --lrg}, a dedicated process that owns the
window from startup and serves later clients.

@command{emacsclient} accepts @option{--lrg[=MODE]} (shown in both
@code{emacsclient --help} and @code{emacs --help}).  Because the libregnum
backend is one window per process, @option{--lrg} never opens a @emph{second}
raylib window.  It sends a @code{-lrg} request and the server either reuses
and raises its existing @code{output_lrg} frame, or---if it has none
yet---@emph{creates one on demand} in the server process via
@code{cmacs-lrg-attach} (instead of falling through to a pgtk frame).  That is
how lrg attaches to a daemon: the daemon must run inside a graphical session
(@env{DISPLAY}/@env{WAYLAND_DISPLAY} set in its own environment), since GLFW
cannot create a window otherwise; @code{cmacs-lrg-attach} errors cleanly on a
headless daemon.  When no server is reachable, @option{--lrg} defaults
@option{--alternate-editor} to @code{emacs --lrg}.  Thus
@code{emacsclient --lrg file} opens @var{file} in a running libregnum Emacs,
spins up the lrg window inside a running daemon, or launches a fresh one.

@code{M-x cmacs-lrg-attach} (@code{cmacs-lrg-attach &optional @var{mode}})
opens the lrg window in the @emph{current} process on demand, raising the
existing lrg frame if there is one.  @var{mode} is @code{"2d"} (default),
@code{"3d"} or @code{"3dvr"}.

@section Input parity with pgtk

The event loop translates raylib/GLFW input into the same events pgtk
produces.  Modifiers are @emph{event-carried and authoritative}: graylib
chains onto GLFW's key/button/focus callbacks and records the modifier
mask GLFW computes from the window system's own tracking (xkb state on
Wayland) --- the source GTK reads --- so a modifier release swallowed by a
compositor global shortcut (GNOME's @kbd{Super+Shift+S} screenshot) can
no longer latch a phantom @kbd{s-} prefix; the next input event
self-corrects, focus change or not (a polled fallback with
focus-generation staleness covers the pre-first-event window).  Special
keys carry @kbd{S-} like pgtk (@kbd{S-<tab>}, @kbd{C-S-a}); events carry
real monotonic timestamps (double/triple-click counting works);
side/extra mouse buttons map to @kbd{mouse-8}/@kbd{mouse-9}; horizontal
wheel emits @code{HORIZ_WHEEL_EVENT} with pgtk's direction convention.
@code{M-x cmacs-lrgterm-input-state} echoes the live tracker for
diagnosis; check @code{:armed} first --- @code{t} means modifiers are
event-carried, @code{nil} means the polled fallback is in effect ---
and @code{:serial} should climb as you type.

@section Architecture

A strict four-layer stack; the backend calls only graylib / libregnum
GObject APIs, never raylib directly.  Font @emph{rasterisation} stays
Emacs-side (it needs Emacs font objects); only raw RGBA buffers cross into
libregnum, which therefore stays Cairo/FreeType-free and headless-testable.

@table @asis
@item Layer 1 --- graylib
@code{Grl*} raylib wrappers (@code{Graylib-1.typelib}): @code{GrlWindow},
@code{GrlTexture}, drawing, clipboard, input, monitor queries.
@item Layer 2 --- libregnum @code{src/term/}
GObject-Introspectable @code{Lrg*} types (@code{Libregnum-1.typelib}):
@code{LrgGlyphAtlas} (GPU glyph cache), the abstract @code{LrgFrameSurface}
(draw/present/projection facade --- the seam that makes 3D/VR additive) and
its concrete @code{Lrg2DSurface}, the @code{LrgTextRenderer} interface, the
@code{LrgRenderMode} enum, and boxed @code{LrgGlyphKey}/@code{LrgGlyphMetrics}.
@item Layer 3 --- @code{cmacs/lrgterm/}
Plain Emacs C glue (cannot be GObject): the @code{struct terminal} hooks, the
redisplay interface, the font-driver blit, and the event loop.
@item Layer 4 --- Elisp + MCP + GI
@code{lisp/term/lrg-win.el}, @code{cmacs/mcp/cmacs-mcp-tools-lrgterm.c}, and
GObject-Introspection access to the Layer-2 types.
@end table

@section Frame-parameter parity with pgtk

@code{lrg-create-frame} defaults @code{menu-bar-lines}, @code{tab-bar-lines}
and @code{tool-bar-lines} to @emph{0} (a number, like every other GUI
backend), not the unset @code{nil}.  This is load-bearing: generic window
code does arithmetic on them---e.g.@: @code{window-deletable-p} evaluates
@code{(> (frame-parameter f 'tab-bar-lines) 0)}---and a @code{nil} there
signals @code{(wrong-type-argument number-or-marker-p nil)}, which aborts
@code{window--delete} so that @code{quit-window} / @code{quit-windows-on}
silently fail to close a window.  The visible symptom was that every bottom
popup (which-key, transient, @code{*Completions*}, @code{*Help*}) stayed open
under @code{--lrg} and had to be closed by hand, while pgtk (which sets the
same defaults) worked.  lrg draws none of these bars, so it forces @code{0}
rather than following @code{menu-bar-mode}/@code{tab-bar-mode}/@code{tool-bar-mode}
the way pgtk does, consistent with @code{cmacs-lrg-frame-geometry} reporting
all three bar sizes as 0.

@section Text rendering

lrg reuses Emacs's stock Cairo FreeType/HarfBuzz drivers (@code{ftcrfont} /
@code{ftcrhbfont}) unchanged for font discovery, opening, metrics and
shaping; only the final blit is redirected.  The redisplay interface calls
@code{lrg_font_draw_glyph_string}, which rasterises each glyph via the font's
@code{cr_scaled_font} into an alpha-coverage RGBA mask, uploads it to the
per-display @code{LrgGlyphAtlas} on a cache miss, and paints it as a textured
quad tinted by the face foreground.  Full font-lock colour, the region, the
modeline (with its 3D box), text decorations (underline, overline,
strike-through, box), the graphical fringes (continuation/truncation arrows)
and the blinking cursor are all drawn.  Colour names resolve through pango's
X11 @file{rgb.txt} table, so the full Emacs default-face palette renders.

The OS window is created with a transparent (32-bit ARGB) framebuffer, so the
@code{alpha-background} frame parameter (Emacs 29's translucent-background
feature) is honored: the frame is cleared and every face background is filled
at that alpha (with a source/replace blend so non-default backgrounds
composite over the desktop, mirroring pgtk's @code{CAIRO_OPERATOR_SOURCE}),
while text and other foreground stay opaque.  Thus
@code{(set-frame-parameter nil 'alpha-background 85)} --- or
@code{(add-to-list 'default-frame-alist '(alpha-background . 85))} --- lets the
wallpaper show through, as under pgtk (needs a compositing display server).

Inline image glyphs (PNG, XPM, JPEG, @dots{}) are also drawn.  In this Cairo
build the loaded pixels live in the image's @code{cr_data} Cairo pattern; the
backend converts that surface to a straight-RGBA @code{GrlTexture} (cached as
Cairo user-data so it is freed with the image) and blits it with
@code{lrg_frame_surface_draw_texture_region}, honouring @code{:scale} /
@code{:width} and slices --- so the Doom dashboard banner and @file{README.org}
image links display.

@section Rendering and presentation model

lrg presents through a double-buffered swap of the default framebuffer (the
rlgl FBO bug rules out a retained off-screen surface).  Because each swap
shows a different buffer, lrg cannot honour Emacs's @emph{incremental} RIF
draws; those hooks are gated to no-ops and the @emph{whole} frame is repainted
from the current glyph matrix on every present (@code{lrg_present_frame}:
begin frame, clear, @code{expose_frame}, swap), which reuses Emacs's layout and
is immune to the FBO bug.

Timing is the subtle part: Emacs wraps every redisplay in
@code{block_buffer_flips} @dots{} @code{unblock_buffer_flips} so a frame is
never shown half-updated, which means @code{frame_up_to_date} and the RIF draw
hooks all run while buffer flips are @emph{blocked}.  lrg's real present point
is therefore the per-frame @code{terminal->buffer_flipping_unblocked_hook}
(@code{lrg_buffer_flipping_unblocked}), called from @code{unblock_buffer_flips}
once the redisplay completes (mirroring pgtk).  This one hook is what puts
typed text, the blinking cursor and every other redisplay on screen.
@code{lrg-capture-screen} / @code{lrgterm_dump_screen} present out-of-band and
so always reflect current state.

@section Event loop

GLFW requires main-thread event polling, so lrg uses no input thread.
@code{lrg_term_init} arms a @code{timerfd} (~60 Hz) registered with
@code{add_keyboard_wait_descriptor}; @code{pselect} wakes and calls
@code{lrg_read_socket}, which polls the window once (the single poll point)
and translates the queued mouse, focus and resize events.

Keyboard input drains raylib's key-press @emph{queue}
(@code{grl_input_get_key_pressed}) in press order rather than scanning key
state per frame: a mapped non-character key becomes a NON_ASCII keysym
(ESC @result{} @code{escape}, arrows, Tab, @dots{}); a printable key held
with Ctrl/Meta becomes a char-with-modifiers; a printable key with no
modifier consumes the next Unicode char from the char queue (which encodes
layout and shift), pulled at the key's position so it stays interleaved with
function keys from the same poll.  This delivers every keystroke exactly
once, in order, with no missed fast taps and no 60@tie{}Hz repeat storm,
which is why Evil/Doom keybinds (ESC to normal mode, @kbd{hjkl}, chords)
behave as they do under pgtk.

The OS window is user-resizable: @code{lrg_window_create} sets
@code{GRL_FLAG_WINDOW_RESIZABLE} on the raylib window (raylib otherwise opens
fixed-size windows, with no corner-drag and a disabled maximize button), and
@code{lrg_read_socket} re-fits the Emacs frame to the new size on each
@code{grl_window_is_resized} via @code{change_frame_size}.

@section Libregnum 3D buffers under --lrg

The @emph{cmacs-libregnum} subsystem (the libregnum editor, @code{gnuseye},
the CAD/STL model viewer) also renders through libregnum.  raylib allows one
window per process, so under @option{--lrg} these features do not open a
second window (that would deadlock); they BORROW the lrg backend's window and
GL context.  @code{cmacs_libregnum_render_window_acquire} detects a live lrg
frame (@code{cmacs_lrgterm_active_p}, linked weakly) and uses a windowless
renderer; each view renders its scene into its own FBO (the lrg GL context is
current on the main thread), and the lrg present
(@code{lrg_paint_libregnum_views}) blits each view's FBO texture into its
window rect via @code{lrg_frame_surface_draw_texture_region} --- the analogue
of pgtk's cairo overlay.  So @kbd{M-x gnuseye}, @kbd{M-x
cmacs-libregnum-editor} and opening an STL all render inside the lrg frame.

Mouse navigation works too: @code{lrg_read_socket} routes pointer motion,
button presses/releases and the wheel into the same input router pgtk uses
(@code{cmacs_libregnum_handle_motion}/@code{_button}/@code{_scroll}), so
left/right-drag orbits, middle-drag pans, the wheel zooms the viewport under
the pointer, a click picks a node, and right-click pops the context menu.  lrg
maps raylib L/R/M buttons (0/1/2) to the X11/GDK numbers the router expects
(1/3/2) and negates the raylib wheel sign to match pgtk's zoom direction; a
wheel event not over a view becomes a normal Emacs @code{WHEEL_EVENT} so
buffers scroll with the wheel under @option{--lrg} as well.  In-scene text
labels are still a follow-up.

The @emph{cmacs-gsurf} web browser composites the same way when built with
@option{--with-cmacs-gsurf-lrg}: a page is rendered to a @code{GrlTexture} by
gsurf's libregnum backend (offscreen WebKitGTK) and
@code{lrg_paint_libregnum_window} blits it into the buffer's window rect
alongside the 3D buffers (gsurf's readback is top-down, so it is not
Y-flipped).  @code{lrg_read_socket} offers pointer events to
@code{cmacs_gsurf_lrg_handle_*} after the libregnum handlers, and while a page
holds focus routes the keyboard to it (@code{lrg_route_keys_to_gsurf}) instead
of @code{lrg_drain_keys}; Escape releases focus.  See @ref{CMacs Gsurf} for the
full model.

Because lrg has no native (GTK) popup menus (@code{menu_show_hook} returns
nil), the libregnum/gnuseye right-click context menus are drawn @emph{in
engine}, with cascading fly-out submenus: the @code{lrg-popup-menu} primitive
renders a GTK-like popup with libregnum (panel + hovered/disabled rows +
separators + a submenu arrow, label text via the glyph atlas) and runs a modal
loop over a stack of open panels (hover opens a submenu to the right, flipping
left at the edge; left-click chooses a leaf; right-click/ESC/click-outside
dismiss; up/down move, right/RET open a submenu or choose, left closes one),
like @code{x-popup-menu}'s own modal loop.  @code{cmacs-libregnum-popup-menu}
flattens an Emacs menu (alist or keymap form, submenus kept as real nested
nodes) into the item tree it takes and maps the chosen leaf index back to the
value; graphical frames still pop the native menu.  Runs of separators (common
once kind-filtering drops the items between them) collapse to a single thin
divider, and a separator row is half height, so the menu stays compact.
Those 3D buffers sit in Evil
@emph{emacs state} for their single-key navigation, so @kbd{<escape>} is bound
to drop them to Evil @emph{normal} state (gnuseye's @kbd{<escape>} first
deselects a selected entity); re-enter navigation with @kbd{C-z}
(@code{evil-emacs-state}).

@section Render modes

The abstract @code{LrgFrameSurface} + @code{LrgRenderMode} + @code{pick} seam
makes each render mode additive: Layer 3 is mode-agnostic, the glyph atlas is
projection-free, and input routes through @code{pick} (identity in 2D, a
ray-cast in 3D).  @code{2d} (default, @code{Lrg2DSurface}) is flat and
pixel-exact.  @code{3d} (@code{Lrg3DSurface}) captures the flat frame to a
texture each redisplay and draws it as textured @emph{panels} in a perspective
scene viewed from a gentle elevated 3/4 camera angle (so the panels show real
perspective and the environment floor recedes on launch; orbit/zoom with
@kbd{C-c 3}).  @code{3dvr} is reserved (today it builds the 3D surface, mono).

@section 3D mode

Launch with a SPEC after the mode token,
@code{MODE[:ARRANGEMENT][:ENVIRONMENT]} (@samp{:} or @samp{,} separated), e.g.@:
@code{emacs --lrg=3d:per-window:workshop}.  Two orthogonal, runtime-switchable
axes:

@table @asis
@item Arrangement
@code{single-panel} (whole frame on one panel), @code{per-window} (one panel per
Emacs window), @code{free} (independent panel transforms; basic).
@item Environment
@code{void} (dark backdrop), @code{workshop} (grid floor), @code{cockpit}
(enclosed grid room).
@end table

New arrangements/environments are added by implementing the
@code{LrgSceneArrangement} / @code{LrgPanelEnvironment} GInterfaces and
registering an id with @code{LrgModeRegistry} --- no surface changes.
Depth-of-field focuses the selected window's panel and dims the rest.

The global @code{cmacs-lrg-3d-mode} (auto-enabled for lrg frames) binds runtime
control under @code{C-c 3}: @code{a}/@code{e} switch arrangement/environment,
@code{?} describes, @code{0} resets the camera, @code{+}/@code{-} zoom, the arrow
keys orbit, @code{f} brings the selected window front-and-centre, and
@code{p}/@code{u}/@code{U} pin / unpin / unpin-all panels.

@subsection Spatial interaction

The interaction model is @emph{hybrid}: command-the-room by default, with manual
override.  Focus a panel and the camera flies it front-and-centre while the
others recede --- by @kbd{Ctrl+double-left-click}, @kbd{C-c 3 f}, or voice/AI via
MCP (the same double-click on a @emph{workspace} panel switches to that workspace).
@kbd{C-c 3 2} (@code{cmacs-lrg-maximize-window}) goes further: it maximizes the
selected window's panel head-on and level, filling the viewport edge-to-edge --- a
flat, 2D-like view of the buffer inside the 3D scene (as the 2D backend / PGTK
would show it); @kbd{C-c 3 0} returns to the 3D view.
Manually, @emph{middle-drag} orbits around the @emph{room} (the whole scene, not
just a focused panel), @kbd{Ctrl + left-drag} is a first-person @emph{look} (turn
the camera in place about its own position), @kbd{Ctrl+wheel} dollies in/out, and
@kbd{Ctrl/Super + middle-drag} grabs and moves the panel under the pointer (which @emph{pins} it so
it keeps its place across re-layouts); @kbd{C-c 3 u}/@kbd{U} release panels.
Plain left-click still edits; a bare middle-click is still @code{mouse-2} (yank);
@kbd{Super+drag} is a trackpad alias for middle-drag.  When the pointer is over a
window hosting a @emph{libregnum view} (gnuseye, the editor, a CAD viewer), the
spatial gestures defer to that app's own camera controls, so workspace navigation
never fights an embedded app.

These are @emph{intents}, not raw buttons: each gesture maps to a C method that
is also a DEFUN (@code{cmacs-lrg-3d-focus-panel}, @code{-orbit},
@code{-move-panel}, @code{-pin-panel}, @code{-pick-panel}) and an MCP tool
(@code{lrgterm_3d_focus_selected}, @code{_pin_selected}, @code{_unpin_all}, @dots{}),
with the high-rate drag math in C.  The mouse is just the first input device:
gestures and VR controllers bind to the same intents, and voice/AI drive them
through MCP --- the path toward @emph{stark-mode} without re-architecting.  All
bindings are rebindable.

A connected @emph{gamepad / 6DOF} device already drives the camera on this seam
via a libregnum @code{LrgInputMap} (right stick orbits the room, left stick pans,
triggers dolly), reading signed analog axes and calling the same orbit/pan/dolly
intents; it is a no-op when no device is present.
@code{cmacs-lrg-3d-load-gamepad-bindings} loads a YAML remap, so a SpaceMouse or
other 6DOF device exposed as a joystick is rebound without code (a native
SpaceMouse via @code{libspnav} remains future work).

@subsection Spatial workspaces (the workspace carousel)

With Doom / persp-mode workspaces, the @emph{workspace carousel} shows
@emph{every workspace} as a live panel in 3D: the current workspace is the live
centre (the real window panels), and each other workspace renders its @emph{real
contents} into a panel arranged on a curved arc (``carousel'') that curves away
from you.  You see all your workspaces at once, move/rotate them, and switch
between them in 3D.

It turns on @emph{automatically} on a 3D lrg frame once persp-mode is active (the
@code{cmacs-lrg-3d-workspaces-auto} option; set it to nil, or toggle by hand with
@code{cmacs-lrg-3d-workspaces-mode} / @kbd{C-c 3 m}).  The immediate neighbours
sit in view at the default camera; pull back with @kbd{C-c 3 o} (overview), or
orbit (middle-drag), to see the rest of the ring.

The other workspaces are @emph{not snapshots} --- each keeps rendering its actual
buffers off screen, so a compile running in another workspace updates on its panel
while you work.  A non-current workspace's saved window layout is installed in the
frame off screen, redisplayed so its buffers' current contents draw, captured to
that workspace's own panel texture, and the live layout restored, all without
disturbing what you see (@code{cmacs-lrg-3d-begin-offscreen} /
@code{-render-into-panel} / @code{-end-offscreen}).

A repeating timer keeps them live: each tick re-renders at most @emph{one}
off-screen workspace whose content changed since it was last drawn (round-robin,
with dirty-tracking via buffer modification ticks), so idle workspaces cost
nothing and a changing one updates roughly every (interval times workspace-count)
seconds; the current workspace is always full-rate.  This is the performance
trade-off of live (not snapshot) workspaces; tune it below or set
@code{cmacs-lrg-3d-workspaces-live} to nil to update only on switch / refresh.

Layout follows @code{+workspace-list-names} order: a workspace's slot is its
position @emph{relative to the current one}, so the current workspace is always
centred and switching shifts the carousel.  Switching (via @kbd{SPC TAB @var{n}},
@code{+workspace-switch}, or a click) eases the carousel smoothly to the new
arrangement.  A workspace you move (Ctrl/Super + middle-drag its panel) or rotate
keeps its place, remembered per workspace as an @code{lrg-3d-xform} persp
parameter.

@strong{Switch by clicking}: Ctrl+double-left-click a workspace panel to switch
to it (the same chord focuses a window panel front-and-centre; on a workspace
panel it switches).  The click is applied in the command loop, so the persp hooks
and the eased transition fire exactly as for @kbd{SPC TAB @var{n}}.

Commands (under the @kbd{C-c 3} prefix of @code{cmacs-lrg-3d-mode}):

@table @code
@item cmacs-lrg-3d-workspaces-mode @r{(@kbd{C-c 3 m})}
Toggle the carousel (a global mode; no-op without persp-mode or off a 3D frame).
@item cmacs-lrg-3d-workspaces-toggle @r{(@kbd{C-c 3 SPC})}
Hide / show the ring (the live centre is unaffected).
@item cmacs-lrg-3d-workspaces-overview @r{(@kbd{C-c 3 o})}
Pull the camera back to see all workspaces at once.
@item cmacs-lrg-3d-workspaces-rotate @r{(@kbd{C-c 3 r})}
Rotate a chosen workspace panel (remembered).
@item cmacs-lrg-3d-workspaces-refresh @r{(@kbd{C-c 3 g})}
Relayout and re-render every workspace panel now.
@end table

Tuning (@code{customize-group cmacs-lrg-3d-workspaces}):
@code{cmacs-lrg-3d-workspaces-auto} (auto-enable on a 3D frame, t),
@code{-radius} (arc curve radius, 10.0),
@code{-spacing} (degrees between slots, 28.0),
@code{-height} (panel height; width tracks the frame aspect, 3.2),
@code{-elevation} (vertical offset, 0.0),
@code{-max} (max non-current workspaces shown per side, 8),
@code{-frame-zoom} (optional camera pull-back on enable, 1.0 = none),
@code{-live} (keep panels live via the round-robin updater, t), and
@code{-update-interval} (seconds between updater ticks, 0.12).

@section Commands

@table @code
@item lrg-capture-screen FILE &optional FRAME
Repaint the frame and write a PNG of exactly what the backend renders --- the
reliable rendering oracle for tests and the basis of @code{lrgterm_dump_screen}.
@item lrg-display-pixel-size
@code{(WIDTH . HEIGHT)} of the current monitor.
@item lrg-set-clipboard / lrg-get-clipboard
System-clipboard text (UTF-8).
@item cmacs-lrg-render-mode &optional FRAME
The frame's render mode (@code{"2d"}/@code{"3d"}/@code{"3dvr"}), or nil.
@item cmacs-lrg-3d-set-arrangement / -set-environment ID &optional FRAME
Switch the 3D arrangement / environment at runtime (the C primitives behind the
@code{cmacs-lrg-set-arrangement} / @code{-set-environment} commands).
@item cmacs-lrg-3d-camera OP &optional AMOUNT FRAME
Move the 3D camera (@code{"reset"}, @code{"zoom-in"}, @code{"orbit-left"}, @dots{}).
@end table

@section MCP tools

With @code{--with-cmacs-mcp}, the backend registers @code{lrgterm_frame_info},
@code{lrgterm_render_stats}, @code{lrgterm_force_redraw},
@code{lrgterm_dump_screen} (captures the lrg frame to a PNG and returns it as an
image), and the 3D tools @code{lrgterm_3d_state},
@code{lrgterm_3d_cycle_arrangement}, @code{lrgterm_3d_cycle_environment} and
@code{lrgterm_3d_camera_orbit}.

@section Known v1 limitations

One OS window per terminal: a second @emph{top-level} frame is refused
(@code{lrg-create-frame} signals an error rather than open a stray second OS
window).  @emph{Child} frames (@code{parent-frame} set) -- corfu/company popups,
@code{posframe}, tooltips -- DO work: they are created without their own OS
window and composited as overlays inside the parent's single window (the present
pass draws each visible child at its @code{(left, top)} via a per-surface draw
offset and @code{expose_frame}; see @code{lrg_composite_child_frames}).  So
corfu's popup shows at point (in 2D, and in 3D captured onto the panel); the
child takes no keyboard input (corfu redirects focus to the parent, so that is
fine), but mouse-clicking a candidate is not yet routed to the child (keyboard
selection works).  The menu @emph{bar} is not drawn (use @code{M-x tmm-menubar}
/ @key{F10}; dialogs go to the minibuffer; the libregnum/gnuseye right-click
@emph{context} menus, though, are drawn in-engine via @code{lrg-popup-menu});
scroll bars and the tool bar are not drawn (inline images @emph{are} drawn); the
mouse pointer does not change shape; IME is direct/Quail input only; @code{--lrg}
and @code{--gowl} are mutually exclusive.

In 3D mode, panels re-fit on a window resize: the surface reconciles its
geometry to the live window each frame, re-runs the arrangement layout, and eases
the panels to the new size.  Sizing is always-fit (a fixed world height, width
following the frame aspect), so the whole frame stays visible and consistently
framed at every window size.

@strong{Related pgtk-on-Wayland caveat (a reason some prefer @code{--lrg}).}  An
ordinary PGTK frame on @emph{native Wayland} can freeze right after a child-frame
popup (an LSP / @code{corfu} / @code{company} autocomplete, a tooltip): GNOME
then offers ``Force quit or Wait'' and a @code{--fg-daemon} eventually exits.
This is a GTK limitation -- Emacs built with PGTK cannot survive a Wayland
display-connection disconnect (it prints this warning at startup) -- and
child-frame popups are the usual trigger (a Wayland popup-protocol hiccup wedges
or drops the compositor connection).  It is @emph{not} a cmacs/lrg bug: the
process sits at about 0% CPU in the plain idle command loop (a thread
dump shows @code{command_loop_1 -> read_char -> pselect}, an empty Lisp
backtrace, and no coredump -- a lost display, not a deadlock).  Work around it by
running the daemon under XWayland (@code{Environment=GDK_BACKEND=x11} in
@file{emacs.service}, or @code{GDK_BACKEND=x11 emacs}), or by using the lrg
backend, whose child frames are in-window overlays so there is no Wayland popup
protocol to break.  See the Troubleshooting section of
@file{doc_org/cmacs/cmacs-lrgterm.org} for the full writeup.

See @file{doc_org/cmacs/cmacs-lrgterm.org} for the living document.

@c ===================================================================
@node CMacs Imgedit
@chapter CMacs Imgedit: 2D Image / Sprite Editor

@code{cmacs-imgedit} is a layered 2D raster image and sprite editor built on
libregnum's @code{LrgImageDocument} / @code{LrgImageLayer} (a CPU layer
compositor: opacity, blend modes REPLACE/OVER/ADD/MULTIPLY/SUBTRACT, per-layer
offset, undo).  Enable with @option{--with-cmacs-imgedit} (requires
@option{--with-cmacs-libregnum}).  Off by default.

It has two facets: a handle-based model API (the @code{cmacs-imgedit-*}
primitives, scriptable and MCP/headless-driveable) and @code{cmacs-imgedit-mode},
which shows the composited document as a native Emacs image and supports mouse
painting plus command-driven tools.  See
@file{doc_org/cmacs/cmacs-imgedit.org} for the full reference.

@section Commands

@table @kbd
@item M-x cmacs-imgedit
New image (prompts width x height); with a prefix arg, open a file.
@item M-x cmacs-imgedit-open-file
Open an existing image (PNG/JPG/BMP/@dots{}).
@item M-x cmacs-imgedit-from-clipboard
Open the image on the system clipboard (gowl image clipboard or
@code{wl-paste}) in a new editor buffer.
@item M-x cmacs-imgedit-from-screenshot
Capture a gowl screenshot into a new editor buffer.
@end table

In @code{cmacs-imgedit-mode}: @kbd{mouse-1} paints; @kbd{mouse-3} pops the
context menu (a native GTK menu under pgtk, the in-engine libregnum menu under
@option{--lrg}).  Tools: @kbd{b} brush, @kbd{l} line, @kbd{>} arrow (filled
head at the release point), @kbd{r} rectangle, @kbd{c} circle, @kbd{E}
ellipse, @kbd{t} text (@kbd{T} sets the text size), @kbd{k} bucket, @kbd{e}
eyedropper; also @kbd{f} fill layer, @kbd{p} pencil, @kbd{G} flood fill,
@kbd{C} colour.  Layers: @kbd{L} add, @kbd{K} remove, @kbd{a} select, @kbd{v}
visibility, @kbd{o} opacity.  @kbd{u}/@kbd{U} undo/redo, @kbd{s} save,
@kbd{y} (or @kbd{w}) @emph{copies the flattened image to the clipboard}
(evil-yank semantics), @kbd{C-y} pastes a clipboard image as a layer,
@kbd{P} pastes a screenshot, @kbd{+}/@kbd{-} zoom, @kbd{D} diagnose.

The annotation round-trip: @kbd{M-x cmacs-imgedit-from-clipboard} on a copied
screenshot, mark it up with arrows/shapes/text, then @kbd{y} and paste the
annotated PNG anywhere.  The drawing DEFUNs gained
@code{cmacs-imgedit-draw-arrow}, @code{-draw-ellipse} and @code{-draw-text}
(TrueType, or the engine's embedded bitmap font when headless).

Clipboard backends, tried in order: gowl's native image clipboard (when its
DEFUNs exist); Emacs's own in-process GTK clipboard
(@code{cmacs-imgedit-clipboard-set-png} / @code{-get-png} /
@code{-available-p}) --- a normal GTK client clipboard, so it behaves
identically under gowl, GNOME/Mutter, KDE or X11 with no external tools;
and @command{wl-copy}/@command{wl-paste} as the fallback for GTK-less
sessions (@option{--lrg}, tty).  @file{install-deps} installs
@code{wl-clipboard}.

Opened images auto-fit their zoom (sprites high, screenshots 1x); transparent
areas render on @code{cmacs-imgedit-canvas-background} so dark strokes stay
visible on dark themes.  Every stroke echoes what it painted and on which
layer.  The canvas carries its own @code{keymap} text property, which outranks
Evil state maps and @code{context-menu-mode}, and the mode map is registered
with @code{evil-make-overriding-map} --- mouse painting, the right-click menu
and the tool keys all work unmodified under Doom/Evil.

@section Model API

Documents are referenced by an integer handle; layers are indexed bottom-up.
Key primitives: @code{cmacs-imgedit-new}, @code{-open}, @code{-save},
@code{-export-png-bytes}; layers @code{-add-layer},
@code{-add-layer-from-file}, @code{-add-layer-rgba}, @code{-remove-layer},
@code{-move-layer}, @code{-duplicate-layer}, and the per-layer
opacity/blend/visibility setters; drawing @code{-set-color}, @code{-fill},
@code{-set-pixel}, @code{-draw-line}, @code{-draw-rect}, @code{-draw-circle},
@code{-flood-fill}, @code{-pixel-at}; undo @code{-push-undo}, @code{-undo},
@code{-redo}.  Clipboard / screenshot use @code{wl-copy}/@code{wl-paste} and
@code{gowl-screenshot-monitor}.

A sprite saved here is a normal PNG that the 3D @code{cmacs-libregnum-editor}
can reference as a sprite or tilemap asset; right-clicking such a node there
offers @emph{Edit sprite image@dots{}}, which opens the asset here and
reloads the node's texture on save
(@code{cmacs-imgedit-after-save-functions}).  With
@option{--with-cmacs-mcp}, typed @code{imgedit_*} MCP tools (new / open /
save / info / set_color / draw_shape / draw_text / add_layer / undo) let an
agent do all of this headlessly.

