@node CMacs Vidstudio
@chapter CMacs Vidstudio: Reel-based Video Editor

@code{cmacs-vidstudio} is a video editor built on libregnum's @emph{Reel}
composition system.  A project is an @code{LrgReel}; each @emph{track} is an
@code{LrgReelTransitionSeries} of clip segments joined by transitions, with
per-clip effects.  Frames are composited on the CPU and exported through the
ffmpeg-backed reel exporters (which invoke the @command{ffmpeg} binary; no
libav linkage is needed for the editor itself).  Enable with
@option{--with-cmacs-vidstudio} (requires @option{--with-cmacs-libregnum}).
Off by default.

This is distinct from @ref{CMacs Video}, which is GStreamer @emph{playback};
vidstudio is a Reel-based @emph{editor}.  Smooth real-time scrubbing of source
clips is served by the libregnum @code{LrgVideoPlayer} FFmpeg decode backend
(built with @code{FFMPEG=1}).  See @file{doc_org/cmacs/cmacs-vidstudio.org} for
the full reference.

@section Commands

@kbd{M-x cmacs-vidstudio} creates a project (prompts width x height x fps).  In
@code{cmacs-vidstudio-mode} the buffer previews the frame at the playhead;
@kbd{mouse-3} pops the context menu (GTK under pgtk, in-engine under
@option{--lrg}).  Keys: @kbd{i} import clip, @kbd{C} solid colour, @kbd{T}
title, @kbd{n} add track, @kbd{a} active track, @kbd{t} transition, @kbd{e}
effect, @kbd{s} split at playhead, @kbd{g} go to frame, @kbd{p} (or
@kbd{SPC}) play/pause, @kbd{@key{left}}/@kbd{@key{right}} step, @kbd{E}
export MP4, @kbd{G} export GIF.
Like imgedit, the rendered buffer carries a @code{keymap} text property and
the mode map is registered with @code{evil-make-overriding-map}, so the menu
and keys work unmodified under Doom/Evil and @code{context-menu-mode} ---
except @kbd{SPC}, which Doom's leader owns; use @kbd{p} there.

Video imports never block: the clip is probed at import and the whole-clip
ffmpeg decode runs on a worker thread
(@code{lrg_reel_video_source_set_async_decode}); the preview shows a dark
placeholder until @code{cmacs-vidstudio-clip-ready-p} reports readiness
(the editor polls and re-renders automatically), and export always waits
for real frames.  Playback advances the playhead from the wall clock,
skipping slow frames and yielding to pending input, so it never freezes
the editor; preview redraws are capped at
@code{cmacs-vidstudio-preview-fps} and each is followed by an explicit
@code{redisplay} so the screen actually repaints between timer runs.
Play at the end of the timeline rewinds; clips still decoding show a
@samp{decoding@dots{}} marker in the timeline.  Playback frames render
through @code{cmacs-vidstudio-render-ppm} --- composited, downscaled to
preview width in C, and emitted as uncompressed binary PPM (roughly 5x
faster end-to-end than the per-frame PNG round-trip), with the previous
frame's image flushed from the cache on each redraw.

@section Model API

Projects are referenced by an integer handle; clips by a stable id.  Key
primitives: @code{cmacs-vidstudio-new}, @code{-add-track}, the clip inserters
@code{-add-solid-clip} / @code{-add-image-clip} / @code{-add-video-clip} /
@code{-add-text-clip}, @code{-set-transition} (fade/dissolve/wipe/slide/zoom/
iris/flip/push/clock-wipe), @code{-add-effect}
(blur/bloom/color-grade/vignette/grain), the editing ops
@code{-set-clip-duration} / @code{-split-clip} / @code{-move-clip} /
@code{-remove-clip}, the queries @code{-clip-at} / @code{-clip-duration} /
@code{-clip-start-frame} / @code{-total-frames}, and rendering / export
@code{-render-png}, @code{-render-ppm}, @code{-frame-pixel},
@code{-export-video}, @code{-export-gif}.  With @option{--with-cmacs-mcp},
typed @code{vidstudio_*} MCP tools (new / info / add_track / add_clip /
set_transition / add_effect / split / render_frame / export) let an agent
cut a video headlessly.

