@section Bundled Models (container builds)

@code{./build-container} bundles a default English STT model and
TTS voice into the staged image at
@file{/usr/share/cmacs/whisper-models/} and
@file{/usr/share/cmacs/piper-voices/}.  Downstream images that copy
@file{/build/stage/usr/.} into @file{/usr/.} inherit them
automatically; cmacs's @code{cmacs-whisper-models-search-path} and
@code{cmacs-piper-voices-search-path} pick them up at runtime
(user-installed models in @file{~/.local/share/cmacs/} take
precedence).

Defaults (~210 MB):

@multitable @columnfractions .35 .65
@headitem ARG @tab Default
@item @code{WHISPER_MODEL_NAME} @tab @code{ggml-base.en.bin}
@item @code{WHISPER_MODEL_URL} @tab huggingface.co/ggerganov/whisper.cpp
@item @code{PIPER_VOICE_NAME} @tab @code{en_US-amy-low.onnx}
@item @code{PIPER_VOICE_DIR} @tab @code{en/en_US/amy/low}
@item @code{PIPER_VOICE_BASE_URL} @tab huggingface.co/rhasspy/piper-voices
@end multitable

Override at build time via env vars (the @code{build-container}
wrapper forwards them as @code{--build-arg}):

@example
WHISPER_MODEL_NAME=ggml-small.en.bin \
WHISPER_MODEL_URL=https://huggingface.co/ggerganov/whisper.cpp/resolve/main/ggml-small.en.bin \
PIPER_VOICE_NAME=en_GB-alba-medium.onnx \
PIPER_VOICE_DIR=en/en_GB/alba/medium \
  ./build-container
@end example

@section Building the Bacon Module

The @command{cmacsgi} module is compiled into the CMacs binary by
default (via the bundled libbacon static link).  For standalone or
development use, it can also be built as a loadable @file{.so}:

@example
cd cmacs/bacon/modules/
make
@end example

This produces @file{cmacs_gi.so} for use with an external
@command{bacon} binary.

@section Runtime Feature Flags

For every @option{--with-cmacs-@var{name}} /
@option{--enable-cmacs-@var{name}} option, cmacs exposes an
always-bound Lisp variable @code{IS-CMACS-@var{NAME}} (plus a
lower-case alias @code{is-cmacs-@var{name}}) that is @code{t} when the
subsystem was compiled into this build and @code{nil} otherwise.  A
config can branch on features without a @code{void-variable} error:

@example
(when IS-CMACS-AI
  (global-set-key (kbd "C-c a a") #'cmacs-ai-call))

(when is-cmacs-gowl                 ; the lower-case alias works too
  (cmacs-gowl-mode 1))
@end example

The flags reflect compile-time configuration only.  The full set of
compiled-in subsystems is @code{(cmacs-compiled-features)} (a list of
symbols); @code{(cmacs-feature-p 'video)} additionally applies a
runtime availability check for a few media subsystems.  The variables
are defined even in an upstream-shaped build with every cmacs feature
off (they all read @code{nil} there), so a config is portable across
builds.

@section Compile Flags

The Emacs build system picks up CMacs objects via @code{configure.ac}:

@example
CMACS_GLIB_OBJ="cmacs-glib-loop.o cmacs-gobject.o
                 cmacs-gclosure.o cmacs-dbus-service.o
                 cmacs-eval-dispatch.o"
CMACS_GI_OBJ="cmacs-gi.o"
CMACS_CRISPY_OBJ="cmacs-crispy.o"
CMACS_BACON_OBJ="cmacs-bacon.o cmacs-bacon-ipc.o"
@end example

C source files live under @file{cmacs/} and are symlinked into
@file{src/} for the build system.

@section Building on Ubuntu 24.04 / older hosts

gowl builds against wlroots 0.19 @emph{or} 0.20 (newest present wins;
pin with @code{make WLROOTS=0.19}).  wlroots 0.20 is recommended --- it
unlocks per-window screencast capture (sharing a specific window); 0.19
gives monitor-only capture.  See @file{deps/gowl/docs/building.org}
(``wlroots version'') for the trade-off and a build-wlroots-0.20-from-source
recipe.

Ubuntu 24.04 LTS (noble) --- and other distributions that have not
yet caught up --- ship versions of several gowl dependencies that are
too old to satisfy @code{pkg-config --exists wlroots-0.19} (let alone
0.20).  Noble specifically ships
wlroots 0.17, wayland 1.22, wayland-protocols 1.34, and pixman
0.42, while gowl requires wlroots @geq{}0.19, wayland
@geq{}1.23.1, wayland-protocols @geq{}1.38, and pixman
@geq{}0.43.  @code{libdex-dev} (0.6.0) does ship on noble, but in
the @code{universe} component --- enable it with
@code{sudo add-apt-repository universe} if @code{apt} cannot find
it.  Its unversioned @code{pkg-config --exists libdex-1} gate is
satisfied by that 0.6.0, so libdex no longer needs a source build
on noble.

Because gowl's @file{config.mk} queries all required pkg-config
modules in a single invocation, a single missing package causes
@code{pkg-config --cflags} to return an empty string, which then
cascades into a @code{fatal error: glib.h: No such file or
directory} when the vendored @file{yaml-glib} sources are
compiled.  The real fix is to satisfy the version gates, not to
patch the include paths.

First run @code{./install-deps --system}.  It installs every cmacs
dependency @code{apt} can provide on your release --- including the
full LibreClaw set (@code{libcmark-dev}, @code{libpq-dev},
@code{libsqlite3-dev}, @code{libdex-dev} from @code{universe}, and
so on) --- and prints a @code{WARNING} listing any package not in
your apt repos instead of aborting the whole install.  If
@code{libdex-dev} appears in that warning, enable @code{universe}
(@code{sudo add-apt-repository universe && sudo apt-get update})
and re-run it.

The only dependencies @code{apt} cannot satisfy on noble are the
gowl stack (wlroots / wayland / wayland-protocols / pixman).
Install the extra build tools for those, including the mcp and
podomation runtime deps:

@example
sudo apt install \
  meson ninja-build pkg-config \
  libglib2.0-dev libjson-glib-dev libsoup-3.0-dev libetpan-dev \
  libdrm-dev libxkbcommon-dev libudev-dev libinput-dev \
  libseat-dev libdisplay-info-dev libliftoff-dev hwdata \
  libgbm-dev libvulkan-dev glslang-tools \
  libxcb1-dev libxcb-composite0-dev libxcb-ewmh-dev \
  libxcb-icccm4-dev libxcb-render-util0-dev libxcb-res0-dev \
  libxcb-xinput-dev
@end example

Then build the out-of-date components into @file{/usr/local}, in
dependency order: wayland 1.23.1, wayland-protocols 1.38, pixman
0.44, and finally wlroots 0.19 (libdex 0.6.0 from @code{universe}
already satisfies the gate; build libdex 0.9.1 from source only if
you need a newer release).  Each uses the standard meson recipe,
e.g.@:

@example
git clone -b 0.19 https://gitlab.freedesktop.org/wlroots/wlroots.git
cd wlroots
meson setup build --prefix=/usr/local
ninja -C build && sudo ninja -C build install
sudo ldconfig
@end example

See @file{doc_org/cmacs/build.org} for the full step-by-step
recipe including every intermediate dependency.

Everything installs under @file{/usr/local}, which is @emph{not}
on Ubuntu's default pkg-config search path.  Export
@code{PKG_CONFIG_PATH} in every shell where you run
@command{./configure} or @command{make} for cmacs:

@example
export PKG_CONFIG_PATH=/usr/local/lib/x86_64-linux-gnu/pkgconfig:/usr/local/lib/pkgconfig
pkg-config --modversion wlroots-0.19   # => 0.19.x
pkg-config --modversion libdex-1       # => 0.9.x
@end example

If a gowl compile fails with @code{glib.h: No such file or
directory} despite glib being installed, run
@code{pkg-config --print-errors --exists} on the full
@code{DEPS_REQUIRED} list from @file{deps/gowl/config.mk} to
identify the missing module.  Do not try to patch the include
paths directly --- the empty @code{CFLAGS_DEPS} is a symptom of
an unmet version gate somewhere in the list.

@section Embedded Dependency Documentation

Each vendored dependency under @file{deps/} ships its own
@file{docs/} folder.  The @code{cmacs-deps-docs} Makefile target ---
a prerequisite of @code{actual-all}, so it runs on every @code{make}
--- invokes @file{tools/sync-deps-docs.sh}, which mirrors each
@file{deps/<dep>/docs/} tree verbatim into
@file{doc_org/cmacs/deps/<dep>/}.  That tree installs alongside the
rest of the manual and is browsable from Emacs with
@code{M-x cmacs-manual-topic} (the topic list is discovered
dynamically) or via the generated
@file{doc_org/cmacs/deps/index.org}.

@itemize @bullet
@item
The generated @file{doc_org/cmacs/deps/} tree is a build artifact:
it is git-ignored and refreshed from the current submodule checkouts
on every build.  Regenerate it manually by running
@file{tools/sync-deps-docs.sh}.
@item
Files are copied as-is --- both @file{.org} and @file{.md} --- so no
Markdown conversion or extra build dependency is needed.
@item
The script is a clean no-op on a tree without @file{deps/}, so an
upstream-style build is unaffected.
@end itemize

@c ===================================================================
@node Upstream Changes
@chapter Upstream Changes

cmacs tracks upstream GNU Emacs and merges it in every few weeks.  To keep
those merges painless, new features live in @emph{new} files under
@file{cmacs/<subsystem>/} and @file{lisp/cmacs/}; edits to stock Emacs
sources (@file{src/*.c}, @file{src/*.in}, @file{lisp/*.el},
@file{src/Makefile.in}, @file{configure.ac}) are kept as small and as
guarded as possible --- ideally a single @code{#ifdef HAVE_CMACS_*} block
calling into a @code{cmacs_*} function defined in a new file.  This chapter
is the authoritative catalogue of @emph{every} such edit, so a merge
conflict on one is easy to resolve.  Many hunks carry a
@code{/* CMACS ... */} comment, but not all, so use this list as the index.
When you add or remove an upstream touch-point, update this chapter and
@file{doc_org/cmacs/cmacs-upstream-changes.org} in the same commit.

Everything cmacs adds is gated behind a @code{HAVE_CMACS_*} macro, so with
the cmacs flags off an upstream-style build still works and every hunk
below compiles out.

@menu
* Upstream Generated Files::    Build artifacts that auto-pick-up cmacs.
* Upstream C Sources::          emacs.c, process.c, pgtkterm.c, xwidget.c, lisp.h.
* Upstream Build System::       configure.ac, config.in, epaths.in, Makefile.in.
* Upstream Lisp::               lisp/startup.el.
* Re-applying After a Merge::   How to restore the hunks.
@end menu

@node Upstream Generated Files
@section Generated files (not hand-edited)

These contain @code{cmacs} strings but are build artifacts regenerated from
the sources below --- do not edit them, and ignore them in a merge:
@file{src/config.h} (from @file{configure.ac} + @file{src/config.in} via
autoheader), @file{src/globals.h} (make-docfile, from the cmacs DEFUNs),
@file{src/buildobj.h}, @file{src/epaths.h} (from @file{src/epaths.in}), and
the generated lisp loaders @file{lisp/loaddefs.el}, @file{lisp/cus-load.el},
@file{lisp/finder-inf.el}, @file{lisp/subdirs.el} (they discover
@file{lisp/cmacs/} automatically).

@node Upstream C Sources
@section C sources

@subsection src/emacs.c

Several small hunks in @code{main()} / its init path:

@itemize @bullet
@item
@strong{Includes} (gated): @file{cmacs-bacon.h}, @file{cmacs-crispy.h},
@file{<gowl.h>} / @file{<wayland-server-core.h>}.
@item
@strong{@option{--bacon} entry} (@code{HAVE_CMACS_BACON}): scan @code{argv}
and enter the embedded shell via @code{cmacs_bacon_main} before any Emacs
init; never returns.  @code{cmacs_bacon_main} itself also handles the
batch forms @option{--bacon -c CMD} and @option{--bacon SCRIPT} (no
extra @file{emacs.c} hunk).
@item
@strong{@option{--crispy} entry} (@code{HAVE_CMACS_CRISPY}): identical
shape to the @option{--bacon} scan, directly below it.  Calls
@code{cmacs_crispy_main} to run a crispy script / inline code / stdin /
terminal REPL without any Emacs initialization; never returns.
@item
@strong{@code{usage_message} entries} (gated, @code{/* CMACS: ... */}
marked): one line each for @option{--bacon} and @option{--crispy} so
@command{emacs --help} documents the batch entry points.
@item
@strong{@option{--gowl} early start} (@code{HAVE_CMACS_GOWL}): create and
configure the @code{GowlCompositor} (including the
@code{evaluate-gowl-config-with-cmacs} / @code{evaluate-c-config-with-cmacs}
gates) and start it before display init, so PGTK/GDK connects to gowl's own
Wayland display.
@item
@strong{JSC GC-signal redirect} (@code{HAVE_CMACS_GSURF || HAVE_XWIDGETS}),
plus a guarded @code{#include <dlfcn.h>}, at the very top of @code{main()}:
@example
#if defined HAVE_CMACS_GSURF || defined HAVE_XWIDGETS
  int (*cmacs_jsc_configure_gc_signal) (int)
    = (int (*) (int)) dlsym (RTLD_DEFAULT, "JSConfigureSignalForGC");
  if (cmacs_jsc_configure_gc_signal != NULL)
    cmacs_jsc_configure_gc_signal (40);   /* 40 = SIGRTMIN+5 on Linux */
#endif
@end example
WebKit's JavaScriptCore (pulled in by gsurf and/or @option{--with-xwidgets})
pauses threads for GC using a signal defaulting to @code{SIGUSR1}
(signal@tie{}10) --- the same signal podomation's @code{engine} source
(@code{on_sigusr1}), libreclaw's thread dump, and Emacs's @code{[sigusr1]}
use, so JSC steals it.  JSC installs its handler during early GTK/library
init, before any cmacs runtime code runs, so this is the only reliable
place to redirect it to a free real-time signal.  We call JSC's private
@code{JSConfigureSignalForGC()} (resolved by @code{dlsym}, so it is a no-op
if absent) rather than setting the @code{JSC_SIGNAL_FOR_GC} environment
variable: the env var works, but JSC's option parser also scans every
@code{JSC_*} var and rejects it with @samp{ERROR: invalid option:
JSC_SIGNAL_FOR_GC=40} (once per JSC process).  The programmatic call sets
the same config field with no such noise.  @code{SIGUSR2}/@code{SIGHUP}
are unaffected.
@item
@strong{@code{syms_of_cmacs_*} block}: register every subsystem's DEFUNs at
dump time (one @code{#ifdef HAVE_CMACS_*} per subsystem: glib, gobject,
dbus, gi, crispy, bacon, gowl, podomation, libreclaw, org-ex, mcp,
cintrospect, cpatch, video, audio, whisper, piper, ai, libregnum, gsurf).
The block opens with one @emph{unconditional} @code{syms_of_cmacs_features ()}
call (the always-linked @code{IS-CMACS-*} feature-flag registry in
@file{cmacs/core/cmacs-features.c}).
@item
@strong{@code{init_cmacs_*} late-init block} (marked
@code{/* CMACS: late init ... */}): run each subsystem's runtime init after
terminal setup (@code{init_cmacs_glib}, @code{init_cmacs_dbus},
@code{init_cmacs_gowl}, @dots{}, @code{init_cmacs_gsurf}).
@end itemize

@subsection src/process.c --- GLib event-loop hooks

cmacs does @emph{not} replace Emacs's event loop; it hooks @code{pselect()}
so a @code{GMainContext} is iterated around the same wait.  Include of
@file{cmacs/glib/cmacs-glib-loop.h}, then (@code{HAVE_CMACS_GLIB})
@code{cmacs_glib_prepare} before the wait and @code{cmacs_glib_dispatch}
after.  @code{cmacs_glib_dispatch} must clear @code{waiting_for_input}
before dispatch and restore it after, or a Lisp error from a GLib callback
aborts with @samp{Fatal error 6: Aborted}.

@subsection src/pgtkterm.c --- paint hooks + libregnum input

@itemize @bullet
@item
@strong{Overlay includes} (gated): @file{cmacs-ink-overlay.h}
(@code{HAVE_CMACS_GLIB}), @file{cmacs-video.h}, @file{cmacs-audio.h},
@file{cmacs-libregnum.h}.
@item
@strong{Paint hooks} in @code{pgtk_handle_draw} (after the back-surface
blit): @code{cmacs_video_overlay_paint} (@code{HAVE_CMACS_VIDEO}),
@code{cmacs_audio_overlay_paint} (@code{HAVE_CMACS_AUDIO}),
@code{cmacs_libregnum_overlay_paint} (@code{HAVE_CMACS_LIBREGNUM}), and
@code{cmacs_ink_overlay_paint} (@code{HAVE_CMACS_GLIB}, painted last).
@item
@strong{libregnum pointer input} (@code{HAVE_CMACS_LIBREGNUM}): when the
selected window shows a libregnum view, route motion / button / scroll into
the libregnum input controller before Emacs's own mouse machinery; other
buffers fall through unchanged.  Viewport orbit/pan/zoom, picking,
gizmo-handle drags, and object move-drags all run through it.
@item
@strong{libregnum GDK drag-source} (@code{HAVE_CMACS_LIBREGNUM}): two thin
hunks call helpers from @file{cmacs/libregnum/cmacs-libregnum-dnd.c}:

@enumerate
@item
In @code{pgtk_set_event_handler} (near the closing brace):
@example
#ifdef HAVE_CMACS_LIBREGNUM
/* CMACS: libregnum editor drag-source -- connect drag-data-get/drag-end
   to supply the palette/asset payload when a GDK drag is initiated. */
cmacs_libregnum_dnd_setup_frame (f);
#endif
@end example
This connects @code{drag-data-get} and @code{drag-end} signals once per
frame so @code{gtk_drag_begin_with_coordinates} can supply data.

@item
Inside the existing @code{#ifdef HAVE_CMACS_LIBREGNUM} block in
@code{motion_notify_event}, after the @code{cmacs_libregnum_handle_motion}
check:
@example
/* CMACS: libregnum editor drag-source -- if a palette/asset drag was
   armed from Elisp ([down-mouse-1]), check whether the pointer has
   moved past GTK's drag threshold and, if so, begin a GDK drag. */
if (cmacs_libregnum_dnd_check_motion (frame, event))
  return TRUE;
@end example
@end enumerate

The @code{#ifdef HAVE_CMACS_LIBREGNUM} include block gains
@code{#include "cmacs-libregnum-dnd.h"}.  All three sub-hunks compile out
when @code{HAVE_CMACS_LIBREGNUM} is unset.  @strong{Key invariant:}
@code{gtk_drag_source_set} is @emph{not} used (it would intercept every
button press on the whole frame widget, breaking text selection and scroll
bars).  Instead the drag is armed from Elisp @code{[down-mouse-1]} and
started from the motion callback.
@end itemize

@subsubsection Editor drag-and-drop

Two drag paths complement each other:

@itemize
@item @strong{Intra-Emacs drag} (@code{drag-mouse-1}, no additional upstream
edit beyond the input-routing hook): Emacs delivers the drag to the start
buffer's keymap with the release position; the palette/asset handler
ray-casts the viewport drop point to the ground plane.
@item @strong{GTK-native GDK drag-source} (@code{[down-mouse-1]} →
@code{cmacs-libregnum-dnd-arm} → @code{cmacs_libregnum_dnd_check_motion}
→ @code{gtk_drag_begin_with_coordinates}): allows dragging palette/asset
items to external GTK apps.  The one upstream hunk is the pair in
@code{pgtkterm.c} described above.
@item @strong{External file drag} (e.g. a @file{.glb} from a file manager) is
handled by stock @code{pgtk-dnd} extended with the
@code{"application/x-libregnum"} entry in @code{pgtk-dnd-types-alist} (pure
Elisp, no upstream edit).
@end itemize

@subsubsection Editor right-click context menu --- no upstream change

The viewport entity context menu (right-click an entity for a GTK menu of
actions) needs @emph{no new upstream edit}.  It rides the existing libregnum
pointer-input hunk: a right-click (button-3 press+release without movement) is
detected in @code{cmacs_libregnum_handle_button} (pure C in
@file{cmacs/libregnum/cmacs-libregnum-input.c}), which ray-picks the node and
defers @code{cmacs-libregnum-editor--context-menu}.  The menu itself is Emacs's
own @code{x-popup-menu} (a native @code{GtkMenu} in the pgtk build), popped from
the command loop via a 0-delay timer, so no GTK-menu code is added and the
existing button hook already routes the event.  A right-drag still pans.

@subsection src/xwidget.c / src/xwidget.h --- gowl client embedding

All gated @code{HAVE_CMACS_GOWL} (and @code{HAVE_PGTK} for events).  gowl
embeds Wayland clients as a kind of xwidget: a @code{Qgowl} xwidget type,
two opaque fields on @code{struct xwidget} (@code{gowl_client},
@code{gowl_view}), @code{cmacs_xwidget_allocate_gowl} (called from
@file{cmacs-gowl.c}), and the focus / event-forwarding helpers
(@code{xwidget_gowl_set_focused_view} / @code{_clear_focused_view},
@code{xwidget_gowl_forward_event}) that route GTK key/pointer events to the
focused gowl view's Wayland client.

@subsection src/lisp.h --- init/syms externs

A single @code{/* CMACS */} block of @code{extern void init_cmacs_*} /
@code{syms_of_cmacs_*} declarations, one @code{#ifdef HAVE_CMACS_*} per
subsystem, so @file{emacs.c} can call them.  The block opens with the
@emph{unconditional} @code{extern void syms_of_cmacs_features (void);}
(the always-linked IS-CMACS-* registry, outside any @code{#ifdef}).

@node Upstream Build System
@section Build system

@subsection configure.ac + src/config.in

@file{configure.ac} adds all the @option{--with-cmacs-*} /
@option{--enable-cmacs-*} options (plus the @option{--with-gsurf} /
@option{--with-gowl} aliases), their dependency checks (e.g.@: gsurf and
libregnum require @option{--with-cmacs-glib} and @option{--with-pgtk}; ai
shares libreclaw's @code{ai-glib} archive), the
@code{AC_DEFINE([HAVE_CMACS_*])} and @code{AC_SUBST} of each subsystem's
@code{OBJ}/@code{CFLAGS}/@code{LIBS}/@code{BUNDLED} variables.
@file{src/config.in} carries the matching @code{#undef HAVE_CMACS_*} lines
(autoheader-maintained) plus @code{#undef CMACS_PIPER_DEFAULT_BINARY}.  Each
@code{CMACS_*_OBJ} list grows with its subsystem --- e.g. @code{CMACS_LIBREGNUM_OBJ}
gained @code{cmacs-libregnum-dnd.o} (editor GDK drag-source) and
@code{CMACS_MCP_OBJ} gained @code{cmacs-mcp-tools-libregnum.o} (editor MCP tools),
each under its existing guard.  Re-run @code{autoconf} + @code{./config.status
--recheck} after editing (not bare @code{./configure}).

@subsection src/epaths.in

Adds @code{#define PATH_CMACS_API "/usr/local/lib/cmacs"} --- where crispy
and bacon locate @file{libcmacs-api.so} / @file{cmacs-api.h} on an installed
system.

@subsection Makefile.in (top-level) --- install & docs targets

The top-level build/install Makefile gains: the @code{cmacsapidir} install
location and rules installing @file{cmacs/api/libcmacs-api.so} +
@file{cmacs-api.h} and the bacon @file{.so} modules; a @code{sed} step
that rewrites @code{PATH_CMACS_API} in the installed @file{epaths.h} to
@code{cmacsapidir}; the @code{cmacs-deps-docs} target (a prerequisite of
@code{actual-all}, so it runs on every @code{make}) that mirrors each
@file{deps/<dep>/docs} tree into @file{doc_org/cmacs/deps/}; and the
optional ``Print to cmacs'' printer and @code{org.cmacs.Editor} D-Bus
service install/uninstall targets.

@subsection java/Makefile.in --- Android Doom bundle

When @file{build-aux/android-doom-bundle/} exists (populated by
@file{build-aux/android-build.sh} from the host's
@file{~/.config/@{emacs,doom@}}), copy it into
@file{install_temp/assets/doom-bundle} so the APK ships an embedded Doom
config.

@subsection src/Makefile.in --- linking & static-library dedup

@itemize @bullet
@item
@code{vpath %.c} / @code{vpath %.h} entries for each
@file{cmacs/<subsystem>/} dir; the @code{CMACS_*_OBJ}/@code{_CFLAGS}/
@code{_LIBS}/@code{_BUNDLED} variable block.
@item
@strong{Always-linked core}: @code{CMACS_CORE_OBJ = cmacs-features.o} is a
plain (non-@code{@@...@@}) assignment appended to @code{base_obj} +
@code{CMACS_ALL_OBJ}, with @code{core} in @code{CMACS_DIRS} and the
vpath/@code{CMACS_INTERNAL_CFLAGS} lists.  It links
@file{cmacs/core/cmacs-features.c} in @emph{every} configuration so the
@code{IS-CMACS-<NAME>} flags are always bound; being in @code{base_obj}
also puts it in @code{doc_obj} so @code{make-docfile -g} generates the
@code{DEFVAR_BOOL} globals.
@item
Subsystem static libraries (e.g.@: @file{libgsurf.a},
@file{liborgex-1.0.a}) are whole-archived into temacs.
@item
@strong{Static-library dedup} (critical invariant): crispy and yaml-glib
objects are vendored in several archives, possibly from different commits.
The @code{_bacon_strip} / @code{_gowl_strip} / @code{_libreclaw_strip} /
@code{_gsurf_strip} machinery runs @code{ar d} to delete duplicate objects
from the downstream archives before linking, and strips any
@option{-Wl,-z,muldefs} from the per-lib LIBS.  @strong{Never} re-add
@option{-Wl,-z,muldefs} --- it silently picks one implementation, giving an
ABI mismatch and non-deterministic crashes.
@item
@code{make-docfile} / @code{gl-stamp} need the cmacs @file{.c} files
visible from @code{srcdir}; a @code{cmacs-symlinks} prerequisite plus the
@code{/* CMACS: make-docfile needs .c files visible from srcdir. */} hunk
arrange that.  The @code{cmacs-symlinks} recipe walks @code{CMACS_ALL_OBJ}, so
new subsystem objects (e.g.@: @code{cmacs-libregnum-dnd.o}) are symlinked
automatically once added to a @code{CMACS_*_OBJ} var.
@item
@strong{Submodule build rules}: the @code{deps/libregnum} rule passes
@code{CRISPY=1 HAS_GI=1} + @code{GI_RUNTIME_@{CFLAGS,LIBS@}} (crispy + the
gjs/PyGObject scripting backends compile) and @code{BUILD_GIR=1} @emph{when
@code{g-ir-scanner} is present} (else @code{BUILD_GIR=0}, for cross/sandboxed
builds where the scanner's @code{dlopen} of @code{liblibregnum.so} is
unreliable).  @code{BUILD_GIR=1} produces @code{Libregnum-1.typelib} so cmacs's
@code{gi} subsystem and the libregnum gjs/PyGObject backends can introspect the
editor types; the Justfile @code{run} recipe exports @code{GI_TYPELIB_PATH} to
the libregnum + graylib gir dirs (symbols resolve from temacs's @code{-rdynamic}
statics, so no @code{LD_LIBRARY_PATH} is added).  It deliberately does @emph{not}
pass @code{BUILD_EDITOR_UI=1}, so the engine's standalone editor app stays out of
temacs (a separate optional frontend).  @code{deps/gsurf} pins
@code{STATIC=1 BUILD_GIR=1 GTK_BACKEND=gtk3}.
@end itemize

@subsection test/Makefile.in --- cmacs test-suite support

Three small hunks (each marked @code{## CMACS: ...}):

@itemize @bullet
@item
Add @code{cmacs} to the @code{SUBDIRS} find so the @code{check-cmacs}
target exists (runs every @file{test/cmacs/*-tests.el}).
@item
Skip the test-to-source dependency heuristic for @code{cmacs/%} tests
(@code{$(filter cmacs/%,$(1))} in the @code{test_template} condition):
the heuristic derives @file{../cmacs/FOO.el}, but cmacs sources live in
@file{lisp/cmacs/} and @file{cmacs/<subsystem>/}, so the bogus
prerequisite made every individual @code{make -C test cmacs/FOO-tests}
target fail.
@item
Export a writable @env{XDG_CACHE_HOME} (@code{TEST_XDG_CACHE}, next to
@code{TEST_HOME}) in the two test-running recipes: crispy tests compile
C via gcc into the user cache dir, which cannot be created under the
sandboxed @code{HOME=/nonexistent}.
@end itemize

@node Upstream Lisp
@section Lisp

@subsection lisp/startup.el

One hunk (marked @code{;; CMacs: ...}): after Elisp init, on an interactive
session, @code{(require 'cmacs-config)} and call
@code{cmacs-config-load-all} so the user's bacon / C config files load.

@file{lisp/site-start.el} is a @emph{new} cmacs file (not an edit): it
rides Emacs's stock @code{site-run-file} load to bootstrap the embedded
Doom config on the first Android launch.  Everything else cmacs adds is a
@emph{new} file under @file{lisp/cmacs/} picked up by the generated
loaders.  @file{.gitignore} also gains entries for cmacs build artifacts.

@node Re-applying After a Merge
@section Re-applying after a merge

@enumerate
@item
Search the upstream files for @code{CMACS} comments; each marks a hunk
above.
@item
For hunks without a marker, use this catalogue to re-create them.
@item
Rebuild, forcing a relink if temacs-linked objects changed:
@code{rm -f src/temacs && make -j$(nproc)}.
@item
Sanity-check the DEFUNs are present: @code{just check-defuns} (or
@kbd{M-x} a known @code{cmacs-} command).
@end enumerate

@bye
